\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,069 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: U.S. stocks are expected to post strong opening gains Monday as investors respond positively to news that the nuclear meltdown in Japan appeared to be receding.\par \par \uc2 At 1125 GMT, S&P 500 futures were up 14.70 points at 1288.90, Nasdaq 100 futures were up 25.63 points at 2247.13 and Dow industrial futures were up 87 points at 11907.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European markets higher, led by telecoms. \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2  led blue-chip gainers, up 13%, after it agreed to a sale of its US assets to \uc2 AT&T\uc2 . Indeed, U.S. investors will likely focus on \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , up about 0.2% in pre-market trading. Oil prices were also being eyed as air strikes in Libya continued.\par \par \uc2 Traders warned of bearish indicators. One noted in particular that European sovereign debt issues will come to the fore later this week, with Portugal expected to vote on austerity measures Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 At 1140 GMT, the Euro Stoxx 50 index was trading 1.8% higher at 2843.33.\par \par \uc2 London's FTSE 100 was up, coming off earlier highs. Despite concerns over Western \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya, the mood remained buoyant with progress at \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis improving, said \uc2 IG Index\uc2 . "The FTSE 100 is almost back to where it was a week ago--investors had got used to rallies being one day wonders, but this one has proved to be more sustainable," added IG.\par \par \uc2 At 1130 GMT, June bunds were down 0.26 at 122.37, while June gilts were down 0.36 at 118.23.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets the euro was trading a touch lower midday but still well supported above the $1.41 level as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still expected to raise interest rates. The bombing in Libya and protests in Bahrain propelled oil prices higher and put worries about spiralling inflation back at the top of the agenda. The yen remained little changed after Friday's concerted G-7 move to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscored the political will to keep the yen from rising too far.\par \par \uc2 At 1130 GMT, the dollar was up at Y81.27, the euro was down at $1.4169 and the pound was up at $1.6268.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:\par \par \uc2 DEUTSCHE TELEKOM\uc2  SURGES ON SALE OF US \b \highlight22\uc2 OPERATIONS\b0 \highlight\uc2  TO \uc2 AT&T\uc2 : \uc2 Deutsche Telekom\uc2 's shares are sharply higher after it says it will sell its \uc2 T-Mobile USA\uc2  unit to \uc2 AT&T\uc2  in a $39 billion deal that will create by far the biggest telecom provider in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 OFGEM TELLS UK BIG ENERGY COS TO AUCTION POWER OUTPUT: The U.K.'s big six gas and electricity suppliers will be required to auction up to 20% of their electricity generation output, Ofgem says, as part of its proposals to break the companies' stranglehold on the market.\par \par \uc2 GADHAFI FORCES PULL BACK AFTER LIBYA AIR STRIKES: Libyan government forces pull back 100 kilometers from the rebel-held city of Benghazi after international air strikes destroy much of their armor and leave dozens of tanks wrecked.\par \par \uc2 JAPAN TO MONITOR SEAWATER NEAR NUCLEAR PLANT: Japan is set to monitor radiation levels in the Pacific Ocean near an earthquake-hit nuclear plant, where firefighters have been using seawater to cool reactor units.\par \par \uc2 WORLD BANK ECONOMIST: IMPACT OF JAPAN DISASTER $100B-$230B: The earthquake and its aftermath in Japan likely caused $100 billion to $230 billion worth of damage, equivalent to 2.5%-4% of the country's gross domestic product, World Bank chief economist Justin Yifu Lin is cited as saying.\par \par \uc2 ============================\par \par \uc2 INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES:\par \par \uc2 =THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Nasdaq OMX's potential unsolicited bid for \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  would face substantial antitrust questions in the U.S., where it would merge two direct competitors and create a monopoly in the market for corporate listings.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ET/GMT   COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 0830/1230  US   Feb     Chicago Fed National Activity Index \par 1000/1400  US   Feb     Existing Home Sales \par 1000/1400  EU           ECB President Trichet at quarterly session with MEPs \par N/A        CAN  Mar     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  Banking and Financial Statistics \par                         expected \par N/A        EU           Eurozone finance ministers extraordinary meeting \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SWISS RE ESTIMATES JAPAN QUAKE, TSUNAMI CLAIMS AT $1.2B: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss Re\f28 \uc2 \f2  says it expects claims costs of $1.2 billion from the earthquake and tsunami in Japan, but its estimate is subject to a higher than usual degree of uncertainty and may need to be adjusted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RUSSIA MAY LAUNCH PACIFIC OIL PIPELINE IN 2012: The second stage of the East Siberia-Pacific Ocean pipeline carrying Russian crude to Asian-Pacific markets and the U.S. may be put into service more than a year ahead of plan by the end of 2012, the country's pipeline operator says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMANY MULLS BOOST TO POWER LINES FOR RENEWABLES: German Economy Minister Rainer Bruederle is working on a draft of new legislation to accelerate the massive development of power lines through Germany to transport more wind energy the Sueddeutsche Zeitung says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROCKHOPPER FINDS SIGNIFICANT FALKLAND OIL: Oil and gas explorer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rockhopper Exploration\f28 \uc2 \f2 's shares rise 37% after the company say it made a significant discovery in its first appraisal well in the Sea Lion area of the Falkland Islands.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN ECONOMIC GROWTH TO GAIN STRENGTH: Germany's economy continued to grow early in the year, and should gain strength again throughout 2011, enabling the country to hit it's growth target, the finance ministry says in its monthly report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEIGHTENED RISKS FROM COMMODITIES WEIGH ON INFLATION: Luxembourg's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief warns of heightened risks to inflation in the euro zone due in large part to rising global commodities prices and says the ECB stands ready to react in a way to head them off.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IRISH PM TO MEET MINISTERS AHEAD OF EU DEBT TALKS: Irish Prime Minister Enda Kenny is expected to host a meeting of his most senior ministers ahead of a series of key meetings with European Union leaders later this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO TINTO FACES KEY WEEK FOR RIVERSDALE BID: Australian mining giant \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  faces a crucial week in its quest to take over Africa-focused coal producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riversdale Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2 , after a slow take-up of acceptances since the offer was first made.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Simon Varcoe, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9449; simon.varcoe@dowjones.com [ 21-03-11 1208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 70737   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l000up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
