\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS: European Stocks Set To Open Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- European stocks expected to open higher\par \par \uc2 -- Concerns about Japan's nuclear plant wane\par \par \uc2 -- Oil prices jump as Western forces strike Libya\par \par \uc2 -- Yen pressured by prospect of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Ishaq Siddiqi\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--European stock markets are expected to open higher Monday, helped by gains in Asia overnight after Japanese officials announced that conditions at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant have continued to improve over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 This has been enough to provide support to risk assets so far, said \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . "When confronted by a possible nuclear meltdown, the absence of further bad news is, indeed, good news," added \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Officials cautioned that many steps remain before the plant is fully under control, and that the possibility of larger radiation discharges still exists, but, for the first time, Japanese leaders voiced optimism that the worst of the crisis is over.\par \par \uc2 Against this backdrop, Jonathan Sudaria, a dealer with Capital Spreads, called London's FTSE 100 index to open 34 points higher at 5752, Frankfurt's DAX index to open 56 points higher at 6720 and the CAC-40 index in Paris up 31 points at 3841.\par \par \uc2 Still, Sudaria cautioned that even though stocks are expected to open higher, significant downside risks remain, with the crisis in the Middle East and North Africa still being played out.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, tensions in North Africa damaged sentiment as Western forces struck in Libya, tightening enforcement of a United Nations resolution aimed at halting attacks on civilians to suppress a month-long uprising against Moammar Gadhafi's regime. The Libyan headlines sent crude oil prices higher and fueled concerns that expensive crude could derail global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 By 0715 GMT, April Nymex crude oil futures were up $1.98 at $103.05 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 In Asia Monday, stock markets were mostly higher amid growing optimism about progress in resolving Japan's nuclear crisis, while energy stocks benefited from higher oil and commodity prices on heightened geopolitical concerns in the Middle East and North Africa.\par \par \uc2 "We think a protracted Libyan civil war may be an economic non-event as Saudi will make up for [oil] supply shortfalls. More importantly, serious nuclear fallout in Japan looks increasingly unlikely, with some power restored to plants, and fire engines having good success in keeping spent fuel rods cool," said Phillip Securities in a research note.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi Composite was 1.1% higher and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index rose 1.5%. The Shanghai Composite Index fell from its highs to trade down 0.2%, while the Japanese markets were shut for Vernal Equinox Day.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street Friday, stocks climbed as fears of increased violence in Libya ebbed and the Group of Seven's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to relieve investors' worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished Friday with a gain of 83.93 points, or 0.7%, at 11,858.52. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 5.49, or 0.4%, to 1279.21, while the Nasdaq Composite added 7.62, or 0.3%, to 2643.67.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen was weaker on the prospect of further moves by the G-7 central banks to sell it even though the safe-haven Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by the ongoing military action in Libya and the surge in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 At 0720 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y80.92, up from Y80.60 in late New York trade on Friday, while the euro was at Y114.67, up from Y114.35, and at $1.4176, down from $1.4182.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, spot gold was at $1428.00 per troy ounce, up $10.10 from New York Friday. In the bond markets, the June bund futures contract was down 0.13 at 122.50.\par \par \uc2 Few economic data releases are scheduled from the U.K. or Europe. In the U.S., however, existing home sales data for February are due at 1400 GMT.\par \par \uc2 -By Ishaq Siddiqi, Dow Jones Newswires; +44-20-7842-9488; ishaq.siddiqi@dowjones.com [ 21-03-11 0724GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l000d6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
