\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Shares Mostly Up; Focus On Japan, Libya Developments\b0\par\par\uc2 901 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds information, quotes, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 By Colin Ng and Ga-Woon Philip Vahn\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Most Asian stock markets were higher Monday amid growing optimism over progress in resolving Japan's nuclear crisis, while energy stocks benefited from higher oil and commodity prices on heightened geopolitical concerns in the Middle East and North Africa.\par \par \uc2 "We think a protracted Libyan civil war may be an economic non-event as Saudi will make up for (oil) supply shortfalls. More importantly, serious nuclear fallout in Japan looks increasingly unlikely with some power restored to plants, and fire engines having good success in keeping spent fuel rods cool," said Phillip Securities in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 rose 0.2%, South Korea's Kospi Composite was 0.9% higher and the Shanghai Composite Index was up 0.3%. Japanese markets were shut for Vernal Equinox Day.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index rose 1.2%, while Taiwan's main index gained 0.8% and India's Sensex fell 0.4%. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were up 62 points in screen trade.\par \par \uc2 Markets were supported by Wall Street's gains on Friday as well as news over the weekend that Japan had made progress with its nuclear crisis, restoring power to some of its stricken reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi plant. Still, some traders were cautious. "We question whether the stabilisation in the yen is sufficient reason for a sustained gain in risk appetite when so many questions hang over the outlook for Japan, not least surrounding the nuclear threat," Credit Agricole said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The tensions in North Africa also tempered sentiment, as allied forces struck in Libya, tightening enforcement of a United Nations resolution aimed at halting attacks on civilians to suppress a month-long uprising against Moammar Gadhafi's regime.\par \par \uc2 Libyan headlines sent crude oil prices higher and fueled concerns expensive crude could derail global economic growth. April Nymex crude oil futures were up $1.88 at $102.95 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks were outperforming, with Australia's \uc2 Woodside Petroleum\uc2  up 1.7% and Santos up 1.7%, while in South Korea, S-Oil rose 1.1%. \uc2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . rose 0.4% and \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  gained 1.6% in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 In China, investors appeared to largely look past the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move Friday to raise banks' reserve requirement ratio as it was largely priced-in. Property developers were mostly higher with \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  up 2.3% and \uc2 Poly Real Estate Group\uc2  up 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  said Friday it will raise the share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by 0.50 percentage points, the third hike this year, as inflationary pressures remain in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Australian uranium stocks continued to recover from heavy losses caused by the Japanese nuclear crisis. Paladin was up 2.5% and \uc2 Extract Resources\uc2  added 13.3%.\par \par \uc2 China's latest reserve ratio requirement hike had little impact on Australian miners as the move had been well flagged. BHP Billiton was flat and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  rose 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, New Zealand's NZX-50 was up 0.3%, Philippine shares were up 0.1%, while Singapore's Straits Times Index rose 1.0%, Thai shares gained 1.0%, Malaysia's KLCI added 0.1% and Indonesian shares rose 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen was weighed by the prospect of further moves by major central banks to weaken it even as the safe-haven Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was supported by ongoing military action in Libya and a surge in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 A Japan market holiday was adding to the yen's whipsaw moves, as overall trading activity tapered off, and with traders in other parts of Asia on close watch for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen, after coordinated action by the Group of Seven industrialized nations Friday to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 UBS\uc2  noted that in the wake of the Japanese developments, and given the heightened Middle East tensions, Tokyo leaders seemed determined to draw a line in the sand on the U.S. dollar against the yen, "but the process may take longer than most would expect as wider conditions are not conducive for yen weakness."\par \par \uc2 They added further action by central banks was "very likely, either through deployment of their own resources or custodial transactions on behalf of the Bank of Japan."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at Y80.87, from Y80.62 in late New York trade on Friday while the euro was at Y114.61, from 114.34 and at $1.4171, from $1.4175.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold was at $1,428.10 per troy ounce, up $8.40 from its New York settlement Friday.\par \par \uc2 -Colin Ng, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4142; colin.ng@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 21-03-11 0453GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125