\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD BANK: JAPAN'S ECONOMY TO SLOW TEMPORARILY AFTER DISASTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy will temporarily slow after this month's devastating earthquake and tsunami, but growth will likely accelerate in the second half of the year, the World Bank said in a report Sunday. (published 0233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN RULING PARTY OFFICIAL: 2 EXTRA BUDGETS NEEDED BY END OF JUNE - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruling Democratic Party of Japan's Diet affairs chief Jun Azumi said Monday that the government may need to compile two large-scale supplementary budgets for post-quake reconstruction by the end of June, Kyodo News reported. (published 0242 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FURTHER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEPENDS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET MOVES - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry will likely stay on high alert against possible rebounds in the yen Monday, leaving the door open for further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after last week's concerted action by the world's central banks to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surge. (published 1907 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM DEPUTY PRIME MINISTER: 1Q GDP GROWTH LIKELY +5.5% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Vietnamese economy is forecast to grow 5.5% in the first quarter from a year earlier, Deputy Prime Minister Nguyen Sinh Hung said Monday. (published 0322 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN MERCHANDISE IMPORTS UP ADJUSTED 6% IN FEB VS JAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian merchandise imports rose 6% to A$16.57 billion in February from A$15.78 billion in January in seasonally adjusted terms, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said Monday. (published 0034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI PRIME MINISTER PLEDGES CUT TO CORPORATE TAX RATE - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai government has pledged to cut the corporate tax rate to help businesses deal with a possible increase in the minimum wage, a key election promise of the ruling Democrat party, Bangkok Post reported on Monday, citing Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva. (published 0134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI FINANCE MINISTER: NEW GOVERNMENT WON'T IMPACT ECONOMIC POLICY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thai Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Monday that he doesn't expect any significant change in Thai economic policy once a new government is installed this year. (published 0353 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EMERGING COUNTRIES MUST MOVE QUICKLY AGAINST INFLATION - IMF'S LIPSKY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many emerging economies are in danger of overheating and should act quickly to tighten monetary policy, a top International Monetary Fund official said. (published 0158 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOE DALE: UK RECOVERY SET TO CONTINUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s economic recovery looks set to continue, the Bank of England's chief economist said Monday. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK MARCH PROPERTY ASKING PRICES EDGE UP - RIGHTMOVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K. housing market ticked over in March, with sellers slightly more upbeat about the price they might get for their property despite a cloudy economic outlook, a survey showed Monday. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK HOUSEHOLD FINANCES WORSEN SHARPLY IN MARCH - SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britons were more pessimistic about their finances than at any time in the past two years in March, a survey showed Monday, a warning sign for Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne just days before his crucial Budget announcement March 23. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: JAPAN MAKES GAINS IN BATTLING NUCLEAR REACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan appears to have turned the tide in its battle to stave off nuclear disaster, restoring power to parts of the Fukushima Daiichi plant and bringing down radiation levels with a marathon water-spraying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 3RD WSJ UPDATE: US, COALITION FORCES ATTACK LIBYA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In one opening salvo, U.S. and U.K. forces unleashed around 110 Tomahawk cruise missiles against Libyan targets. Vice Adm. William Gortney Saturday told reporters that the missiles, which struck Libya around 3:00 p.m. EDT, were aimed at "more than 20" Libyan air-defense sites. (published 1024 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN VOLATILE BUT DRIFTING LOWER; CONFLICTING SIGNALS REMAIN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen was volatile but trending lower in Asia Monday, as the prospect of further moves by major central banks to weaken it offset the impact of ongoing military action in Libya and a surge in oil prices. (published 0124 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EXPECTED TO HIKE BY 25 BPS THURSDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to raise overnight rates, which it has kept at record lows since July 2009, at its policy meeting Thursday to