\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Volatile But Drifting Lower; Conflicting Signals Remain\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to recast the first graph and update prices. Includes new comments on the yen in graphs 7-8, plus comments on the Swiss franc in graphs 17-18.)\par \par \uc2 -Yen volatile in Asia, touches low of Y81.02 against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 -Coalition efforts in Libya support yen, oil rises\par \par \uc2 -Potential for more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  caps yen\par \par \uc2 By Geoffrey Rogow\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The yen was volatile but trending lower in Asia Monday, as the prospect of further moves by major central banks to weaken it offset the impact of ongoing military action in Libya and a surge in oil prices.\par \par \uc2 A Japan market holiday added to the whipsaw moves in the yen as overall activity tapered off and with traders in other parts of Asia on close watch for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen.\par \par \uc2 So far in Asia the U.S. dollar had swung back and forth between a low of Y80.63 and a high of Y81.02. At the same time, Nymex crude oil futures jumped $2.20 a barrel to $103.27 on Globex.\par \par \uc2 Pushing oil higher, and likely to help safe-haven assets such as the yen, U.S. and coalition forces launched military strikes over the weekend against Libya in a bid to knock out loyalist support for leader Col. Moammar Gadhafi. Early Monday, Coalition forces continued to move to impose a no-fly zone over Libya.\par \par \uc2 But for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, these developments in the short-term will be somewhat overshadowed by the potential for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and major central banks around the world.\par \par \uc2 The effect of Friday's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the first in more than a decade--pushed the U.S. dollar as high as Y81.97 from below Y76.80 on Thursday, though the gains had waned by late Friday in New York. The pair was recently at Y80.96.\par \par \uc2 "The threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to see dollar/yen track modestly higher and cap any downside," said Joseph Capurso, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For now, much of the focus remained on the technicals surrounding the pair, with \uc2 Deutsche Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Horner noting solid resistance around Y82.00 and the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any dip towards Y80. Others have noted Y81.20 could be a difficult short-term resistance point.\par \par \uc2 Complicating matters for the yen and risk sentiment generally, Japan has been battling to avert a nuclear crisis in the wake of March 11's massive earthquake and tsunami, which devastated large parts of the northeast and caused a key nuclear power facility to become unstable.\par \par \uc2 The yen has risen sharply since the quake hit, partly on the expectation of repatriation flows to Japan for insurance and reconstruction costs, and as speculators try and get ahead of the flows by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But as the nuclear crisis looks to be turning a corner, with operator \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . saying some reactors have been reconnected to the power grid, some of that pressure could ease.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 UBS\uc2  note that in the wake of the Japanese developments, and given the heightened Middle East tensions, Tokyo leaders seems determined to draw a line in the sand on the U.S. dollar against the yen, "but the process may take longer than most would expect as wider conditions are not conducive for yen weakness."\par \par \uc2 They added further action by central banks is "very likely, either through deployment of their own resources or custodial transactions on behalf of the Bank of Japan."\par \par \uc2 Away from the Japanese yen, one pair that could rise in the near term is the euro/dollar, said Thomas Stolper, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research for \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Stolper noted rising oil prices, a deteriorating trade balance and stock flow data all hint at gains for the pair.\par \par \uc2 "Together with the more hawkish stance of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to the Fed, this will likely translate into further (euro/dollar) upside," said Stolper, who targets the pair at $1.50.\par \par \uc2 The euro was recently at $1.4172, from $1.4180 late in New York on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was making modest ground against the Swiss franc, recently at CHF0.9032 from CHF0.8985 earlier, despite the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's status also as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Partly helping curb the Swiss franc was that the nuclear situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be improving, while the prospect of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen could be a weight against it.\par \par \uc2 "If Japan comes in and puts a floor (under dollar/yen), (and) pumps liquidity into the global financial system, we would expect the asset markets to set off on another rally and the Swissie would underperform in that scenario," said Rob Ryan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Though that requires the yen to weaken in the first place. This may just be a reflection of the holiday market."\par \par \uc2 -By Geoffrey Rogow, Dow Jones Newswires; +61-2-8272-4686; geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Enda Curran in Sydney contributed to this article. [ 21-03-11 0124GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
