\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF: Decision To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  Again Depends On Market Moves - Source\b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 01:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 Japan's Finance Ministry will likely stay on high alert against possible rebounds in the yen Monday, leaving the door open for further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after last week's concerted action by the world's central banks to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge.\par \par \uc2 "Everything depends on how the market will move," a person familiar with Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy recently told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 His comments suggest that the finance ministry may order the Bank of Japan to sell the yen if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises in a way it considers excessively sharp.\par \par \uc2 The ministry appears to maintain an elevated level of vigilance even after the Group of Seven industrial powers together sold the yen Friday, helping stabilize a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had surged even as the recent earthquake and tsunami devastated the nation's northern regions. Lights were on inside the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Division--which executes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders from the finance minister--on Sunday even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was closed.\par \par \uc2 Gains in the yen could damage the nation's export-led economy by making Japanese exports less competitive abroad.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit an all-time high Thursday, with the dollar falling to Y76.25. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later recovered, and it was trading around Y79.00 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started in the Friday morning in Tokyo. The yen ended well above the Y80 mark that day.\par \par \uc2 The precise scope of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't known, but traders estimated the yen selling by the club was in the neighborhood of $20 billion. The Bank of Japan likely accounted for much of the yen-dumping. The rest was done by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Federal Reserve and monetary authorities in Canada, the U.K., and individual central banks in the European continent.\par \par \uc2 Fumihiko Igarashi, one of the two senior Japanese vice finance ministers, told Dow Jones Newswires Friday that the G-7 is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cooperation again if "excessive" speculative moves push the yen higher. Igarashi said his "desire" is to see the dollar-yen exchange rate returning to the levels seen before the massive earthquake and tsunami on March 11, which were around Y83. Yet ministry officials have privately emphasized that their target is volatility, not a specific dollar-yen level. Also, the person familiar with the matter said that while the G-7 may discuss the need of further concerted action if deemed necessary, the group on the whole didn't signal commitment during its telephone conference held Friday to continuing the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into this week.\par \par \uc2 Because the amounts of yen-selling by the G-7 members other than Japan appeared small, some traders speculate that the other G-7 economies just wanted to ease and prevent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market volatility from destabilizing the global stock markets and economy. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone from now, the traders say. -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020110322e73l0008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
