\par\par \b \uc2 AT A GLANCE: Japan Makes Some Progress In Nuclear Plant Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 1,301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THE NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities are reporting progress Monday in the twin goals of cooling the affected reactors at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant using fire trucks and water cannon while restoring electric power to internal cooling systems.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The plant operator said gray smoke was coming out of the No. 3 reactor around 0655 GMT, however, and said it is examining the situation after workers at the site were evacuated.\par \par \uc2 The developments come a day after the International Atomic Energy Agency said radiation levels measured in major Japanese cities haven't changed significantly and remain below levels dangerous to human health.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government is working on a set of emergency tax breaks for businesses and individuals to help speed reconstruction in the ravaged areas of eastern Japan, the Nikkei reported. It may need to compile two large-scale supplementary budgets for post-quake reconstruction by the end of June, Kyodo News reported.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, low-level radiation was detected on some food products in Japan and exported to Taiwan. Testing of tap water is continuing in Fukushima Prefecture after more than three times a guideline level of radiation was found in water at a village there.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STATUS OF REACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's nuclear safety agency said Monday that power has been restored to reactors No. 5 and 6.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (9501.TO) said power had been restored to the plant's No. 1 and 2 reactors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pressure built up in reactor No. 3 Sunday but stabilized at elevated levels, the plant operator said Sunday. The nuclear safety agency also said the spent fuel at the No. 4 reactor appeared to be immersed in water.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Efforts to cool other reactors by spraying them with water also appear to be paying off. Tepco said the radiation level at reactor No. 3 fell to 2,758 microsieverts on Saturday, compared with 3,443 microsieverts earlier, after hours of dousing it with water.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People in a 20-kilometer radius, about 12 miles, around the plant have been evacuated while those in the area between 20 kilometers and 30 kilometers of the plant have been told to stay indoors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par YEN HELD IN CHECK FOLLOWING G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen settled down after some early volatility in Asia Monday, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market holiday, with the prospect of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  curbing yen appreciation for now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Deputy Vice Minister of Finance for International Affairs Naoko Ishii said Monday that she expects the spirit of cooperation among the Group of Seven industrial powers to continue, following a rare joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week. She spoke to reporters on the sidelines of a World Bank conference in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But she was vague about whether and under what circumstances the Japanese government would engage in further yen-selling, saying only "Let's ask the markets." That echoed news from Tokyo overnight, where a person familiar with Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy recently told Dow Jones Newswires: "Everything depends on how the market will move."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CASUALTIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's national police agency said the number of people killed by the earthquake and tsunami as of 0300 GMT Monday was 8,649, with 12,877 missing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Newswires' coverage of the event and related news can be found by searching the code R/JA.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LATEST STORIES: \par  \par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 TOKYO ELECTRIC POWER\f28 \uc2 \f2 : SAW GRAY SMOKE FROM DAIICHI REACTOR NO. 3 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it saw gray smoke come out from the southeast of the No. 3 reactor at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant at around 0655 GMT. (published 0810 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN: NUCLEAR SITUATION GETTING BETTER BIT BY BIT - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Prime Minister Naoto Kan said conditions at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant, which was crippled by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, are improving gradually, Kyodo News reported Monday. (published 0743 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN NUCLEAR AGENCY: NO NEED TO WIDEN EVACUATION AREAS AROUND NUCLEAR REACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's nuclear safety agency on Monday said there isn't any need to widen the evacuation zone around an earthquake-stricken nuclear power station in northern Japan from the current 20 kilometer radius. (published 0443 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: JAPAN MAKES GAINS IN BATTLING NUCLEAR REACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan appears to have turned the tide in its battle to stave off nuclear disaster, restoring power to parts of the Fukushima Daiichi plant and bringing down radiation levels with a marathon water-spraying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WATER TESTING TO CONTINUE IN FUKUSHIMA AFTER RADIATION DETECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukushima Prefecture will continue to test tap water after more than three times a guideline level of radiation was found in water at a village in the prefecture. (published 0027 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: DOWNSIDE RISKS OF JAPAN'S DISASTERS HAVE INCREASED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The downside risks posed by Japan's recent earthquake, tsunami and nuclear crisis are larger than previously thought, and Moody's Investors Service's view on the country assumes the swift containment of its nuclear problem, the credit rating agency said Monday. (published 0728 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN SENTIMENT HURT BY NUCLEAR CRISIS, MAY HIT ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's business and consumer confidence have been hurt by the nuclear plant crisis that authorities are still grappling with, and the weakening in sentiment could hit the economy, a senior government official said Monday. (published 0643 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN RULING PARTY OFFICIAL: 2 EXTRA BUDGETS NEEDED BY END OF JUNE - KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ruling Democratic Party of Japan's Diet affairs chief Jun Azumi said Monday that the government may need to compile two large-scale supplementary budgets for post-quake reconstruction by the end of June, Kyodo News reported. (published 0242 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WORLD BANK: JAPAN'S ECONOMY TO SLOW TEMPORARILY AFTER DISASTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy will temporarily slow after this month's devastating earthquake and tsunami, but growth will likely accelerate in the second half of the year, the World Bank said in a report Sunday. (published 0233 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WHO SPOKESMAN SAYS JAPAN FOOD SAFETY SITUATION 'SERIOUS' -REUTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The World Health Organization said Monday the detection of radiation in food was a more serious problem than the WHO had first expected, after the March 11 earthquake damaged a Japanese nuclear plant, Reuters news agency reported on its website. (published 0719 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PAYOUTS SEEN REACHING Y1T ON EARTHQUAKE POLICIES - NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The insurance industry expects payouts for earthquake coverage taken out by individuals to total a record Y1 trillion, eclipsing the Y80 billion from the 1995 temblor that devastated the Kobe area. (published 2047 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAX RELIEF ON THE TABLE FOR DISASTER AREAS - NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government is working on a set of emergency tax breaks for businesses and individuals to help speed reconstruction in the ravaged areas of eastern \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Nikkei reported. (published 2025 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: FURTHER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEPENDS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET MOVES - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry will likely stay on high alert against possible rebounds in the yen Monday, leaving the door open for further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after last week's concerted action by the world's central banks to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surge. (published 1907 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ(3/21) JAPAN PLANT HAD TROUBLED HISTORY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fukushima Daiichi power plant was already one of the most trouble-prone nuclear facilities in Japan, even before the devastating earthquake and tsunami that knocked out its cooling systems and precipitated the worst nuclear crisis in 25 years, a Wall Street Journal analysis of regulatory documents shows. (published 0238 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: MOODY'S: GENERAL ELECTRIC FACES NEGATIVE CREDIT RISKS FROM JAPAN'S NUCLEAR CRISIS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Electric Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., designer of the reactors at Japan's stricken Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Station, faces damage to its reputation and nuclear business as a result of the nuclear crisis in Japan, Moody's Investors Service said Monday. (published 0811 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-03-11 1021GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110321e73l000p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
