\par\par \b \uc2 FED WATCH: Fed Has Limited Yen War Chest For Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 12:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Fed, Treasury Yen Holdings Small Relative To Market Size\par \par \uc2 --Analysts Suspect Fed Not Just Broker, Is Using Its Own Holdings\par \par \uc2 --No Balance Sheet, Monetary Policy Implications To Fed Selling Its Yen\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Michael S. Derby \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The Federal Reserve's contribution to a global effort to weaken the yen is limited by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's war chest of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -like assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's modest holdings are indicative of the dollar's position as the world's key reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . That's meant the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not faced a need to accumulate much in the way of any foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , at least relative to the other holdings on its balance sheet. It also reflects U.S. policymakers' long-standing hands-off relationship with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While reports from traders about the size of the Fed's participation in Friday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were as uneven as one would expect in the absence of official confirmation--it seems they sold around $600 million--the overarching fact is the Fed does not have much yen to sell. The aim of weakening the yen is to prevent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciating value from hurting Japan's recovery prospects as it tries to rebound from last week's earthquake and resulting disaster.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to fourth quarter 2010 data from the Fed, the most recent available, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held a total of $11.922 billion in yen denominated assets. That consists of $3.882 billion in cash and $8.039 billion in securities, the type of which the Fed does not describe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's holdings may seem significant, but bear in mind that's part of a balance sheet that tops $2.6 trillion. Plus, the Fed would be trying to put those yen to work in a yen-dollar \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market that trades $570 billion every day. The Treasury's yen portfolio is similarly modest, at $15.775 billion. The Fed isn't that fat on other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  either, with its only other notable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holding its $14.127 billion in euros.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For some, the size of the Fed's holdings isn't all that germane, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could act as an agent on behalf of a large yen-holder--read the Bank of Japan--and sell \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  it does not itself possess, to the same market impact.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Fed has a balance sheet that is publicly available and eventually its activities will be known. A large part of what makes Friday's action more significant is that U.S. authorities, who have for many years been so reluctant to engage in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , are getting in the ring.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As policymakers have sought to reform the mortgage market they've made a big point of the value of having participants have "skin in the game," or exposure to activities they've undertaken. The impact of this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has more authority if the Fed and Treasury commit some of their own assets, and serve as more than a broker for other central banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ray Stone, of forecasting firm Stone & McCarthy Research, said the nature of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggested the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  money would come equally from the Fed and Treasury yen holdings, although he noted "I thought we would see greater validation" of this fact from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All of that points to participation by the Fed that's important, but largely symbolic. In this action, "the impact is not going to come from the G-7, non-Japan. The holdings are just too limited," said Tom Porcelli, economist with RBC Securities. The heavy lifting "is going to have to be done by the Bank of Japan" because they've got the yen to make a real difference, should that sort of force be required.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The mechanics of the Fed acquiring more yen for its balance sheet are unclear. The New York Fed declined to say how it possess the yen and yen-assets it has, and did not say how it could get more if it wanted. It seems unlikely the Fed's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap arrangements, which exist to provide dollar liquidity to major central banks, could be used to get more yen, given how they are set up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Observers agreed the Fed's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would in any case be too small to have any impact on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  balance sheet and thus its influence on the economy. What the Fed appears to have done is a "blink of an eye" compared to the daily ebb and flow of managing its holdings, Stone said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The forecasters of Wrightson ICAP said the Fed may have had another reason for embracing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . While the Fed would clearly be amenable to aiding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "We suspect that at least some FOMC [Federal Open Market Committee] members would be pleased by the idea of zeroing out the Fed's yen-denominated portfolio."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's because it would streamline the eventual contraction of the balance sheet and help limit the options of future policymakers who wish to pursue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for more typical goals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Michael S. Derby, a special writer with Dow Jones Newswires, has covered the Federal Reserve since 2001. He also writes about bond markets and the economy, and can be reached at 212 416 2214 or via email: michael.derby@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 03-21-11 0735ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1043 4043   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110321e73l0002l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
