\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/KRW May Fall; 1,117-1,125 Band Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 00:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2351 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW may fall from 1,124.90 late Monday in Seoul following the rise in the EUR/USD overnight amid anticipation for an interest rate hike by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . With worries over the damaged nuclear facilities in Japan subsiding, a recovery in investors' risk appetite may continue to help raise demand for the KRW, participants say. A local bank trader tips a 1,117-1,125 range for the session; "if the Kospi shows a sound performance, the pair is expected to fall below the 1,120 level." But uncertainties persist relating to the political unrest in the Middle East and north Africa and its impact on oil prices, so this may limit any fall in the pair, participants say. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] The continued threat of joint G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep downward pressure on the JPY, say dealers in Tokyo. A person familiar with international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy tells Dow Jones that the G7 pledge Friday to conduct joint JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be considered limited to that day. "It's now a lot harder for speculators to buy the yen, which means for the time being those kinds of speculative moves have likely been suppressed," says Hideaki Inoue, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY may test 82.00 amid ongoing concerns over the possibility that the Japanese MOF may conduct more JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Hideaki Inoue, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Trust and Banking. "I think the trend should be toward a weaker yen for now given the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the moves by the BOJ to ease further, which make it harder for speculators to buy the yen." With speculators sidelined, Inoue says moves will be driven by Japanese exporters, importers and other "real money" players. He says the USD/JPY's support around 80.00 looks solid for now and it's trading at 81.27. The EUR/JPY is at 115.50 and it may rise to 116.00. The EUR/USD is at 1.4206 and it may target 1.4283. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is tipped to rise, tracking strong gains in U.S. shares following news of a major telecoms deal, while easing concerns over the nuclear crisis in Japan will likely continue to support market sentiment, analysts say; they tip key resistance at the 60-day moving average near 2031. Foreigners' moves in the market will likely be in focus after their three-day buying streak, amid doubts that their buying will develop into a firm trend as uncertainties relating to the unrest in the Middle East and north Africa continue. Analysts cite securities firms and airline stocks as among their top picks following their recent weakness. The Kospi ends up 1.1% at 2003.42 Monday. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds are likely to hover in range trade with yields biased a tad higher on intermittent profit-taking, says a dealer with a local bank. "With a recovery in risk appetite and yields still at relatively high levels, investors are expected to continue to book profits amid a dearth of domestic leads," says the dealer; he adds, the focus will again be on short-term paper and Bank Negara notes as they are more liquid. Monday, the yield on August 2014 MGS ended up 2 bps at 3.38%, September 2016 MGS was up 3 bps at 3.56%, July 2021 MGS was up 5 bps at 4.01% and the September 2017 MGS was down 1 bp at 3.70%. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Brent crude could reach $150/bbl if oil supplies from another medium-sized exporter were disrupted, on top of lost Libyan supply totaling more than 1 million barrels a day, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . That scenario reflects the inability of Saudi Arabia to raise production to cover a wider shortfall in the oil market. "If large-scale unrest were to spread to Saudi Arabia and threaten Saudi crude exports, we could see Brent trade in a $150-$200/bbl range," SocGen says. However, it's central scenario is that there's no wider contagion and Brent crude oil prices won't trade much above $125, allowing global economic growth to remain strong over the remainder of 2011. SocGen adds that current Brent prices would be $95-$100 if there wasn't any geopolitical risk premium built in. Brent crude on the ICE futures exchange ended Monday $1.03 higher at $114.96/bbl. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2328 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year U.S. Treasury yield is expected to trade in a 3.0%-3.6% range this week, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Akihiro Nishida; "movements in long-term (Treasury) yields in the near term are likely to be affected by high uncertainty over the economic impact of the catastrophic earthquake and nuclear troubles in Fukushima, as well as turmoil in Libya and Bahrain," he says. But in the long-term, whether there will be a change of views in economic fundamentals and policy directions is more important. 10-year U.S. Treasurys fell to 102 16/32, yielding 3.326% at the NY close Monday, while 2-year notes stood at 99 31/32 to yield 0.637%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may rise following gains in U.S. and European stocks while Japanese markets were closed Monday for a public holiday. The index range for today is pegged at 9450-9600 vs Friday's close of 9206.75 (up 2.7%), according to Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. "There may be some share buying back among foreign investors as excessive concerns over the nuclear power plant situation recede," he says. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) continued to report progress in bringing under control reactors at the stricken Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant, although smoke was seen rising from two of the plant's six reactors as of Monday. While conditions remain uncertain, Nishi says market players are likely to expand their focus from the nuclear issue to U.S. economic data, Libya, and crude prices. Nikkei 225 June futures ended Chicago trading at 9505 Monday vs the Osaka close at 9130 Friday. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese utilities may seek an extra 5-7 million metric tons of spot LNG this year, and 8-11 million tons of LNG in 2012 to fill an energy supply gap caused by the recent earthquake and tsunami damaging nuclear and coal-fired power plants, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  analyst Marco Boeri. This compares with available spot capacity globally of around 70 million tons a year, and signals that some cargoes could be diverted from Europe. Most LNG regasification plants escaped the earthquake unscathed so Japan is able to cope with extra LNG import volumes. "All in all, we don't believe the oil to gas price parity--which would currently be $17-$18 per million British thermal units--will be attained in the short-term LNG market, as was the case in 2008," Boeri says. He expects some LNG price rises of $2-$3 per million British thermal units by the end of the year, on top of current levels of $11 per million British thermal units. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open lower on falls in U.S. Treasurys Monday; "financial markets, including the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, are regaining stability, and that's likely to be negative for the yen bond market," says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. Falls in JGB futures could widen, depending on whether and how much Tokyo shares rise, though the pace of improvement in market liquidity is expected to be slow, he adds. Lead June JGB futures are tipped in a 139.05-139.59 band vs 139.74 at their last close on Friday, while the benchmark 10-year yield is eyed in a 1.220%-1.255% range vs 1.210%.(megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2302 GMT [Dow Jones] Any push higher in the USD index will be limited to 75.75 given fading risk aversion and the better mood in equity markets, says \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos. "Event risk for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will come from familiar sources this week. In particular, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis and the possibility of further BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain in focus, as will the unrest in Libya and Middle East more generally. The effects of such on investors' risk appetite should largely dictate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market sentiment this week." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's February consumer price index likely rose 3.2% on-year, lower than January's 3.6% increase, according to the median forecast of seven economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. Economists say February's CPI eased compared to January's as the Lunar New Year was in early February, meaning that some holiday-linked price increases would have already been reflected in January's CPI. But they expect inflation to pick up again in March and peak around mid-2011 because of higher rents and global food and commodity prices. "Private housing rentals have registered 14 consecutive months of double-digit year-on-year growth due to tight supply. The anticipated tight future housing supply would likely keep private housing rentals on an uptrend," says Hang Seng bank chief economist Joanne Yim, adding that Hong Kong's CPI is tipped to rise 4.7% this year. The government is scheduled to release the February CPI data at 0830 GMT. (chester.yung@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-11 1951ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l000bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
