\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Australian Share Market Correction Complete -Herston\b0\par\par\uc2 1,676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's share market correction is complete, says Herston Economics chief economist Clifford Bennett. "What happened during the second half of last week with further bad news being unable to create new lows, and markets beginning to rally, was confirmation of our point of view," says Bennett. "Yet it is only today that we detect market sentiment starting to turn bullish. The comfortable and bid reaction of the market to the aggressive escalation of military involvement in Libya is a very strong sign indeed that markets in general have turned the corner." Index last up 0.1% at 4631.8. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand shares end up 0.3% at 3348.365, bolstered by a 0.3% gain in bellwether Telecom (TEL.NZ) to NZ$1.985 on heavy volume, although most involved off-market cross trades. "It has been a good day and U.S. futures are up quite strongly," which bodes well for Tuesday, says Macquarie Equities broker Brad Gordon. \uc2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\uc2  are up 0.9% at NZ$0.575 as investors digest news that it has entered an agreement with China's \uc2 Haier Group\uc2  for supply of components and technology that will generate annual revenues of NZ$20 million-NZ$35 million. Gordon notes, however that investors may be waiting for more details as the company has said it will require NZ$25 million of investment. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end up 1.6% at 468.25, as investors continue buying select blue-chip stocks after the government forecasts 1Q GDP to grow 5.5% on-year and exports to rise 29.7%. Dealers say sentiment is strong because players share the view that economy is expected to grow faster in 2Q. "Deputy Prime Ministr Nguyen Sinh Hung told lawmakers this morning that the government will implement measures to help the economy recover faster this year, and I expect the pace of economic growth to accelerate towards the second and third quarter, and it believe it will be the highest in the fourth quarter," says an analyst with MBCapital fund management. Among the most actives, Vietcombank (VCB.VH) ended up 4.4% at VND32,900, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) closed up 4.8% at VND19,600 and Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) finished 0.8% lower at VND24,800. Traders tips more gains Tuesday with resistance for the key index at 475. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR touches a near-two week low of 45.00 tracking a strong EUR/USD and flat local stocks that are alternating between gains and losses, says a trader with a state-run bank. The pair is at 45.03 vs 45.12 late Friday. "The rupee is mainly being supported by gains in the euro on expectations that the ECB may hike rates and on local dollar selling by exporters. The rupee is likely to hold its gains because no one wants to short the euro," he says. He notes that the USD may find support from oil importers at INR45 or below. He tips the pair in a 44.90-45.10 band. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is choppy, now up 0.2% at 17,907.93, and is likely to trade in a narrow range with an upward bias today in the absence of domestic triggers, says a local analyst. "We're seeing some bargain hunting but high oil prices and tension in the Middle-East are likely to weigh on the market," the analyst says. He tips state-owned fuel retailers such as \uc2 Indian Oil Corp\uc2 . (530965.BY) and \uc2 Bharat Petroleum Corp\uc2 . (500547.BY) to fall due to the rise in crude prices. A local dealer tips resistance for the Sensex at 18,000 and thereafter at 18,199, with support at 17,794. \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 . (500010.BY) is up 1.1% at INR627.90, while ITC (500875.BY) is up 0.9% at INR627.20. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) opens higher but is now off 0.7% at INR986.00 weighed by a media report Friday that the output from two gas fields operated by the company may fall. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects the People's Bank of China to hike the required reserve ratio again in April, as the large amount of bills and repos maturing next month will offset the effects of further liquidity tightening. The house also says that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates in April in a bid to tame inflation as "inflation is expected to rise further in the next couple of months, due to higher input prices and labor costs, as well as base effects." The PBOC announced its third 50 bps RRR hike for 2011 on Friday, effective March 25, which will bring the RRR to 20% for large banks and 18% for small banks. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 1.2% at 22,574.01 at midday, tracking gains in U.S. stocks although volume is not terribly exciting at HK$38.85 billion. Daiwa says Asian markets are looking vulnerable from a technical perspective, with almost all now below their 100-day moving averages (the HSI's 100-day MA is at 23,430). It adds bad news from Fukushima, especially if combined with a resurgence of problems in the Middle East, "could tip them into oversold territory (but) strong underlying fundamentals and relatively modest valuations should provide support." \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2  (2038.HK) is the best-performing blue chip and rises 4.2% to HK$4.97 on news the company plans to sell a unit to its parent company for CNY463.3 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] The devastating events in Japan have rocked market sentiment, slashed grains futures and sent dairy prices downwards, "but assuming the Japanese nuclear crisis is brought under control, we expect that the net impact of the earthquake and tsunami will prove relatively limited for dairy," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  its March 2011 Dairy Quarterly. It notes Japan is a top five global dairy importer and the number two cheese buyer on the international market. "Despite the heavy localized disruption and cost of rebuilding, history suggests that the net impact of similar natural disasters on GDP is likely to be limited once stimulatory responses are accounted for." It adds that from a dairy perspective there is likely to be little impact on milk supply, consumption or trade, and notes that cheese imports were largely unimpeded by the 1995 Kobe quake. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are lower as oil prices rise to $103 per barrel following air strikes on Libya as investors worry about a possible prolonged disruption in Libyan oil supply, says a trader with a primary dealership. The 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.68 from 99.84 at Friday's close. He adds, traders are avoiding taking long positions ahead of the fiscal year end and on a cash deficit in the banking system after companies withdrew heavily last week to pay advance tax. Indian banks borrowed INR1.4 trillion Friday to meet a liquidity shortfall. He tips the 2022 bond in a 99.60-99.90 band. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says the supply-side disruption from Japan's massive earthquake is more serious than earlier expected and will weigh on short-term growth in Asia. It says the fall in Japan's productive capacity may result in higher prices of inputs/final goods of items where Japan is a significant supplier until production recovers. "On the growth front, we could see production of goods reliant on Japanese inputs without sufficient inventories temporarily stall, such as electronics, autos, and shipbuilding," the report says. It notes that Thailand looks relatively more reliant on Japanese imports for key inputs such as electronics (30% of total imports), iron & steel (38%), vehicle parts (63%), while Korea and Taiwan seem to be reliant on imports of plastics and electronic components.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are up 1.6% at 468.94, with investors buying in select blue chip stocks after Deputy Prime Minister Nguyen Sinh Hung says 1Q GDP is expected to grow by 5.5% on-year, while exports are expected to rise 29.7%. "Sentiment is improving though there are some negative factors, including high inflation and tight credit supply. Players are also buying on 1Q earnings hopes," says a trader with Hanoi-based FPT Secrities. Volume is thin at 36 million shares valued at VND750 billion. Among advancers, \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 is up 4.9% at VND46,700, Vincom (VIC.VH) is up 4.3% at VND122,000 and \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) is up 2.3% at VND22,000. Traders tip immediate resistance for the key index at 470. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is ticking higher at 0.7332, although trading is very light, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager Murray Hindley. "With nothing really fresh out of Japan and with Tokyo on holiday, markets are paring back some of their shorts after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday," he says. Attention remains focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the Middle East "but we are seeing some natural demand out of the export sector on some of these crosses," Hindley says, adding that the AUD/USD has moved back over parity, which has also helped pull the Kiwi higher. He says support should hold at 0.7280 and a break through 0.7335 could lead to 0.7360 being targeted. (rebec