es.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is choppy, now up 0.2% at 17,907.93, and is likely to trade in a narrow range with an upward bias today in the absence of domestic triggers, says a local analyst. "We're seeing some bargain hunting but high oil prices and tension in the Middle-East are likely to weigh on the market," the analyst says. He tips state-owned fuel retailers such as \uc2 Indian Oil Corp\uc2 . (530965.BY) and \uc2 Bharat Petroleum Corp\uc2 . (500547.BY) to fall due to the rise in crude prices. A local dealer tips resistance for the Sensex at 18,000 and thereafter at 18,199, with support at 17,794. \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 . (500010.BY) is up 1.1% at INR627.90, while ITC (500875.BY) is up 0.9% at INR627.20. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) opens higher but is now off 0.7% at INR986.00 weighed by a media report Friday that the output from two gas fields operated by the company may fall. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects the People's Bank of China to hike the required reserve ratio again in April, as the large amount of bills and repos maturing next month will offset the effects of further liquidity tightening. The house also says that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates in April in a bid to tame inflation as "inflation is expected to rise further in the next couple of months, due to higher input prices and labor costs, as well as base effects." The PBOC announced its third 50 bps RRR hike for 2011 on Friday, effective March 25, which will bring the RRR to 20% for large banks and 18% for small banks. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 1.2% at 22,574.01 at midday, tracking gains in U.S. stocks although volume is not terribly exciting at HK$38.85 billion. Daiwa says Asian markets are looking vulnerable from a technical perspective, with almost all now below their 100-day mo