\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/INR Hits Near 2-Week Low On EUR/USD,Local Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR touches a near-two week low of 45.00 tracking a strong EUR/USD and flat local stocks that are alternating between gains and losses, says a trader with a state-run bank. The pair is at 45.03 vs 45.12 late Friday. "The rupee is mainly being supported by gains in the euro on expectations that the ECB may hike rates and on local dollar selling by exporters. The rupee is likely to hold its gains because no one wants to short the euro," he says. He notes that the USD may find support from oil importers at INR45 or below. He tips the pair in a 44.90-45.10 band. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is choppy, now up 0.2% at 17,907.93, and is likely to trade in a narrow range with an upward bias today in the absence of domestic triggers, says a local analyst. "We're seeing some bargain hunting but high oil prices and tension in the Middle-East are likely to weigh on the market," the analyst says. He tips state-owned fuel retailers such as \uc2 Indian Oil Corp\uc2 . (530965.BY) and \uc2 Bharat Petroleum Corp\uc2 . (500547.BY) to fall due to the rise in crude prices. A local dealer tips resistance for the Sensex at 18,000 and thereafter at 18,199, with support at 17,794. \uc2 Housing Development Finance Corp\uc2 . (500010.BY) is up 1.1% at INR627.90, while ITC (500875.BY) is up 0.9% at INR627.20. \uc2 Reliance Industries\uc2  (500325.BY) opens higher but is now off 0.7% at INR986.00 weighed by a media report Friday that the output from two gas fields operated by the company may fall. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0407 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  expects the People's Bank of China to hike the required reserve ratio again in April, as the large amount of bills and repos maturing next month will offset the effects of further liquidity tightening. The house also says that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will raise interest rates in April in a bid to tame inflation as "inflation is expected to rise further in the next couple of months, due to higher input prices and labor costs, as well as base effects." The PBOC announced its third 50 bps RRR hike for 2011 on Friday, effective March 25, which will bring the RRR to 20% for large banks and 18% for small banks. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is up 1.2% at 22,574.01 at midday, tracking gains in U.S. stocks although volume is not terribly exciting at HK$38.85 billion. Daiwa says Asian markets are looking vulnerable from a technical perspective, with almost all now below their 100-day moving averages (the HSI's 100-day MA is at 23,430). It adds bad news from Fukushima, especially if combined with a resurgence of problems in the Middle East, "could tip them into oversold territory (but) strong underlying fundamentals and relatively modest valuations should provide support." \uc2 Foxconn International\uc2  (2038.HK) is the best-performing blue chip and rises 4.2% to HK$4.97 on news the company plans to sell a unit to its parent company for CNY463.3 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] The devastating events in Japan have rocked market sentiment, slashed grains futures and sent dairy prices downwards, "but assuming the Japanese nuclear crisis is brought under control, we expect that the net impact of the earthquake and tsunami will prove relatively limited for dairy," says \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  its March 2011 Dairy Quarterly. It notes Japan is a top five global dairy importer and the number two cheese buyer on the international market. "Despite the heavy localized disruption and cost of rebuilding, history suggests that the net impact of similar natural disasters on GDP is likely to be limited once stimulatory responses are accounted for." It adds that from a dairy perspective there is likely to be little impact on milk supply, consumption or trade, and notes that cheese imports were largely unimpeded by the 1995 Kobe quake. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are lower as oil prices rise to $103 per barrel following air strikes on Libya as investors worry about a possible prolonged disruption in Libyan oil supply, says a trader with a primary dealership. The 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.68 from 99.84 at Friday's close. He adds, traders are avoiding taking long positions ahead of the fiscal year end and on a cash deficit in the banking system after companies withdrew heavily last week to pay advance tax. Indian banks borrowed INR1.4 trillion Friday to meet a liquidity shortfall. He tips the 2022 bond in a 99.60-99.90 band. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  says the supply-side disruption from Japan's massive earthquake is more serious than earlier expected and will weigh on short-term growth in Asia. It says the fall in Japan's productive capacity may result in higher prices of inputs/final goods of items where Japan is a significant supplier until production recovers. "On the growth front, we could see production of goods reliant on Japanese inputs without sufficient inventories temporarily stall, such as electronics, autos, and shipbuilding," the report says. It notes that Thailand looks relatively more reliant on Japanese imports for key inputs such as electronics (30% of total imports), iron & steel (38%), vehicle parts (63%), while Korea and Taiwan seem to be reliant on imports of plastics and electronic components.(chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares are up 1.6% at 468.94, with investors buying in select blue chip stocks after Deputy Prime Minister Nguyen Sinh Hung says 1Q GDP is expected to grow by 5.5% on-year, while exports are expected to rise 29.7%. "Sentiment is improving though there are some negative factors, including high inflation and tight credit supply. Players are also buying on 1Q earnings hopes," says a trader with Hanoi-based FPT Secrities. Volume is thin at 36 million shares valued at VND750 billion. Among advancers, \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 is up 4.9% at VND46,700, Vincom (VIC.VH) is up 4.3% at VND122,000 and \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) is up 2.3% at VND22,000. Traders tip immediate resistance for the key index at 470. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is ticking higher at 0.7332, although trading is very light, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager Murray Hindley. "With nothing really fresh out of Japan and with Tokyo on holiday, markets are paring back some of their shorts after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday," he says. Attention remains focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the Middle East "but we are seeing some natural demand out of the export sector on some of these crosses," Hindley says, adding that the AUD/USD has moved back over parity, which has also helped pull the Kiwi higher. He says support should hold at 0.7280 and a break through 0.7335 could lead to 0.7360 being targeted. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are a tad higher midday, after dipping into negative territory briefly, as the ongoing unrest in Libya fuels gains in oil companies and coal miners. The Shanghai Composite is up 0.2% at 2911.51 and analysts tip immediate resistance at 2950. But these gains may not be sustainable as "there is heightened uncertainty coming from different places affecting the market. You've got the unrest in Libya and the lingering radiation concerns from Japan, so investors could turn more cautious," says Zhou Xu, an analyst from Nanjing Securities. Among active shares, \uc2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (600028.SH) is up 0.4% at CNY8.58, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is up 1.6% at CNY11.75. Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 0.9% at CNY31.99, while \uc2 China Coal Energy\uc2  (601898.SH) is 0.7% higher at CNY11.28. The Shenzhen Index is down 0.3% at 1288.60. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mostly lower with local banks seen selling early amid light trade. Japan's nuclear crisis, which helped strengthen sentiment last week, is now becoming less of an issue, says a Bangkok-based dealer, while the air strikes in Libya are also not weighing much on sentiment. "There's not a lot of activity from offshore players yet, but I think we may start to see some more flows from there, with a focus on short term notes from six months to 1 year bonds," he says. The market is expected to remain lackluster Monday as it awaits fresh leads. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.28%/3.24% vs 3.26%/3.23% in late Asian trade Friday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.54%/3.51% vs 3.54%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.75%/3.72% vs 3.73%/3.68%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise overnight rates by 25 by from a record low of 4.0% for borrowing and 6.0% for lending at Thursday's policy meeting to stay ahead of the inflation curve, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists also project policy rates will be raised by a total 100 bps this year. "It is clear, especially after the release of the February CPI data, that inflationary pressures are intensifying," says Sherman Chan, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 ; "Our forecast shows that headline inflation is set to even breach the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (3%-5% target) as early as 2Q. To prevent runaway inflation, real interest rates should at least be positive." The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is expected to announce the results of its policy meeting around 0800 GMT Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-11 0014ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
