ion and tight credit supply. Players are also buying on 1Q earnings hopes," says a trader with Hanoi-based FPT Secrities. Volume is thin at 36 million shares valued at VND750 billion. Among advancers, \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 is up 4.9% at VND46,700, Vincom (VIC.VH) is up 4.3% at VND122,000 and \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) is up 2.3% at VND22,000. Traders tip immediate resistance for the key index at 470. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is ticking higher at 0.7332, although trading is very light, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager Murray Hindley. "With nothing really fresh out of Japan and with Tokyo on holiday, markets are paring back some of their shorts after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday," he says. Attention remains focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the Middle East "but we are seeing some natural demand out of the export sector on some of these crosses," Hindley says, adding that the AUD/USD has moved back over parity, which has also helped pull the Kiwi higher. He says support should hold at 0.7280 and a break through 0.7335 could lead to 0.7360 being targeted. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are a tad higher midday, after dipping into negative territory briefly, as the ongoing unrest in Libya fuels gains in oil companies and coal miners. The Shanghai Composite is up 0.2% at 2911.51 and analysts tip immediate resistance at 2950. But these gains may not be sustainable as "there is heightened uncertainty coming from different places affecting the market. You've got the unrest in Libya and the lingering radiation concerns from Japan, so investors could turn more cautious," says Zhou Xu, an analyst from Nanjing Securities. Among active shares, \uc2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (600028.SH) is up 0.4% at CNY8.58, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is up 1.6% at CNY11.75. Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 0.9% at CNY31.99, while \uc2 China Coal Energy\uc2  (601898.SH) is 0.7% higher at CNY11.28. The Shenzhen Index is down 0.3% at 1288.60. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mostly lower with local banks seen selling early amid light trade. Japan's nuclear crisis, which helped strengthen sentiment last week, is now becoming less of an issue, says a Bangkok-based dealer, while the air strikes in Libya are also not weighing much on sentiment. "There's not a lot of activity from offshore players yet, but I think we may start to see some more flows from there, with a focus on short term notes from six months to 1 year bonds," he says. The market is expected to remain lackluster Monday as it awaits fresh leads. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.28%/3.24% vs 3.26%/3.23% in late Asian trade Friday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.54%/3.51% vs 3.54%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.75%/3.72% vs 3.73%/3.68%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise overnight rates by 25 by from a record low of 4.0% for borrowing and 6.0% for lending at Thursday's policy meeting to stay ahead of the inflation curve, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists also project policy rates will be raised by a total 100 bps this year. "It is clear, especially after the release of the February CPI data, that inflationary pressures are intensifying," says Sherman Chan, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 ; "Our forecast shows that headline inflation is set to even breach the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (3%-5% target) as early as 2Q. To prevent runaway inflation, real interest rates should at least be positive." The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is expected to announce the results of its policy meeting around 0800 GMT Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower midday at 6.5650 from 6.5691 late Friday, but is trading in a tight 15-pip range; a Shanghai-based local bank trader tips immediate support at 6.5640. "Whether we are entering another round of PBOC-guided yuan appreciation remains to be seen. Exports are forecast to be lower for the first quarter due to the Japanese and Libyan crises, so personally I don't think the PBOC will let the yuan strengthen significantly for fear it will further hurt exporters," she says. The central parity is set at a fresh record-low of 6.5632 from 6.5668 Friday. The previous record was 6.5651 set Mar. 7. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4370/6.4400 from 6.4480/6.4550 late Friday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are lower, weighed by rising local stocks (the Taiex is +0.8%), says a local trader; "investors are not paying much attention to non-economic factors such as tensions in Libya and the situation in Japan," he notes. He adds the market has already priced in a 12.5 bps interest rate hike at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board meeting scheduled Mar.31. The most-traded 10-year government bond yield rises to 1.3412% vs 1.3376% in late Asian trade Friday; he tips a 1.3300%-1.3500% range. The five-year yield is at 1.0698% vs 1.0608% late Friday. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are higher on overseas cues, but gains limited by lingering concerns over oil prices and cost to the economy of Japan and Middle East woes. "While sentiment has improved, we continue to see low trading volume because of lingering uncertainty," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities; "investors are still waiting for the better picture on which companies will really take a hit." The benchmark PSEi is up 0.3% to 3849.94 in light volume; resistance is pegged at 3900. Leading advancers, which are beating decliners 63 to 44, include Cebu Air (CEB.PH), up 2.9% at PHP87.95, Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 1.4% at PHP62.15 and Ayala Land (ALI.PH), up 1.2% at PHP15.04. (cris.larano@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-11 0006ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
