d to remain lackluster Monday as it awaits fresh leads. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.28%/3.24% vs 3.26%/3.23% in late Asian trade Friday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.54%/3.51% vs 3.54%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.75%/3.72% vs 3.73%/3.68%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise overnight rates by 25 by from a record low of 4.0% for borrowing and 6.0% for lending at Thursday's policy meeting to stay ahead of the inflation curve, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists also project policy rates will be raised by a total 100 bps this year. "It is clear, especially after the release of the February CPI data, that inflationary pressures are intensifying," says Sherman Chan, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 ; "Our forecast shows that headline inflation is set to even breach the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (3%-5% target) as early as 2Q. To prevent runaway inflation, real interest rates should at least be positive." The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is expected to announce the results of its policy meeting around 0800 GMT Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower midday at 6.5650 from 6.5691 late Friday, but is trading in a tight 15-pip range; a Shanghai-based local bank trader tips immediate support at 6.5640. "Whether we are entering another round of PBOC-guided yuan appreciation remains to be seen. Exports are forecast to be lower for the first quarter due to the Japanese and Libyan crises, so personally I don't think the PBOC will let the yuan strengthen significantly for fear it will further hurt exporters," she says. The central parity is set at a fresh record-low of 6.5632 from 6.5668 Friday. The previous record was 6.5651 set Mar. 7. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4370/6.4400 from 6.4480/6.4550 late Friday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are lower, weighed by rising local stocks (the Taiex is +0.8%), says a local trader; "investors are not paying much attention to non-economic factors such as tensions in Libya and the situation in Japan," he notes. He adds the market has already priced in a 12.5 bps interest rate hike at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board meeting scheduled Mar.31. The most-traded 10-year government bond yield rises to 1.3412% vs 1.3376% in late Asian trade Friday; he tips a 1.3300%-1.3500% range. The five-year yield is at 1.0698% vs 1.0608% late Friday. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are higher on overseas cues, but gains limited by lingering concerns over oil prices and cost to the economy of Japan and Middle East woes. "While sentiment has improved, we continue to see low trading volume because of lingering uncertainty," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities; "investors are still waiting for the better picture on which companies will really take a hit." The benchmark PSEi is up 0.3% to 3849.94 in light volume; resistance is pegged at 3900. Leading advancers, which are beating decliners 63 to 44, include Cebu Air (CEB.PH), up 2.9% at PHP87.95, Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 1.4% at PHP62.15 and Ayala Land (ALI.PH), up 1.2% at PHP15.04. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is likely to open higher tracking gains in most Asian markets and is likely to be supported by bargain buying after having shed nearly 500 points over the past two sessions, says a local analyst. The SGX Nifty is now up 0.9%. "The situation in Japan appears to be stabilizing but tensions in the Middle-East are still a concern," the analyst says. He adds, high oil prices are also likely to limit gains today. He expects \uc2 Hero Honda Motors\uc2  (500182.BY) and Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) on value-buying following recent sharp falls in the wake of the earthquake in Japan. A local dealer tips the Sensex in a 17,750-18,050 range. The Sensex ended down 1.5% at 17,878.81 Friday. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com) [ 03-21-11 0006ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
