\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZD/USD Tad Higher As Markets Pare Back Short Positions - ANZ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is ticking higher at 0.7332, although trading is very light, says ANZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Manager Murray Hindley. "With nothing really fresh out of Japan and with Tokyo on holiday, markets are paring back some of their shorts after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday," he says. Attention remains focused on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and on the Middle East "but we are seeing some natural demand out of the export sector on some of these crosses," Hindley says, adding that the AUD/USD has moved back over parity, which has also helped pull the Kiwi higher. He says support should hold at 0.7280 and a break through 0.7335 could lead to 0.7360 being targeted. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0344 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are a tad higher midday, after dipping into negative territory briefly, as the ongoing unrest in Libya fuels gains in oil companies and coal miners. The Shanghai Composite is up 0.2% at 2911.51 and analysts tip immediate resistance at 2950. But these gains may not be sustainable as "there is heightened uncertainty coming from different places affecting the market. You've got the unrest in Libya and the lingering radiation concerns from Japan, so investors could turn more cautious," says Zhou Xu, an analyst from Nanjing Securities. Among active shares, \uc2 China Petroleum & Chemical Corp\uc2 . (600028.SH) is up 0.4% at CNY8.58, \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (601857.SH) is up 1.6% at CNY11.75. Yanzhou Mining (600188.SH) is up 0.9% at CNY31.99, while \uc2 China Coal Energy\uc2  (601898.SH) is 0.7% higher at CNY11.28. The Shenzhen Index is down 0.3% at 1288.60. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds are mostly lower with local banks seen selling early amid light trade. Japan's nuclear crisis, which helped strengthen sentiment last week, is now becoming less of an issue, says a Bangkok-based dealer, while the air strikes in Libya are also not weighing much on sentiment. "There's not a lot of activity from offshore players yet, but I think we may start to see some more flows from there, with a focus on short term notes from six months to 1 year bonds," he says. The market is expected to remain lackluster Monday as it awaits fresh leads. The bid/offer yield of bonds due December 2015 are quoted at 3.28%/3.24% vs 3.26%/3.23% in late Asian trade Friday, the October 2017 yield is at 3.54%/3.51% vs 3.54%/3.50% and the December 2021 yield is at 3.75%/3.72% vs 3.73%/3.68%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0333 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise overnight rates by 25 by from a record low of 4.0% for borrowing and 6.0% for lending at Thursday's policy meeting to stay ahead of the inflation curve, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Economists also project policy rates will be raised by a total 100 bps this year. "It is clear, especially after the release of the February CPI data, that inflationary pressures are intensifying," says Sherman Chan, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 ; "Our forecast shows that headline inflation is set to even breach the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (3%-5% target) as early as 2Q. To prevent runaway inflation, real interest rates should at least be positive." The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is expected to announce the results of its policy meeting around 0800 GMT Thursday. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0330 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY remains lower midday at 6.5650 from 6.5691 late Friday, but is trading in a tight 15-pip range; a Shanghai-based local bank trader tips immediate support at 6.5640. "Whether we are entering another round of PBOC-guided yuan appreciation remains to be seen. Exports are forecast to be lower for the first quarter due to the Japanese and Libyan crises, so personally I don't think the PBOC will let the yuan strengthen significantly for fear it will further hurt exporters," she says. The central parity is set at a fresh record-low of 6.5632 from 6.5668 Friday. The previous record was 6.5651 set Mar. 7. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4370/6.4400 from 6.4480/6.4550 late Friday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds are lower, weighed by rising local stocks (the Taiex is +0.8%), says a local trader; "investors are not paying much attention to non-economic factors such as tensions in Libya and the situation in Japan," he notes. He adds the market has already priced in a 12.5 bps interest rate hike at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board meeting scheduled Mar.31. The most-traded 10-year government bond yield rises to 1.3412% vs 1.3376% in late Asian trade Friday; he tips a 1.3300%-1.3500% range. The five-year yield is at 1.0698% vs 1.0608% late Friday. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are higher on overseas cues, but gains limited by lingering concerns over oil prices and cost to the economy of Japan and Middle East woes. "While sentiment has improved, we continue to see low trading volume because of lingering uncertainty," says Luz Lorenzo, market strategist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities; "investors are still waiting for the better picture on which companies will really take a hit." The benchmark PSEi is up 0.3% to 3849.94 in light volume; resistance is pegged at 3900. Leading advancers, which are beating decliners 63 to 44, include Cebu Air (CEB.PH), up 2.9% at PHP87.95, Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), up 1.4% at PHP62.15 and Ayala Land (ALI.PH), up 1.2% at PHP15.04. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is likely to open higher tracking gains in most Asian markets and is likely to be supported by bargain buying after having shed nearly 500 points over the past two sessions, says a local analyst. The SGX Nifty is now up 0.9%. "The situation in Japan appears to be stabilizing but tensions in the Middle-East are still a concern," the analyst says. He adds, high oil prices are also likely to limit gains today. He expects \uc2 Hero Honda Motors\uc2  (500182.BY) and Maruti Suzuki (532500.BY) on value-buying following recent sharp falls in the wake of the earthquake in Japan. A local dealer tips the Sensex in a 17,750-18,050 range. The Sensex ended down 1.5% at 17,878.81 Friday. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is lower, weighed by USD weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike interest rates by 25 bps this week. The pair is last at 43.57 from 43.70 late Friday, in moderate volume. "We expect a tight trading range today with the likelihood of an interest rate hike this Thursday although anything can happen until then," says a local bank trader, who pegs next support at 43.50 while resistance is tipped at 43.80. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares are up 0.9% at 1012.17 in moderate trade, though the market is expected to be volatile with some improvement in the nuclear situation in Japan, while tensions in Libya escalated over the weekend, says KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul. He says unrest in the Middle East remains the major swing factor for traders, but should imply further upside for oil prices in the near-term, which should offer support for Thai energy plays, which make up over 30% of total market capitalization. He tips resistance at 1015, with support at 1000. Among the most actively traded stocks, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is up 2.5% at THB51.25, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) is up 0.8% at THB24.90, PTT (PTT.TH) is up 1.2% at THB341, \uc2 PTT Exploration and Production\uc2  (PTTEP.TH) is up 0.8% at THB181.50, and Land & Houses (LH.TH) is up 4.7% at THB5.60. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are up 0.3% at 3503.081 in light volume, helped by a stronger rupiah and gains in most Asian markets, traders say. They expect the main index to top 3520. "The main index is moving in a very limited range which could reflect caution over the situations in Japan and Libya," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. Among gainers: cigarette maker \uc2 Gudang Garam\uc2  (GGRM.JK) is up 1.8% at IDR42,500, food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) is up 1.6% at IDR4,925, while BCA (BBCA.JK) is up 1.5% at IDR6,650.(edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/INR is expected to open lower mirroring strength in the EUR/USD and the slight buoyancy in Asian stocks, which may rub off on local indices, says a senior trader with a privately-run bank. He says the pair may open around 45.00 vs 45.12 late Friday and may trade in a narrow band of 44.95-45.10 through the session. "Even though oil prices have edged up at $103 per barrel, I think the positives for the rupee far outweigh the negatives," he says. He notes with a holiday in Tokyo and just a few days to go before the Indian fiscal year closes on March 31, interbank trading activity could be muted. Foreign funds were net sellers of a provisional INR5.24 billion worth of Indian stocks on Friday, One-month NDFs are quoting at 45.37/45.41. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 03-20-11 2344ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
