\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Philippine Shares May Rise; Cap At 3900\b0\par\par\uc2 1,610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may advance on gains in Wall Street and easing concerns over the nuclear crisis in Japan. The bellwether PSEi may head to 3880 after gaining 0.6% to 3839.88 on Friday, with traders pegging support at 3800 and resistance at 3900. "The Dow Futures is also moving higher while the Libyan situation may be shortened given the UN action," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. The market's expected advance may again be led by blue chips PLDT (TEL.PH), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aboitiz Equity Ventures\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AEV.PH) and Alliance Global Group (AGI.PH). (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is at 7.7994, steady with late Friday's level. "If the local stock market performs well today, it will provide support to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says a senior trader at a Chinese bank. He tips the pair is biased down, with support at the level of 7.7970 and he says the pair is unlikely to rise above 7.8000 during the session. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] China's latest reserve requirement ratio hike of 50bps, announced Friday, appears to be a more compromised solution than an outright rate hike due to concerns about the Japanese earthquake's economic impact. The house estimates the latest hike will freeze about CNY363 billion of liquidity in the banking system. "After the intensive tightening over the past six months (six RRR hikes, three interest-rate hikes, plus aggressive tightening measures targeting the property market), we believe we are now near the end of the tightening campaign. Weaker-than-expected loan growth, money growth, retail sales and export numbers for February should also give policy makers more reassurance in their tightening efforts thus far." Daiwa forecasts one more 50bp RRR hike and one more 25bp interest-rate hike this year, tipping slight upside risk for the latter. The house estimates even with just one more 25bp rate hike for the rest of 2011, real interest rates should turn positive by 4Q11, with Daiwa expecting CPI inflation to fall to 2.6% on-year by then. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB falls to 30.28-30.30 from 30.31-30.32 late Friday in Asia, tracking a stronger EUR tone, though trade is tepid. Easing concerns over Japan's nuclear crisis is also bolstering baht sentiment, but the THB's upside is likely limited, says a dealer at a local bank; "concerns over the tensions in Libya are escalating (after the UN approved the no-fly zone in Libya) and the appetite for safe-haven assets such as the dollar is likely to be seen in the next one to two days." He tips support at 30.25 and resistance at 30.40. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Asia inflation risks are skewed to the upside, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  strategists, noting its AlphaWise survey results with a majority of respondents expressing worries over prices pressures. "We continue to see significant upside risks to our upwardly revised inflation outlook," they say, and expect Asia-wide inflation, excluding Japan and India, to peak at 5.7% in the June 2011 quarter instead of 4.9% previously forecast. "Our core thesis on (Asia ex Japan) inflation is that a combination of relatively strong domestic demand, recent stronger-than-expected external demand, a lagged response from policy makers, and supply shocks in form of higher food and global commodity prices bring upside risks to inflation forecasts." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0107 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP may decline due to an expected increase in remittance inflows, while the EUR's recent strength vs the greenback is likely to support risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair is tipped to fall to 43.60 after settling at 43.70 in late Asian trade Friday; traders peg support at 43.50 and resistance at 44. "We're entering the remittance season because of the coming graduations and that should help the peso," says \uc2 Marcelo Ayes\uc2 , senior vice president at \uc2 Rizal Commercial Banking Corp\uc2 . (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] Air strikes against Libya over the weekend will heighten volatility in Nymex crude futures, but the strength of any rally will likely depend less on military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya than on unfolding events in Bahrain, says Xinyi Chen, an analyst at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Nymex crude for April delivery is up $2.20 at $103.27/bbl on Globex; ICE Brent crude is up $2.26 at $116.19/bbl. "The market has come to accept that oil output from Libya, the 1.65 million barrels a day of production, will be out of the market for some time" due to the U.N.-sanctioned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Chen says. How Western nations react to protests in Bahrain, especially if the unrest worsens, is a bigger concern as it's on the doorstep of several big oil producers, including Saudi Arabia, Chen says. The U.S. is a major ally of Saudi Arabia, but its stance during the Middle East unrest has been to back the right of protesters to air their grievances peacefully and without fear of a crackdown. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI may open higher, tracking gains on Wall Street Friday, with Mark To, head of research at Wing Fung Financial, tipping the index may rise as Japan continues cooling efforts at its Fukushima nuclear reactors. He expects the index could mostly trade in a 22,300-22,500 range today, but adds the uncertainty in Libya after the weekend strikes by U.S. and European forces is expected to cap gains. Also, the PBOC's third reserve requirement ratio hike this year may also dampen interest in mainland banks and developers, but as tightening moves have been well anticipated, Wing Fung's To doesn't expect these sectors to take a big hit. "Trading sentiment is expected to be a bit mixed, and I think any gains in the local index won't be major." The HSI ends up 0.1% at 22,300.23 Friday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is stable in early Asia trade, as questions of further G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen hang over the market. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nuclear crisis, combined with the overhang from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yen-selling, sent USD/Asia crosses on a wild ride last week and the market seems to be awaiting fresh cues. Over the weekend, Japan made progress on getting its nuclear crisis under control, which could bring some risk appetite back, but airstrikes in Libya could offset any improved sentiment and favor the USD. The "conflicting global themes" make the outlook "hazy," says a local bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, but the USD/SGD should trade in a range of 1.2700-1.2780. The pair is at 1.2734 from 1.2744 in late Asian trade Friday. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are expected to consolidate with a downward bias, as the latest hike in banks' reserve requirement ratio will further squeeze liquidity. Analysts tip the Shanghai Composite Index in a 2850-2900 band for most of the session; the index ended up 0.3% at 2906.89 Friday. "There's no catalyst for the stock market to rise," says Jacky Zhang at Capital Securities, citing uncertainties ranging from still-high inflation to an uninspiring prospect for exporters. "The RRR hike, which came after Japan's earthquake and its aftermath, shows the Chinese government remains hawkish in fighting inflation," he says. China said Friday it will raise the share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by half a percentage point, the third increase this year. The Shenzhen Composite Index rose 0.6% to 1292.93 Friday. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may stay in a narrow range as investors assess options with an eye on a possible stock market recovery from last week's selloff, an analyst at a local bank says. Key themes remain the unrest in Libya and Japan's earthquake and nuclear crisis, but domestic inflationary woes may weigh, says Selena Ling, OCBC's Head of Treasury Research. "Looking at U.S. Treasurys on Friday, the risk aversion trade-off remains. The (bond price) range may not be too dramatic, depending on where Asian equity markets go today." She tips the two-year bond yield to stay flat and the 10-year yield may ease 1-2 bps. On Friday, two-year yields rose 2 bps to 0.38%, while the 10-year yield climbed 7 bps to 2.37%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is higher at 1,128.40, vs 1,126.60 late Friday in Seoul as uncertainties over Libya provides upward bias for the pair. Fears over the nuclear facilities in Japan have eased a bit, but ongoing military tensions in Libya and the possibility of oil price hikes weigh on the risk-sensitive KRW, traders say. A local bank trader tips a 1,124-1,132 band for the session with firm support from USD buying by importers and fund repatriation related to dividend payouts for foreign investors. But sharp gains from here look unlikely amid the Kospi's 0.5% gains, traders say. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com) [ 03-20-11 2131ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
