e share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by half a percentage point, the third increase this year. The Shenzhen Composite Index rose 0.6% to 1292.93 Friday. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may stay in a narrow range as investors assess options with an eye on a possible stock market recovery from last week's selloff, an analyst at a local bank says. Key themes remain the unrest in Libya and Japan's earthquake and nuclear crisis, but domestic inflationary woes may weigh, says Selena Ling, OCBC's Head of Treasury Research. "Looking at U.S. Treasurys on Friday, the risk aversion trade-off remains. The (bond price) range may not be too dramatic, depending on where Asian equity markets go today." She tips the two-year bond yield to stay flat and the 10-year yield may ease 1-2 bps. On Friday, two-year yields rose 2 bps to 0.38%, while the 10-year yield climbed 7 bps to 2.37%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is higher at 1,128.40, vs 1,126.60 late Friday in Seoul as uncertainties over Libya provides upward bias for the pair. Fears over the nuclear facilities in Japan have eased a bit, but ongoing military tensions in Libya and the possibility of oil price hikes weigh on the risk-sensitive KRW, traders say. A local bank trader tips a 1,124-1,132 band for the session with firm support from USD buying by importers and fund repatriation related to dividend payouts for foreign investors. But sharp gains from here look unlikely amid the Kospi's 0.5% gains, traders say. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may fall, after China's decision to hike banks' reserve requirement ratio for the third time this year, says a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank; Monday's central parity may be set around 6.5650, compared to 6.5668 on Friday. "There was upward pressure on the dollar against the yuan last week, despite the PBOC's efforts to stabilize the yuan's exchange rate amid volatile markets overseas. The hike in the banks' RRR will soak up some liquidity and prop up the yuan, making sure it stays strong amid high domestic inflation," the trader says. But the unrest in Libya, where U.S. and coalition forces launched military strikes at the weekend, could drive safe-haven demand for the dollar, limiting any yuan gains. The pair was at 6.5691 late Friday. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is up 0.7% at 1995.80 in early trade on modest foreign buying interest after U.S. shares end higher Friday, with stocks that underperformed recently like heavy industry and airline stocks leading gains, analysts say; resistance is tipped at 2000. "Though we can't yet be confident about (any) improvement regarding Japan's nuclear issue," the situation with Japan's quake-damaged nuclear reactors is likely to have passed its worst, which is helping overall market sentiment, says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2  analyst Won Jong-hyuck; but he doesn't expect any strong buying momentum from foreign investors yet. \uc2 Doosan Heavy Industries\uc2  & Construction (034020.SE) is up 3.4% at KRW63,600, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  (042660.SE) is 2.7% higher at KRW34,000, while \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) rises 2.8% to KRW39,250; \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) is up 3.1% at KRW59,600 and \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) 4.9% higher at KRW9,250. Oil refining related stocks are higher, with \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) +0.2% at KRW210,000 and S-Oil (010950.SE) +1.1% at KRW141,500. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Any upside for Singapore shares from Wall Street's rise Friday is likely to be minimal, as the market remains biased lower amid ongoing worries over Japan and the military action in Libya. Friday, the STI closed down 0.2% at 2935.78. "With the air strikes in Libya over the weekend, I think the oil price should move higher today, which means the market this week is likely to be on the weaker side, and there's also the ongoing Japan nuclear crisis," says a trader at a local brokerage. He adds, "Friday we had a weak debut for HPH Trust (NS8U.SG), so I would think that is a signal for the market to go down further." He says Friday's STI close corresponds with support at 2935 and the next support would be about 2910; "I expect the market to test this level this week." Thin corporate news flow is expected and the SGX (S68.SG) may be in focus after reports that its ASX bid is likely to be opposed by Australia's government. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com) [ 03-20-11 2122ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110321e73l0000t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
