\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Steady As JPY Eyed; Support Tipped At 1.2700\b0\par\par\uc2 1,660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is stable in early Asia trade, as questions of further G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the yen hang over the market. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's nuclear crisis, combined with the overhang from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yen-selling, sent USD/Asia crosses on a wild ride last week and the market seems to be awaiting fresh cues. Over the weekend, Japan made progress on getting its nuclear crisis under control, which could bring some risk appetite back, but airstrikes in Libya could offset any improved sentiment and favor the USD. The "conflicting global themes" make the outlook "hazy," says a local bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, but the USD/SGD should trade in a range of 1.2700-1.2780. The pair is at 1.2734 from 1.2744 in late Asian trade Friday. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are expected to consolidate with a downward bias, as the latest hike in banks' reserve requirement ratio will further squeeze liquidity. Analysts tip the Shanghai Composite Index in a 2850-2900 band for most of the session; the index ended up 0.3% at 2906.89 Friday. "There's no catalyst for the stock market to rise," says Jacky Zhang at Capital Securities, citing uncertainties ranging from still-high inflation to an uninspiring prospect for exporters. "The RRR hike, which came after Japan's earthquake and its aftermath, shows the Chinese government remains hawkish in fighting inflation," he says. China said Friday it will raise the share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by half a percentage point, the third increase this year. The Shenzhen Composite Index rose 0.6% to 1292.93 Friday. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds may stay in a narrow range as investors assess options with an eye on a possible stock market recovery from last week's selloff, an analyst at a local bank says. Key themes remain the unrest in Libya and Japan's earthquake and nuclear crisis, but domestic inflationary woes may weigh, says Selena Ling, OCBC's Head of Treasury Research. "Looking at U.S. Treasurys on Friday, the risk aversion trade-off remains. The (bond price) range may not be too dramatic, depending on where Asian equity markets go today." She tips the two-year bond yield to stay flat and the 10-year yield may ease 1-2 bps. On Friday, two-year yields rose 2 bps to 0.38%, while the 10-year yield climbed 7 bps to 2.37%. (chunhan.wong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is higher at 1,128.40, vs 1,126.60 late Friday in Seoul as uncertainties over Libya provides upward bias for the pair. Fears over the nuclear facilities in Japan have eased a bit, but ongoing military tensions in Libya and the possibility of oil price hikes weigh on the risk-sensitive KRW, traders say. A local bank trader tips a 1,124-1,132 band for the session with firm support from USD buying by importers and fund repatriation related to dividend payouts for foreign investors. But sharp gains from here look unlikely amid the Kospi's 0.5% gains, traders say. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may fall, after China's decision to hike banks' reserve requirement ratio for the third time this year, says a Shanghai-based trader at a local bank; Monday's central parity may be set around 6.5650, compared to 6.5668 on Friday. "There was upward pressure on the dollar against the yuan last week, despite the PBOC's efforts to stabilize the yuan's exchange rate amid volatile markets overseas. The hike in the banks' RRR will soak up some liquidity and prop up the yuan, making sure it stays strong amid high domestic inflation," the trader says. But the unrest in Libya, where U.S. and coalition forces launched military strikes at the weekend, could drive safe-haven demand for the dollar, limiting any yuan gains. The pair was at 6.5691 late Friday. (esther.fung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is up 0.7% at 1995.80 in early trade on modest foreign buying interest after U.S. shares end higher Friday, with stocks that underperformed recently like heavy industry and airline stocks leading gains, analysts say; resistance is tipped at 2000. "Though we can't yet be confident about (any) improvement regarding Japan's nuclear issue," the situation with Japan's quake-damaged nuclear reactors is likely to have passed its worst, which is helping overall market sentiment, says \uc2 SK Securities\uc2  analyst Won Jong-hyuck; but he doesn't expect any strong buying momentum from foreign investors yet. \uc2 Doosan Heavy Industries\uc2  & Construction (034020.SE) is up 3.4% at KRW63,600, \uc2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\uc2  (042660.SE) is 2.7% higher at KRW34,000, while \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) rises 2.8% to KRW39,250; \uc2 Korean Air Lines\uc2  (003490.SE) is up 3.1% at KRW59,600 and \uc2 Asiana Airlines\uc2  (020560.SE) 4.9% higher at KRW9,250. Oil refining related stocks are higher, with \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) +0.2% at KRW210,000 and S-Oil (010950.SE) +1.1% at KRW141,500. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] Any upside for Singapore shares from Wall Street's rise Friday is likely to be minimal, as the market remains biased lower amid ongoing worries over Japan and the military action in Libya. Friday, the STI closed do