\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex May Test 17,930 Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese markets are closed Monday for Vernal Equinox Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                11858.52  +83.93   +0.71% \par Nasdaq              2643.67   +7.62    +0.29% \par S&P 500             1279.20   +5.48    +0.43% \par FTSE 100            5718.13   +22.02   +0.39% \par Nikkei Stock        n/a       n/a      n/a \par Hang Seng           22588.92  +288.69  +1.29% \par Kospi               2000.35   +19.22   +0.97% \par SGX Nifty           5429.00   +46.00   +0.85% \par March contract \par  \par USD/JPY             80.87-90     +0.25% \par Range               80.90-80.58 \par EUR/USD             1.4195-97    +0.12% \par Range               1.4197-1.4175 \par  \par CBOT Wheat May      $7.230 per bushel \par Comex Gold          $1,416.10   +$11.90 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,402.20-$1,424.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $101.07     -$0.35 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's 30-share Sensitive Index could rise to 17,930, according to charts.\par \par \uc2 While there is a possibility of a brief upward move, the Sensex has to move past major resistance at 18,470 to indicate a reversal of the short-term downtrend.\par \par \uc2 Unless the index moves above 18,470, it would be vulnerable to a test of minor support at 17,680. The downtrend would gather momentum on a fall below major support at 17,450.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could test resistance at 5,410.\par \par \uc2 But the Nifty has to close above major resistance at 5,540 to indicate a reversal of the short-term bullish trend. Else, a drift to minor support at 5,340 appears likely.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks climbed Friday, as fears of increased violence in Libya ebbed and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helped to relieve investor worries over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Construction-equipment manufacturer Caterpillar said disruptions in its Japanese supply chain could sporadically affect its assembly plants elsewhere and that it is trying to secure other sources for parts normally produced in Japan. Caterpillar's stock rose 1.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among other stocks in focus, a number of companies announced dividends Friday. Among them, Cisco rose 0.8%, after disclosing its first-ever cash dividend. \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan Chase\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 2.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 1.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks pulled back from their highs of the day Friday afternoon as traders prepared their portfolios to ride out a potentially turbulent weekend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mixed Monday, as optimism over progress in resolving Japan's nuclear crisis was offset by concerns over military action in Libya by allied forces.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Kospi Composite and New Zealand's NZX-50 were both up, while Australia's S&P/ASX 200 was down. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were 41 points higher in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy stocks were outperforming, with Australia's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Woodside Petroleum\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 1.5% and Santos 1.7% higher, while in South Korea, S-Oil rose 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian uranium stocks continued to recover from heavy losses caused by the Japanese nuclear crisis. Paladin was up 3.1% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Extract Resources\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 7.7%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's reserve ratio requirement hike Friday had little impact on Australian miners as the move had been well flagged. BHP Billiton was down 0.5% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Modest foreign buying in recently-beaten-down stocks helped boost the Seoul market, with heavy industry firms and airline companies leading the gainers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Doosan Heavy Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  & Construction rose 3.4%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daewoo Shipbuilding & Marine Engineering\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 2.7% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Korean Air Lines\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 3.1%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen was weighed by the prospect of further moves by major central banks to weaken it even as the safe-haven Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was supported by ongoing military action in Libya and a surge in oil prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at Y80.89, from Y80.62 in late New York trade Friday, while the euro was at Y114.58, from 114.34 and at $1.4161, from $1.4175.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investor demand for a safe haven lifted gold prices Friday as worries about Japan and the Group of Seven's attempts to weaken the yen outweighed the influence of a ceasefire in Libya.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,425.90 per troy ounce, up $6.20 from its New York settlement Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals closed mostly higher on the London Metal Exchange Friday supported by a stronger euro, although fears of slowing demand from China kept the exchange's flagship copper contract in negative territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libyan headlines sent crude oil prices higher and fueled concerns expensive crude could derail global economic growth. April Nymex crude oil futures were up $2.15 at $103.22 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS March 21 \par 1130 IST Mumbai:      Heavy industries minister inaugurates national \par                       corporate social responsibility hub \par 1130 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motorcycle & Scooter India\f28 \uc2 \f2  president and \par                       chief executive at press meeting \par 1230 GMT US:          February Chicago Fed national activity index \par 1400 GMT US:          February existing home sales \par 1400 GMT EU:          European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president at quarterly \par                       session with MEPs \par N/A      EU:          Euro-zone finance ministers extraordinary \par                       meeting \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par UPDATE: FURTHER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DEPENDS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET MOVES - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry will likely stay on high alert against possible rebounds in the yen Monday, leaving the door open for further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after last week's concerted action by the world's central banks to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's surge. (published 1907 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD BANK: JAPAN'S ECONOMY TO SLOW TEMPORARILY AFTER DISASTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy will temporarily slow after this month's devastating earthquake and tsunami, but growth will likely accelerate in the second half of the year, the World Bank said in a report Sunday. (published 0200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WSJ: JAPAN MAKES GAINS IN BATTLING NUCLEAR REACTORS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan appears to have turned the tide in its battle to stave off nuclear disaster, restoring power to parts of the Fukushima Daiichi plant and bringing down radiation levels with a marathon water-spraying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 2134 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 3RD WSJ UPDATE: US, COALITION FORCES ATTACK LIBYA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In one opening salvo, U.S. and U.K. forces unleashed around 110 Tomahawk cruise missiles against Libyan targets. Vice Adm. William Gortney Saturday told reporters that the missiles, which struck Libya around 3:00 p.m. EDT, were aimed at "more than 20" Libyan air-defense sites. (published 1024 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EMERGING COUNTRIES MUST MOVE QUICKLY AGAINST INFLATION - IMF'S LIPSKY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many emerging economies are in danger of overheating and should act quickly to tighten monetary policy, a top International Monetary Fund official said. (published 0158 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE DALE: UK RECOVERY SET TO CONTINUE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.K.'s economic recovery looks set to continue, the Bank of England's chief economist said Monday. (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RIO TINTO EXTENDS A$4 BILLION RIVERSDALE BID BY 3 TRADING DAYS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Monday it will extend its near-A$4 billion bid for Mozambique coking coal miner \f28 \uc2 \f2 Riversdale Mining Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . by three trading days. (published 0040 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LUNDIN RECOMMENDS SHAREHOLDERS REJECT EQUINOX BID \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lundin Mining Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (LUN.T) said Sunday its board "unanimously" recommends the copper mining company's shareholders reject a C$4.8 billon hostile bid from rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Equinox Minerals Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (EQN.T), as it fights to complete its friendly merger of equals with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Inmet Mining Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (IMN.T)(published 2255 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GM KOREA\f28 \uc2 \f2  CUTS PRODUCTION FROM MONDAY ON POSSIBLE LACK OF PARTS FROM JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GM Korea Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it will reduce production to prepare for a possible lack of automotive parts coming from Japan in the wake of the massive earthquake there. (published 0135 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO Makes His Boldest Move With T-Mobile Deal \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When it comes to running \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (T), Chief Executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Randall Stephenson\f28 \uc2 \f2  isn't afraid to make the high-stakes wager, and his $39 billion bid to acquire \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Telekom AG\f28 \uc2 \f2  (DTEGY) marks his boldest--and riskiest--bet yet. (published 2341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINE - Indian Media \par Monnet Ispat Acquires Coal Mine In Indonesia - Business Standard \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-03-11 0300GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020110321e73l00005\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
