\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Libya Assault Could Inject Volatility Into Markets; Eyes Also On Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 3,098 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 23:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese markets are closed for Vernal Equinox Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11858.52  +83.93  +0.71% \par Nasdaq     2643.67   +7.62  +0.29% \par S&P 500    1279.20   +5.48  +0.43% \par FTSE 100   5718.13  +22.02  +0.39% \par Xetra DAX  6664.40   +7.52  +0.11% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 2/32 flat  0.593% flat \par 5Y Tsy  100 27/32 -5/32 1.945% +3.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 102 29/32 -6/32 3.279% +2.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY       80.87-90     +0.25% \par Range         80.90-80.58 \par EUR/USD       1.4195-97    +0.12% \par Range         1.4197-1.4175 \par AUD/USD       0.9956-62    -0.07% \par Range         0.9962-0.9929 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   12.95/13.55% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   10.10/10.50% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   11.70/12.30% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,416.10   +$11.90 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,402.20-$1,424.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $101.07    -$0.35 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks climbed Friday, as fears of increased violence in Libya ebbed and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to relieve investor worries over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished Friday with a gain of 83.93 points, or 0.71%, to close at 11858.52, ending a week of tumultuous trading that on Wednesday briefly sent major U.S. indexes into negative territory for the year. It was the Dow's second straight losing week and third down week in the last four.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite, still below even for the year, added 7.62, or 0.29%, to 2643.67 Friday, but losses amid the recent nuclear worries made it the Nasdaq's worst week since mid-August. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 5.49, or 0.43%, to 1279.21, as it closed out its worst week since November.\par \par \uc2 Construction-equipment manufacturer Caterpillar said disruptions in its Japanese supply chain could sporadically affect its assembly plants elsewhere and that it is trying to secure other sources for parts normally produced in Japan. Caterpillar's stock rose $1.94, or 1.9%, to $105.06.\par \par \uc2 Among other stocks in focus, a number of companies announced dividends Friday. Among them, Cisco rose 14 cents, or 0.8%, to $17.14, after disclosing its first-ever cash dividend. \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  jumped $1.18, or 2.7%, to $45.74 and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  added 47 cents, or 1.5%, to $31.83.\par \par \uc2 Stocks pulled back from their highs of the day Friday afternoon as traders prepared their portfolios to ride out a potentially turbulent weekend.\par \par \uc2 After tumbling for the week's first three days, stocks rose Thursday and added further gains Friday. The latest came after Libya's foreign minister said Moammar Gadhafi's regime had declared an immediate cease-fire in the wake of the United Nations resolution passed late Thursday authorizing military action against Gadhafi's security forces.\par \par \uc2 In the event, U.S. and coalition forces launched military strikes against Libya Saturday after Gadhafi's troops continued an assault on the rebel stronghold of Benghazi.\par \par \uc2 U.S. radar-jamming aircraft and combat jets flew sorties to strike Gadhafi's ground forces and air defences, as coalition forces moved quickly to impose a no-fly zone over Libya.\par \par \uc2 In Tripoli, a defiant Gadhafi said he would arm all Libyans and called on citizens, especially those in the eastern rebel bastion of Benghazi, to rise up against what he called a foreign aggression to occupy the country and steal its oil wealth.\par \par \uc2 A Libyan military spokesman announced a new cease-fire effective 1900 GMT Sunday, as a second night of international airstrikes on the country appeared imminent. The spokesman said the cease-fire had been decided following an African Union call for an immediate cessation of hostilities.\par \par \uc2 An assault on Gadhafi and his loyalists could send oil prices on an erratic ride, injecting volatility into stock, bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 An escalation in the conflict would likely send oil prices sharply higher, causing a stampede out of riskier assets, including stocks and higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Australian dollar.\par \par \uc2 But the allied military action could also be seen as finally moving toward stability in the region, which has been rocked by insurrections against despotic leaders, calming oil prices and leading to a positive tone in investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock market investors said the campaign against Libya would likely viewed as a positive step. The coordinated effort should be helpful in "accelerating some stability into the Libyan situation, which ought to be recognized as a good thing and control energy prices at or around current prices," Mark Luschini, chief investment strategist at \uc2 Janney Montgomery Scott\uc2 , said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, stocks may also get a boost Monday after Japan reported progress in its battle to stave off nuclear disaster, restoring power to parts of the Fukushima Daiichi plant and bringing down radiation levels with a marathon water-spraying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "With Japan you're talking about a really significant trading partner," Tom Villalta, lead portfolio manager at Jones/Villalta Opportunity Fund, said. "The quicker Japan can get back to normal, the better it is for us."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly higher Friday, tracking the rebound in other markets in the region, due to bargain hunting in brokerages and property developers, though concerns Beijing might introduce further monetary tightening measures over the weekend pared the market's rise.\par \par \uc2 And indeed the People's Bank of China did announce a 0.50-percentage-point increase in banks' reserve requirement ratio after the market closed. The increase, which takes effect March 25, is the third this year, and comes as inflationary pressures remain in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Wu Xiaoling, a member of the country's legislative body and a former deputy governor of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was reported by local media as saying Saturday that the country still has room to raise the ratio.\par \par \uc2 As long as the interest rate on banks' required reserves isn't lower than banks' funding costs, China can continue to raise banks' reserve requirement ratios, internet portal Sina.com reported Wu as saying.\par \par \uc2 Moves by China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including that Friday, will help contain the rise in China's consumer price index this year below 4%, Xinhua News Agency reported, citing former People's Bank of China adviser Fan Gang.\par \par \uc2 PBOC moves to soak up liquidity in the last year have reduced excessive liquidity and will help contain price rises, the report cited Fan as saying.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares edged higher Friday due to bargain hunting and easing concerns about the nuclear crisis in Japan, with property developers leading the gains.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks closed higher Friday, as the world's biggest industrial powers agreed on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock back an unwelcome rise in the yen, providing some relief to an earthquake-ravaged nation.\par \par \uc2 Japan's markets are closed on Monday for the spring equinox holiday.\par \par \uc2 Crude prices fell Friday in U.S. trading after Libyan forces called a cease-fire, but reports of renewed fighting on the ground and the mobilization of Western forces raised doubts about any end to the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Investor demand for a safe haven lifted gold prices Friday as worries about Japan and the Group of Seven's attempts to weaken the yen outweighed the influence of a ceasefire in Libya.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mostly higher on the London Metal Exchange Friday supported by a stronger euro, although fears of slowing demand from China kept the exchange's flagship copper contract in negative territory.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The allied airstrikes against Libya are presenting a new challenge for the Bank of Japan in its efforts to stem the rise of the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , long viewed as a global safe haven, rose sharply after the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear crisis in Japan began roiling global markets 10 days ago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moves by the BOJ and other major central banks in a rare show of coordinated force pushed down the value of the yen, but the weekend attacks by U.S. and European forces on the loyalist forces of Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi threaten to put further upward pressure on the yen as traders dial back on risk and assess the new geopolitical outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Escalating violence in Libya is likely to cause some risk-aversion in markets, which tends to drive some U.S. dollar and Japanese yen strength," said Camilla Sutton, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto. "The near-term risk is still yen strength."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's nuclear crisis and the possibility of further BoJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will remain in focus as will unrest in Libya and the Middle East more generally, BNZ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Mike Jones said, noting that there is a market holiday in Japan Monday. "Risk appetite is volatile...sentiment is still vulnerable to general news flow out of Japan, the Middle East. Certainty has gone out the window," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The USD/JPY was at 80.79 early in New Zealand trading Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday, buying dollars and selling yen as part of a coordinated effort by Group of Seven nations to weaken \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It was the first coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since 2000 and started overnight in Tokyo when the Bank of Japan initiated a promised "battle" against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  speculators by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The Bank of England, Bundesbank, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banque de France\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Bank of Italy all then \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during European trading, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  also acted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomura Securities estimated Y2 trillion for the BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and another $5 billion across Europe. Those coordinated efforts helped send the dollar shooting up as high as Y82 from Y78.93 late Thursday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The initial \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Fed came at the start of the New York trading day. Traders in New York estimated that orders were placed in mostly $50 million blocks with multiple banks and that the Fed likely traded about $600 million. [ 20-03-11 2215GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showed the world's central banks stood with the BOJ in a coordinated effort to push back against speculators who had ridden down the dollar against the yen to record levels early Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The question market participants were asking themselves Friday following the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was how long the dollar will remain propped up against the yen, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the reasons why \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been typically frowned upon as a form of market manipulation is that they have typically not worked for long.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar spiked to Y81.76 in the first minute of trading in New York, after the Fed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but it then pulled back to trade around Y80.90 the rest of the Friday session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's human nature to test the limits, but I do think they will respect this, as they [definitely] provoked a response [on Thursday]," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York, about speculators.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Alan Ruskin, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, said there will be "lots of probing of central banks' will power" long term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nomura analysts Friday said from the BOJ's perspective an ideal dollar target level is between Y82.70 and Y83.00.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders are also betting that a large swath of Japanese insurers and companies will begin "repatriating" assets held in other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to begin the rebuilding effort at home, possibly driving up the value of the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The result: Now, even in the face of coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central-bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , "there will be plenty of investors still willing to take [the central banks] on," said Geoffrey Yu, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It really depends on whether the Libya situation develops in a way where it is seen as a drag on global growth/global risk assets," said Jens Nordvig, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Nomura Securities in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If that were to be the case, it would pressure on global [bond] yields and hence indirectly support the yen, which could make the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  task more challenging," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro also climbed Friday to its highest level this year against the dollar, as traders added to bets on expected interest rate hikes in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  began ticking higher after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the bank was "extremely attentive" on anchoring inflation expectations, a signal that rate hikes could be coming shortly. The euro subsequently rose as high as $1.4185.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.4180 from $1.4020 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.62 versus Y78.93 late Thursday. The euro was at Y114.34 from Y110.58. The U.K. pound was at $1.6223 from $1.6137. The dollar was at CHF0.9010 from CHF0.8987.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 75.556 from about 75.968.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys prices fell Friday amid Libya's declaration of a ceasefire and joint actions by major central banks to halt the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two events sparked a rally in global stocks and eased some worries that the aftermath of Japan's earthquake and tsunami could undermine the pace of the global economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark 10-year notes pared losses in the afternoon session. Some investors cast question on the cease-fire in Libya, as assaults by government troops against rebels continued. President Barack Obama Friday afternoon demanded that Libyan ruler Moammar Gadhafi stop attacking rebels and the cease-fire to begin immediately.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concern also remained about the threat of a nuclear crisis in Japan as radiation concerns from a damaged nuclear plant continued. Yemen declared a state of emergency Friday as the death toll from the clashes between government forces and protesters continued to rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Friday afternoon trade in New York, the benchmark 10-year note was down 6/32, pushing the yield up to 3.279%, as prices and yields move inversely. Still, Treasurys enjoyed a strong price rally for the week, with the yield falling from 3.393% a week earlier. It was at 3.77% in early February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-dated notes--those with two-year and three-year maturities--rebounded from early losses, partly on speculation that such notes could benefit in coming sessions if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recycles the dollar proceeds from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There was anticipation that some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  money will flow into the Treasury market," said Tom Tucci, head of U.S. government bond trading in New York at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Tucci said Treasurys with maturities of as much as 10 years could benefit from such flows but typically the two-year and three-year notes have been a big beneficiary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two-year note was flat, to yield 0.593%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark 10-year cash Japanese government bonds ended slightly weaker Friday, as Japan and its Group of Seven counterparts' joint action to weaken the yen boosted stock prices, decreasing the attraction of safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid huge costs expected to reconstruct social infrastructures, bond market participants will be focused on the source of funding," said Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market's reaction would differ, depending on whether the costs will be covered by increasing debt issuance or by drastically revising the ruling party's manifesto," Miura said, adding that the discussion is likely to take some time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese markets are closed Monday for a holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Friday amid ongoing risk-aversion demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Monday, Taiwan export orders likely grew 13.43% in February from a year earlier, according to the median forecast of 11 economists in a Dow Jones survey. Orders rose 13.47% in January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US, Coalition Forces Attack Libya \par Libyan Army Announces New Ceasefire \par Further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Depends On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Moves - Source \par WSJ: US Energy Chief: 'Progress' Made At Japan Reactors \par Nissan To Resume Auto Production In Japan This Week \par Japan Tepco: Pressure Levels In No. 3 Reactor Stabilizing \par Japan Finds Abnormal Levels Of Radioactive Material In Milk, Spinach \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 AT&T\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 T-Mobile USA\f28 \uc2 \f2  For $39 Bln Cash And Stock \par Japan PM Kan Cancels Visit To Quake-Hit Area Due To Bad Weather - Kyodo \par China Commerce Minister: May Have Trade Deficit In 1Q -Radio \par China NDRC Researcher:Difficult To Keep 2011 CPI Rise Under 4% \par Aramco To Supply 200,000 B/D Of Crude To \f28 \uc2 \f2 PetroChina\f28 \uc2 \f2  Refinery \par Most Gulf Stock Markets Rally On Saudi Spending Plan Cheer \par WSJ: Egyptians Approve Constitutional Amendments \par French Voters Slam Sarkozy: UMP Collapses; FN, Socialists Rise \par China Vice Premier: Imported Inflation, Structural Price Changes Intertwined \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Asahi, Others Delay Product Debuts In Quake's Aftermath - Nikkei \par Tepco To Raise Generating Capacity 20% By April-End -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Foxconn International\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Sell A China Unit To Hon Hai For CNY463.3Mln \par German Fin Min: Future Euro Rescue Mechanism May Have EUR80B Capital Base-Report \par Yemen President Sacks Government - State News Agency \par China PBOC Hikes Reserve Requirement Ratio For Banks \par Fed Completes Stress Tests Of 19 Largest US Banks \par Fed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; Buys Dollars And Sells Yen \par SEC Charges IBM With Bribing \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Liberty Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  Closes In On German Cable TV Buy \par Ex-Goldman Programmer Gets 8 Years In Code Theft \par GM Halts Work At 2 European Factories \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0001  UK           Mar Rightmove House Price Index \par 0001  UK           1Q  Bank of England Quarterly Bulletin \par 0030  Australia    Feb International