\par\par \b \uc2 G-7 Central Banks Set For Further Show Of Force To Dampen Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 959 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Further coordinated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible Monday\par \par \uc2 --Middle East tensions complicate outlook for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF on yen\par \par \uc2 --Traders focusing on sub-Y80 levels for potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By James Glynn\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--A further move by the world's major central banks to weaken the yen could come as soon as Monday, though the goal will be to ensure market stability rather than push the yen sharply lower, traders and economists said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Despite a Japan market holiday Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders will be on full alert from trade in New Zealand hours for potential yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday gained back most of the ground it lost earlier in the day against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 That's especially the case as weekend events in the Middle East--U.S. and coalition forces launched military strikes against Libya in a bid to knock out loyalist support for leader Col. Moammar Gadhafi--could push up assets traditionally viewed as safe havens, including the yen.\par \par \uc2 While the G-7's endorsement Friday of the need for more orderly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets gives Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  a mandate to act on its own, other central banks can be expected to be on hand to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again during their time zones if needed, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 That's despite Friday's G-7 statement only referring to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for one day.\par \par \uc2 The effect of that coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the first in more than a decade--was already waning by late in the New York day. The dollar had come down to Y80.59, off an earlier high of Y81.98, and not far from the levels where the MOF began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 Early \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday is something "we have to be alert to," said Craig James, chief economist at Commonwealth Securities in Sydney. "Certainly the global powers look bent on supporting Japan. They have to signal the strength of that commitment."\par \par \uc2 Japan is battling to avert a nuclear crisis in the country in the wake of March 11's massive earthquake and tsunami, which devastated large parts of the northeast and caused a key nuclear power facility to become unstable. Despite the disaster, the yen has risen sharply since the quake hit, partly on the expectation of repatriation flows to Japan for insurance and reconstruction costs, and as speculators try and get ahead of the flows by buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Early in Asian hours on Thursday, the dollar dropped to a record low of Y76.25.\par \par \uc2 Still, G-7 nations won't be trying to reverse the trend with the yen. They are likely to want to bring it back to levels where it was before the quake struck, which means getting the dollar over Y83.\par \par \uc2 "If the G-7 tries to keep a cap on the yen they are fighting a difficult battle...they are going to have to a bit more of a concerted approach," James said.\par \par \uc2 Much will depend on whether the yen gets support Monday from the escalation of tensions in the Middle East. Events there raise the spectre of regional conflict and a further surge in the price of oil, another headwind for the Japan, a large importer of energy products.\par \par \uc2 Tony Morriss, a strategist at the \uc2 Australia and New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 , said the start of the week will see a gravitation toward safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Swiss franc to benefit. The U.S. dollar may also gain broadly as markets assess the heightened level of risk.\par \par \uc2 But a break back above Y82.00 for dollar/yen will be hard to achieve, Morriss said, given the yen's status as well as a safe haven. Markets will be looking at Y80.00 as a natural re-entry point for the G-7 central banks to buy.\par \par \uc2 The nuclear crisis remains a wildcard, he added. "The news out of Japan is not much better, they are not out of the woods yet."\par \par \uc2 The operator of Japan's quake-hit Fukushima nuclear plant said Sunday it was having difficulty restoring power to the cooling system of one reactor, while the country's nuclear safety agency said pressure was rising in another reactor.\par \par \uc2 Despite the events of recent weeks the Australian dollar has remained strong and that looks likely to continue, Morriss added.\par \par \uc2 Yen repatriation flows remain in focus but, to date, there has been little evidence Japanese firms and citizens are liquidating assets en masse.\par \par \uc2 That's important for the Australian dollar. Higher yields in Australia are a big drawcard for money from places like Japan, where interest rates are relatively low.\par \par \uc2 "The markets are more worried than the reality has turned out to be," said Stephen Halmarick, head of investment markets research at Colonial First State. "The Japanese people I've spoken to are saying it is not as big as the market might have thought," he added.\par \par \uc2 Julian Jessop, chief international economist at Capital Economics, said the job of taming the yen will be made easier over time as it falls alongside an expected slowing of the Japanese economy.\par \par \uc2 The adverse economic impact of the quake will be larger than generally anticipated and the BOJ will loosen monetary policy further, Jessop said.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --coordinated or otherwise--is unlikely to be decisive, he added. "To have any chance of a lasting impact the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be more than just symbolic. Hard numbers are not yet available, but Friday's sales appear to have been small."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By James Glynn, Dow Jones Newswires;  61-2-8272-4685;  james.glynn@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 20-03-11 0704GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110320e73k00012\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
