\par\par \b \uc2 DJ 2nd UPDATE: Yen Pushes Higher Despite European  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds Bank of Italy participation in the joint-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in second paragraph, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from fourth paragraph onwards.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Clare Connaghan and Katie Martin \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 --Yen continues to move higher despite European \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Bank of France, Bank of England, Bundesbank, Bank of Italy confirm participation\par \par \uc2 --Bank of Japan ready to buy if dollar falls as low as Y81.25 against the yen\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen providing liquidity and stability to the market, rather than hitting yen\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The yen continued to move higher despite European central banks stepping in to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of coordinated action by the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations to stabilize the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its recent record surge.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of France, the Bank of England, the Bundesbank, the Bank of Italy all confirmed they were participating by selling yen, initially pushing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. But the yen has since rebounded.\par \par \uc2 "It was quite well telegraphed in advance, so there's no element of surprise," said Paul Mackel, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC Holdings PLC\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 The euro was back trading at Y114.750 at 1020 GMT, having traded at a two-week high of Y115.56 at the outset of European trading hours.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar is trading at Y81.450, compared with a high of Y82 in early European trading.\par \par \uc2 The yen has been creeping higher since the European authorities launched their phase of the international \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken and stabilize the yen, but the Bank of Japan stands ready to sell yen again if the dollar drops as low as Y81.25, a Tokyo trader said Friday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has placed an order to buy the dollar against the yen at that level, the trader said. The size of this order is unknown.\par \par \uc2 So far the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central banks has not been in size, traders say, but it is however providing liquidity to the market.\par \par \uc2 "[The G7] are trying to be transparent, and they don't want to introduce further volatility into the market by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Mackel. "They are trying to stabilize the market, not weaken the yen," he added.\par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven leading industrial nations pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped massive quantities of yen Friday, aiming to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by earthquake, tsunami and a nuclear crisis.\par \par \uc2 "At a time when Japan is in a difficult situation, it is extremely significant that G-7 authorities agreed to act in concert and cooperate for the stability of the markets," Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said, as he announced the agreement, the first since 2000, after a 90-minute teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers.\par \par \uc2 The ministry sold yen through the Bank of Japan for the first time since September, sending the dollar surging as high as Y81.93 from a low of Y79.19 around the time of the announcement.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Senior Vice Finance Minister Fumihiko Igarashi said he wanted to see the dollar return to pre-quake levels, adding that he expected G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue next week if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes after the yen hit an all-time high early Thursday in Asian trading, with the dollar falling to Y76.25. The move confounded observers who had expected the yen to weaken at a time of severe crisis for the Japanese economy, especially as the buying did not seem to be driven by major repatriation flows from Japanese firms.\par \par \uc2 That raised the prospect that speculators, aware that the yen had gained on repatriation flows after the country's earthquake in 1995, were trying to profit on the catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 A strong yen is detrimental to Japanese exporters, who are the mainstay of Japan's economy and whose strength will be crucial to the country's recovery. Yen strength also exacerbates Japan's chronic deflation.\par \par \uc2 The efforts to stem the yen's rise come as Japanese authorities claimed modest gains Thursday in their efforts to tame the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power complex by wetting it with some 100 tons of seawater, using helicopters and fire trucks; an urgent effort to combat rising temperatures in two storage pools for spent nuclear fuel. Radiation levels at the site fell slightly and a \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2 . executive said \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were "a good first step" in bringing the reactors under control.\par \par \uc2 -By Clare Connaghan and Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires, +44 (0) 20 7842 9496;\par \par \uc2 clare.connaghan@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Jessica Mead in London and Arran Scott, Takashi Nakamichi and Tatsuo Ito in Tokyo contributed to this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2011 06:40 ET (10:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110318e73i002d0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
