\par\par \b \uc2 4th UPDATE: G-7 Pledges Rare Joint  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  As Crisis-Stricken  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Sells Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 1,115 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 04:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --G-7 pledges joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen\par \par \uc2 --Step marks first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2000\par \par \uc2 --BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , popping dollar above Y81 from Y79.19\par \par \uc2 --Other G-7 central banks will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during their trading days\par \par \uc2 --BOJ appears to sell less than Y1 trillion in first two hours\par \par \uc2 --Japan says ECB may sell yen and buy euros\par \par \uc2 (Adds context, more Noda comments.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Arran Scott and Tatsuo Ito \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones) The Group of Seven industrial powers pledged to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped yen on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Friday to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At a time when Japan is in a difficult situation, it is extremely significant that G-7 authorities agreed to act in concert and cooperate for the stability of the markets," Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said in announcing the agreement, the first since 2000, after a 90-minute teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry sold yen through the Bank of Japan for the first time since September, sending the dollar surging as high as Y81.49 from Y79.19 before the announcement. The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was at Y81.582 at 0300 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda said Japanese authorities are not targeting a specific level for the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes after the yen hit an all-time high early Thursday morning in Asian time, with the dollar falling to Y76.25. The move confounded observers who expected the yen to weaken at a time of severe crisis for the Japanese economy, especially as the buying did not seem to be driven by major repatriation flows from Japanese firms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That raised the prospect that speculators, aware that the yen had gained on repatriation flows following a 1995 earthquake, were trying to profit on the tragedy. But a strong yen is anathema to Japanese exporters, who are a mainstay of Japan's economy and whose strength will be crucial to the country's recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the first two hours after Friday's move the BOJ appeared to sell something less than Y1 trillion, entering the market continually to fight overseas yen-buying, dealers with direct knowledge of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flows said. They predicted that as the day wears on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will top September's record sales of Y2 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese authorities traditionally have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only in the dollar/yen pair. Noda indicated that the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might sell yen and buy euros.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wearing the drab civil-defense jumpsuit that Cabinet members are sporting during Japan's worst postwar crisis, Noda told reporters in a packed elevator hall of the Finance Ministry building that the BOJ's 0000 GMT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be followed by yen-selling from the rest of the G-7 in their own time zones Friday, in response to Japan's request.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "No nation hesitated and all readily accepted Japan's request," Noda told a press conference after a regular Cabinet meeting.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda said G-7 members shared that view that there had been excessive and disorderly movements in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I explained clearly to Japan's partners that it is not the case that Japanese firms need yen cash and need to sell foreign assets," Noda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A French official confirmed that G-7 representatives on the call readily supported Japan's request.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We express our solidarity with the Japanese people in these difficult times, our readiness to provide any needed cooperation and our confidence in the resilience of the Japanese economy and financial sector," the G-7 said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The announcement means "round-the-clock \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and support," said Richard Grace, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "We'll probably see the dollar/yen two or three big figures higher over the next 24 hours."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is an extremely important event here," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets strategists Sean Callow and Robert Rennie. "We have not seen coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or at least a statement of intent towards coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from central banks for many many years. History tells that coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a much greater chance of success."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely work -- but then again it has to, as it's the only ammunition Japan has left, \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Robert Ryan said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's monetary easing, it's exchange-rate easing that's the only way they can stimulate the economy. This is the last tool in the tool box. They have to make it work."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to pump cash into Japan's financial system as needed to stabilize domestic financial markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Bank of Japan strongly expects that Japan's concerted action with G-7 member countries in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market will contribute to the stable formation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates," Shirakawa said in a statement. "The Bank of Japan will pursue powerful monetary easing and to ensure stability in financial markets, will continue to provide ample liquidity."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for short-term funds rose in the wake of the devastating quake, but in the new conference with Noda after the G-7 teleconference, Shirakawa said money markets have regained stability following the BOJ's massive fund injections this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economy Minister Kaoru Yosano said the G-7 decision to conduct joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  means there's a common understanding of the importance of a stable yen, the Nikkei reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even though past cases of joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have marked turning points for the target \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it's not yet clear whether this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be enough to keep the yen from strengthening later, as the duration of the joint efforts isn't clear, said Osamu Takashima, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for Citibank Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March is the final month of Japan's fiscal year, which "was one reason why the government and the G-7 decided to step in," Takashima said, "but next month we can't expect they will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , so there is some possibility that the dollar could drop to around Y75 if they stop."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, if that were to happen, dragging the Nikkei Stock Average down below 8,000, then the G-7 could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again, Takashima said. The Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended the morning up 1.77% at 9,120.93.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Arran Scott and Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4042; arran.scott@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Michael S. Arnold in Singapore and Takashi Mochizuki in Tokyo contributed to this story.) [ 18-03-11 0302GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020110318e73i0000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
