\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Noda: Dollar Fall To Around Y76 Taken Into Account On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 03:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments by Noda from 4th paragraph)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tatsuo Ito and Arran Scott \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Friday that the dollar's plunge to its all-time low around the Y76 level was taken into account in Japan's decision to seek cooperation from its Group of Seven counterparts in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "Naturally, I had that in mind," Noda said, when asked if the dollar's drop to Y76 was a consideration in his request for G-7 assistance in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The G-7 industrial powers earlier Friday, at the request of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in a decade. Japan bought dollars to prevent the yen's surge to a record high from further damaging an economy ravaged by the earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster.\par \par \uc2 "No nation hesitated and all readily accepted Japan's request," Noda told a press conference after a regular Cabinet meeting.\par \par \uc2 Explaining the G-7's decision, Noda said the G-7 shared that view that there were excessive and disorderly movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and that resulted in the decision to take coordinated action.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the euro exchange rate, depending on the situation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, Noda said Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the dollar-yen exchange rate, and said G-7 countries would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during their own trading hours.\par \par \uc2 Noda said that the yen's surge was driven by speculation, not by Japanese insurance firms selling foreign assets to raise funds for quake-related expenses, as many in the market have suspected.\par \par \uc2 "I explained clearly to Japan's partners that it is not the case that Japanese firms need yen cash and need to sell foreign assets," Noda said.\par \par \uc2 -By Tatsuo Ito and Arran Scott, Dow Jones Newswires; arran.scott@dowjones.com [ 03-17-11 2251ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110318e73i0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
