\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Up On Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 22:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA               11858.52    +83.93     +0.71% \par Nasdaq              2643.67     +7.62     +0.29% \par S&P 500             1279.21     +5.49     +0.43% \par FTSE 100            5718.13    +22.02     +0.39% \par Xetra DAX           6664.40     +7.52     +0.11% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 2/32      flat   0.593      0 \par 5Y Tsy   100 27/32    -5/32   1.945%    +3.1 \par 10Y Tsy  102 29/32    -6/32   3.279%    +2.2 \par  \par USD/JPY           80.65-66     +2.14% \par Range             81.98-78.99 \par EUR/USD           1.4177-83    +1.08% \par Range             1.4184-1.3978 \par AUD/USD           0.9961-65    +1.63% \par Range             0.9978-0.9779 \par  \par Comex Gold          $1416.10  +$11.90 \par Comex Gold Range    $1402.20-$1424.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)    $101.07    -$0.35 \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks climbed higher Friday, as fears of increased violence in Libya ebbed and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to relieve investor worries over \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average finished Friday with a gain of 83.93 points, or 0.71%, to close at 11858.52, ending a week of tumultuous trading that on Wednesday briefly sent major U.S. indexes into negative territory for the year. It was the Dow's second straight losing week and third down week in the last four.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite, still below even for the year, added 7.62, or 0.29%, to 2643.67 Friday, but losses amid the recent nuclear worries made it the Nasdaq's worst week since mid-August. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index rose 5.49, or 0.43%, to 1279.21, as it closed out its worst week since November.\par \par \uc2 Stocks pulled back from their highs of the day on Friday afternoon as traders prepared their portfolios to ride out a potentially turbulent weekend.\par \par \uc2 "There's some caution heading into the weekend," said Jim Herrick, director of equity trading at R.W. Baird. "We had some good news coming out of the financial sector with banks boosting dividends and we saw a rally there, but there's still some geopolitical issues."\par \par \uc2 After tumbling for the week's first three days, stocks rose Thursday and added further gains Friday. The latest came after Libya's foreign minister said Col. Moammar Gadhafi's regime had declared an immediate cease-fire in the wake of the United Nations resolution passed late Thursday authorizing military action against Gadhafi's security forces.\par \par \uc2 President Barack Obama on Friday said the U.S. won't deploy ground troops to Libya but vowed that America wouldn't "stand idly by" as attacks against rebels continued.\par \par \uc2 While those political developments helped keep crude-oil prices around $101 a barrel, investors braced for further developments in North Africa.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Friday, buying dollars and selling yen as part of a coordinated effort by Group of Seven nations to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It was the first coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2000 and started overnight in Tokyo when the Bank of Japan initiated a promised "battle" against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of England, Bundesbank, \uc2 Banque de France\uc2  and Bank of Italy all then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during European trading, and the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  also acted.\par \par \uc2 Nomura Securities estimated Y2 trillion for the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and another $5 billion across Europe. Those coordinated efforts helped send the dollar shooting up as high as Y82 from Y78.93 late Thursday in New York.\par \par \uc2 The initial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Fed came right at 8 a.m. EDT, the start of the New York trading day.\par \par \uc2 Traders in New York estimated that orders were placed in mostly $50 million blocks with multiple banks and that the Fed likely traded about $600 million.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the world's central banks stood with the BOJ in a coordinated effort to push back against speculators who had ridden down the dollar against the yen to record levels early Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The strength of the yen has been a long-time problem for Japanese officials even before this week, as it makes it difficult for its high-tech and auto components export-driven economy to compete in the global market. Japan's strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has contributed to deflationary conditions which have proven hard to shake for the world's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 The question market participants were asking themselves Friday following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was how long the dollar will remain propped up against the yen, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons why \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been typically frowned upon as a form of market manipulation is that they have typically not worked for long.\par \par \uc2 The dollar spiked to Y81.76 in the first minute of trading in New York, after the Fed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but it then pulled back to trade around Y80.90 the rest of the Friday session.\par \par \uc2 "It's human nature to test the limits, but I do think they will respect this, as they [definitely] provoked a response [on Thursday]," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York, about speculators.\par \par \uc2 But Alan Ruskin, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York, said there will be "lots of probing of central banks' will power" long-term.\par \par \uc2 Nomura analysts Friday said from the BOJ's perspective an ideal dollar target level is between Y82.70 and Y83.00.\par \par \uc2 As a prelude to Friday, the dollar plunged to a record low of Y76.25 early in Thursday's global session, shattering the previous low of Y79.75.\par \par \uc2 The yen had been strengthening as traders bet Japanese insurers and corporations would need to repatriate yen to help pay for rebuilding the country after last week's major earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the euro also climbed Friday to its highest level this year against the dollar, as traders added to bets on expected interest rate hikes in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  began ticking higher after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the bank was "extremely attentive" on anchoring inflation expectations, a signal that rate hikes could be coming shortly. The euro subsequently rose as high as $1.4185.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the euro was at $1.4180 from $1.4020 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y80.62 versus Y78.93 late Thursday. The euro was at Y114.34 from Y110.58. The U.K. pound was at $1.6223 from $1.6137. The dollar was at CHF0.9010 from CHF0.8987.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 Treasurys prices fell on Friday amid Libya's declaration of a ceasefire and joint actions by major central banks to halt the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 The two events sparked a rally in global stocks and eased some worries that the aftermath of Japan's earthquake and tsunami could undermine the pace of the global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark 10-year notes pared losses in the afternoon session. Some investors cast question on the cease-fire in Libya, as assaults by government troops against rebels continued. President Barack Obama on Friday afternoon demanded that Libyan ruler Col. Moammar Gadhafi stop attacking rebels and the cease-fire to begin immediately.\par \par \uc2 Concern also remains about the threat of a nuclear crisis in Japan as radiation concerns from a damaged nuclear plant continue. Yemen declared a state of emergency on Friday as the death toll from the clashes between government forces and protesters continued to rise.\par \par \uc2 "The market is taking a breather. The situation in Libya didn't spin out of control," said Michael Chang, an interest-rate strategist in New York at Credit Suisse Securities USA. "But you still have the Japan nuclear plant to worry about. The situation there is far from being solved."\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trade, the benchmark 10-year note was down 6/32, pushing the yield up to 3.279%, as prices and yields move inversely. Still, Treasurys enjoyed a strong price rally for the week, with the yield falling from 3.393% a week ago. It was at 3.77% in early February.\par \par \uc2 Short-dated notes--those with two-year and three-year maturities--rebounded from early losses, partly on speculation that such notes could benefit in coming sessions if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  recycles the dollar proceeds from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There was anticipation that some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  money will flow into the Treasury market," said Tom Tucci, head of U.S. government bond trading in New York at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . Tucci said Treasurys with maturities of as much as 10 years could benefit from such flows but typically the two-year and three-year notes have been a big beneficiary.\par \par \uc2 The two-year note was flat, to yield 0.593%.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Fed Completes Stress Tests Of 19 Largest US Banks \par Libya Renews Attacks After Cease-Fire Offer \par Fed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; Buys Dollars And Sells Yen \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Fukushima 1 Situation Not Deteriorating \par US Stocks Up On Dividend News, Calmer Geopolitics \par SEC Charges IBM With Bribing \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Liberty Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  Closes In On German Cable TV Buy \par Oil Dips As Libya Eyes Truce, But Fighting Persists \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fedex\f28 \uc2 \f2  Faces Probe Alongside UPS Over Consultants \par Yankee Bond Issuance Hits Record High \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN ASIA-PACIFIC EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0030  Australia     February International Merchandise Imports \par 0200  New Zealand   February Credit card statistics \par 0300  S Korea       February Corporate Bankruptcies \par 0330  Thailand      February Trade data \par 0530  Japan         February Tokyo area department store sales \par 0530  Japan         February Nationwide department store sales \par 0800  Taiwan        February Export Orders \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN G-7 EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 0001  UK            March Rightmove House Price Index \par 0001  UK            1Q Bank of England Quarterly Bulletin \par 1230  US            February Chicago Fed National Activity Index \par 1400  US            February Existing Home Sales \par 1400  EU            ECB President Trichet at quarterly session with MEPs \par N/A   EU            Eurozone 