\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Europeans Dive Into G-7 Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,164 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --European central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tame yen\par \par \uc2 --Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  defies efforts and pushes higher as nuclear fears continue\par \par \uc2 --Euro powers ahead against sterling and dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Eva Szalay \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--It was an extraordinary trading session in Europe, as several European central banks were seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a bid to tame the sky-high yen, but after an initial skid lower in yen crosses, the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has regained momentum and clawed back some of its losses.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Bundesbank, the Banque of France, the Bank of England and later the Bank of Italy came after the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight, spending around Y1 trillion to Y2 trillion buying dollars against the yen. The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also confirmed that it had participated in selling the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central banks' efforts managed to push the euro and the dollar higher against the yen at the London open, pushing the euro to Y115.56, a two week-high, and the dollar to Y82. But the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  later pushed back up to stronger levels than it held before the European central banks' action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe walked in to find the dollar close to Y82.00 against the yen," said Citibank in a note to clients, adding that this spurred speculation whether euro-area banks would step in to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Banque de France\f28 \uc2 \f2  appeared to be first out of the gates buying the euro against the yen. [This] was followed by the ECB and Bundesbank and later the Banca d'Italia; the BOE also waded in to buy sterling against the yen."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But as the yen bounced back against these \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  immediately after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  noted that "the lack of follow through has drawn a lot of 'sniggering' about the effectiveness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen was pushing higher as investors continued to pile into the relative safety of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the Japanese Nuclear Agency said it had raised the accident scale level of the quake-damaged Fukushima Daiichi nuclear plant to level 5, similar to the severity of the accident at Three Mile Island in 1979.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The agency said the increased safety warning was due to "serious damage" to the plant's reactors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1123 GMT, the euro was trading at Y114.97 against the yen, while the dollar was holding overnight gains at Y81.40.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes after the Group of Seven industrialized countries agreed to co-ordinated action to stabilize the yen after investors pushed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to an all- time high against the dollar Thursday, with the greenback trading at Y76.25 against the Japanese unit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The question now is whether central banks are trying to defend the Y80 level against the dollar, or whether they will try pushing the [dollar] higher," said Geoff Kendrick, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Nomura in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kendrick added that market participants will be eyeing the Federal Reserve and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York hours, to see whether the North American central banks will follow through with their promise of co-ordinated action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As expected, the U.S. Federal Reserve also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market at the start of New York trading, buying dollars and selling yen, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confirmed around 1203 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While all market participants were glued to the screens, waiting for news about the yen, the people's Bank of China announced that it would raise it reserve requirement ratio for its banks by 50 basis points, effective March 25.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro printed a fresh four-month high against the dollar to trade at $1.4136 after the pair breached the technically important $1.4105 level. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  technical analyst Karen Jones said the pair was now heading towards its November high at $1.4283 and the 2008-2011 resistance line at 1.4305.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the pound hit a four-month low against the euro, with the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at GBP0.8749, after a key Bank of England rate setter played down the prospect of an imminent interest rate increase in the U.K. and a sharp drop was reported in a key indicator of the country's consumer confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In pointed comments, Bank of England policy maker Charles Bean said that inflation may prove more persistent next year than the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is forecasting but that doesn't necessarily strengthen the case for an early rate increase, a U.K. daily reported Friday. Meanwhile, Nationwide's consumer confidence index slumped to 38 in February from 48 in January, an all time low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said market participants are starting to scale back expectations of an April rate hike from the BOE, which is weighing on the pound.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Consumer confidence is weakening ahead of the government's austerity measures in April and the BOE won't want to hit consumers with a double whammy," she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She expects the pound to fall against the euro but added that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's prospects against the dollar are less clear given some of the uncertainties surrounding the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1128 GMT, the dollar was at Y81.45 from about Y78.93 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG, while the euro was at Y115.58 from Y110.58. The euro was at $1.4127 from $1.4020 late Thursday, after printing a fresh four-month high at $1.4136. The U.K. pound was at $1.6150 from $1.6137, while the euro hit a four-month high against the pound earlier at GBP0.8749.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 75.875 from about 75.968 Thursday.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/USD    USD/JPY    GBP/USD    USD/CHF \par  \par Spot 1133 GMT     1.4127     81.42      1.6149     0.9017 \par 3 Day Trend       Bullish    Bullish    Range      Bearish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish    Bearish    Bearish    Bearish \par 200 day ma        1.3477     84.32      1.5791     0.9856 \par 3rd Resistance    1.4283     83.98      1.6277     0.9205 \par 2nd Resistance    1.4185     83.30      1.6242     0.9155 \par 1st Resistance    1.4165     82.00      1.6199     0.9090 \par Pivot*            1.3981     78.30      1.6098     0.8973 \par 1st Support       1.4080     81.21      1.6061     0.8958 \par 2nd Support       1.4035     80.00      1.6027     0.8911 \par 3rd Support       1.3980     79.15      1.5978     0.8852 \par  \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spot:       EUR/GBP \par  \par Spot 1133 GMT     0.8742 \par 3 Day Trend       Bullish \par Weekly Trend      Bullish \par 200 day ma        0.8532 \par 3rd Resistance    0.8841 \par 2nd Resistance    0.8815 \par 1st Resistance    0.8759 \par Pivot*            0.8694 \par 1st Support       0.8700 \par 2nd Support       0.8650 \par 3rd Support       0.8630 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Eva Szalay, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9305; (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Francis Bray, Dow Jones technical analyst contributed to this article.) [ 18-03-11 1214GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1035 1074 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000yn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
