\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: U.S. stocks are expected to open higher Friday, comforted by the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations agreement to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the yen's rise and support the Japanese economy. Still, traders noted the situation in Libya remains a concern after the U.N. approved a no-fly zone over the country.\par \par \uc2 "Today's much anticipated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the G-7 has seen a welcome weakening of the yen, and by effect, a boost to global equity markets," said Cameron Peacock, analyst at IG Markets.\par \par \uc2 However, some traders were circumspect about the more tempered market sentiment, noting the G-7 action had been anticipated and Japanese officials still have a massive recovery and rebuilding task on hand.\par \par \uc2 "The problems at the nuclear plant are far from over, and we have a three-day weekend ahead, so it's hard to keep buying on the weaker yen alone," said Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 At 1130 GMT, S&P 500 futures were up 8.9 points at 1277.70, Nasdaq 100 futures were up 14.5 points at 2240.50 and Dow industrial futures were up 108.5 points at 11791.50.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European stocks inched up again after dipping lower on China's move to raise its reserve requirement ratio by 50 basis points. Healthy U.S. stock futures offered support and volatility started to decrease as index options expired, traders said. Elsewhere, the euro-zone trade deficit deteriorated to EUR14.8 billion in January from EUR0.5 billion, which was worse than anticipated, said Newedge. However, markets shrugged off the data, comforted by signs that the Japanese authorities were getting a grip on the nuclear accident Still, a degree of caution remained after the U.N. vote concerning Libya. At 1150 GMT, the Euro Stoxx 50 index was trading 0.6% higher at 2804.06.\par \par \uc2 London's FTSE 100 was up, nudging up again as the G-7's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision continued to underpin sentiment. However, traders note the temptation to square positions ahead of the weekend. "There's without doubt going to be a desire for traders to square-off risk -- the nuclear crisis has already been upgraded to put it on a par with Three Mile Island and, with the IAEA labeling it a race against time, there's going to be little desire to get caught on the wrong side of any panic reaction once the markets close," said IG. In terms of sectors, oil and gas stocks gained in tandem with oil prices, with BP up 1.2% and \uc2 Tullow Oil\uc2  up 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 At 1135 GMT, June bunds were up 0.06 at 122.89, while June gilts were up 0.26 at 118.38.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen was whipsawing against the dollar and the euro after the G-7 agreement. The Bank of France and Bank Of England were the first European central banks to participate by selling yen, initially pushing it lower; it subsequently rebounded. Sterling meanwhile hit a four-month low against the euro after a record low Nationwide consumer confidence survey and dovish comments from the Monetary Policy Committee's Charlie Bean.\par \par \uc2 At 1135 GMT, the dollar was up at Y81.43, the euro was up at $1.4125 and the pound was down at $1.6150.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:\par \par \uc2 YEN PUSHES HIGHER DESPITE EUROPEAN \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The yen continues to move higher despite European central banks stepping in to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of coordinated action by the G-7 to stabilize the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its recent record surge.\par \par \uc2 EURO-ZONE POSTS RECORD TRADE DEFICIT IN JANUARY: The euro zone posts its largest external trade deficit on record in January as imports rise on the previous month but exports fall sharply, official data shows. Non-seasonally adjusted figures show the trade deficit widens to EUR14.8 billion.\par \par \uc2 PAI PARTNERS CONFIRM YOPLAIT STAKE SALE TO \uc2 GENERAL MILLS\uc2 : French private equity fund \uc2 PAI Partners\uc2  says it is in exclusive talks with French dairy cooperative \uc2 Sodiaal\uc2  and \uc2 General Mills\uc2  to sell a 51% stake in dairy products maker Yoplait for a little over EUR800 million.\par \par \uc2 ALLIANZ SEES "11 OPERATING PROFIT AT EUR8B GIVE-OR-TAKE EUR500M: Allianz, Europe's largest primary insurer by premium income, aims to make 2011 operating profit of around EUR8 billion, but said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and interest rate changes could shift that figure up or down by EUR500 million.\par \par \uc2 RIO TINTO: MORE COKING COAL PROJECTS NEEDED TO MEET DEMAND: Anglo-Australian miner \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  says more investment needs to be made in new coking coal projects to satisfy robust demand for the steelmaking raw ingredient.\par \par \uc2 ===========================\par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ET/GMT   COUNTRY/PERIOD \par 0830/1230  UK   Mar     CML Market Commentary \par 1300/1700  CAN          Bank of Canada Financial Statistics \par N/A        US           No major economic indicators scheduled. \par N/A        JPN  Mar     Monthly Economic Report \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CHINA HIKES BANKS RESERVE RATIO FOR THIRD TIME THIS YEAR: China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it will raise the share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by 0.50 percentage points, the third hike this year, as inflationary pressures remain in the spotlight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EURO-ZONE CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT NARROWS TO EUR0.7B IN JAN: The euro zone's current account deficit shrinks sharply to EUR0.7 billion in January, from a revised deficit of EUR12.5 billion in December, as the balance in trade of services moves into surplus, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 AIR FRANCE FACES MANSLAUGHTER PROBE OVER RIO-PARIS CRASH: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air France-KLM\f28 \uc2 \f2  joins Airbus in being probed for alleged manslaughter in connection with the 2009 crash of a Rio de Janeiro to Paris flight with the loss of 228 lives, the carrier's lawyers say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GERMAN FEBRUARY PPI UP ON MONTH AND YEAR: Producer prices in Germany, Europe's largest economy, rise at a faster pace on the year in February largely as a result of higher energy prices, although the monthly increase in prices slows, the Federal Statistics Office says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POUND UNDER PRESSURE AS BOE RATE RISE EXPECTATIONS SCALED BACK: The pound comes under selling pressure after a key BOE rate setter plays down the prospect of an imminent interest rate increase in the U.K. and a sharp drop is reported in a key indicator of the country's consumer confidence.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALKS ONGOING BETWEEN NASDAQ AND ICE: Lingering differences between Nasdaq OMX and potential partner IntercontinentalExchange are among the issues delaying a move toward a counterbid for \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYSE Euronext\f28 \uc2 \f2 , The Wall Street Journal reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Simon Varcoe, Dow Jones Newswires; 4420-7842-9449; simon.varcoe@dowjones.com [ 