\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 674 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par YEN PUSHES HIGHER DESPITE EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen continues to move higher despite European central banks stepping in to sell the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as part of coordinated action by the G7 to stabilize the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after its recent record surge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA HIKES BANKS RESERVE RATIO FOR THIRD TIME THIS YEAR \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says it will raise the share of deposits banks must hold in reserve by 0.50 percentage points, the third hike this year, as inflationary pressures remain in the spotlight.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN BOOSTING FUKUSHIMA WATER COOLING EFFORTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials plan to redouble efforts to cool overheating spent fuel to prevent radiation leaks at the severely damaged Fukushima nuclear power plant, as slight declines in radiation levels offer some encouragement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UN SECURITY COUNCIL ENDORSES LIBYA NO-FLY ZONE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The United Nations Security Council authorizes military force against Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi's security forces, opening the way for European and American airstrikes on the North African country within the coming days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HUTCHISON PORT DEBUTS DOWN 6% IN SINGAPORE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hutchison Port Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  makes a lackluster debut on the Singapore Exchange, falling 6% below its initial public offer price in the largest ever listing in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SAMSUNG EXPECTS RECORD 2011 PROFIT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees record operating profit in 2011, even though trading conditions will remain difficult as competition heats up among electronics companies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER AFTER G-7 ACTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are up, with investors inspired by coordinated action by the Group of Seven to halt the Japanese yen's rise after last week's devastating earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOODY'S CUTS TEPCO'S RATING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Japan K.K. cut downgraded its long-term issuer rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  by two notches on the impact of last week's devastating earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TALKS ONGOING BETWEEN NASDAQ AND ICE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lingering differences between Nasdaq OMX and potential partner IntercontinentalExchange are among the issues delaying a move toward a counterbid for \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYSE Euronext\f28 \uc2 \f2 , The Wall Street Journal reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GOLDMAN REPORTEDLY CUTS 5% OF TRADING DESK STAFF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut 5% of its trading desk staff as part of its annual review process, Reuters reports. Other divisions were also affected by layoffs, according to the report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Morning Briefing \par G-7 ACTION TO SPUR STOCKS HIGHER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks are expected to open higher, comforted by the G-7 agreement to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem the yen's rise and support the Japanese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Taking Stock \par JAPANESE STOCKS WERE TOUTED AS CHEAP BEFORE... \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before the earthquake hit, there were plenty of people who thought Japanese stocks were on offer at bargain-basement prices. Even if you didn't fully agree back then, what about now? David Cottle writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par INDIVIDUAL INVESTORS, HEDGE FUNDS SCRAMBLED FOR YEN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A day after a heart-stopping rally drove the Japanese yen to one of its sharpest ascents in history, traders and bankers blame a freak onslaught of forced buying by Japanese individual investors and hedge funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par The Wall Street Journal \par JAPAN'S CRISIS BRAKES GLOBAL DEAL MAKING \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's disaster hits deal making across the globe, forcing some companies to put plans to raise capital on hold and casting doubt on some bumper share offerings in the pipeline, Alison Tudor reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES HIGHER AFTER G-7 ACTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stock futures are higher after G-7 action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TREASURYS NUDGE HIGHER BUT LACK CONVICTION \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys trade in the positive territory in London on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's nuclear crisis.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS ON \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WATCH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JPY is whipsawing against USD and EUR after several European central banks were seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the London open.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BRENT BRIEFLY TURNS NEGATIVE ON CHINA RATIO HIKE \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude futures dip after China raises its reserve requirement ratio, market focus locked on Libya\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPOT GOLD HIGHER AS OIL RISES, MARKETS EYE LIBYA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold is trading almost 1% higher in Europe as the price of oil rises. [ 18-03-11 1200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000y6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
