\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source: Half Time Score: G7 1 - Market 1\b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 12:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on The Source, the Wall Street Journal Online's site for European real-time analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/source"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/source}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Katie Martin \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A few hours into the European phase of the extraordinary G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame the sky-high yen, and it's tough to pick a winner.\par \par \uc2 Sure, the yen dropped like a stone when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, with the dollar leaping from around Y79 to Y81.49.\par \par \uc2 But early Friday, the combined might of the Bank of England, the Bank of France, the Bundesbank, the Bank of Italy, and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  succeeded in pushing up the dollar by a paltry half a yen. The high for the day so far now stands at Y82, and the yen has been creeping higher ever since.\par \par \uc2 Huh? Is the market not listening? The G-7 made it crystal clear last night that they will fight undue yen strength. The current coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the first in over a decade--is a pretty clear statement of intent. So why is the yen pushing higher in European trading hours?\par \par \uc2 Several reasons.\par \par \uc2 First off, this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lacks the element of surprise. It was very well flagged, and deliberately so, last night, and therefore most of the yen-weakening impact is probably already reflected in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, the G-7 is not necessarily trying to engineer extreme weakness in the yen. After all, Japan has got an awful lot of commodities to import for its rebuilding process. A massively weak yen would not help that. But a stable one would. It's stability we're after here. As a result, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  central banks are said to be selling yen in a series of small chunks, not in a massive splurge.\par \par \uc2 Thirdly, grim news continues to ooze out of the Fukushima plant. Investors are nervous. And when they're nervous, they buy, er, yen--one of the market's favorite safety plays.\par \par \uc2 Traders and investors still hold a lot of yen. They are nowhere near to being net sellers yet. They are taking notice of this joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --how could they not? But the main thing keeping the yen steady now is not the European firepower.\par \par \uc2 It's the presence of a large order to buy dollars if the buck sinks to Y81.25, placed by none other than the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 This battle ain't over yet.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 . [ 18-03-11 1151GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000xr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
