\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ 2nd UPDATE: Nations Act to Put Brakes on Yen's Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 1,177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 09:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (updates \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels in 4th-6th paragraphs; adds Japanese finance minister comments in 12th and 15th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Damian Paletta and David Wessel \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The world's economic powerhouses banded together in an attempt to tame an unwelcome rise in the value of the Japanese yen--a development that had threatened to handicap Japan's recovery from the recent devastating earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 For the first time since they joined to rescue a sinking euro in 2000, the U.S., Japan, U.K., Canada and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday night agreed to "concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in exchange markets." The ECB manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shared by G-7 members France, Germany and Italy.\par \par \uc2 The move came "in response to recent movements in the exchange rate of the yen associated with the tragic events in Japan," according to a statement issued after a conference call among financial ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven economies.\par \par \uc2 Japan's finance ministry sold yen through the Bank of Japan, sending the dollar as high as Y81.93 from a low of Y79.19 around the time of the announcement. The dollar was at Y81.76 at 0739 GMT.\par \par \uc2 When the earthquake struck last week, a dollar bought Y82.80. On Thursday, before the G-7 call, it hit a record of Y76.25 to the dollar. Japan led Asian shares higher Friday, with the Nikkei Stock Average rising 2.7% as concern eased over the damage a rising yen inflicts on Japanese exports.\par \par \uc2 The yen was also sharply down against the euro Friday, although no additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the ECB was apparent when European markets opened. As of 0700 GMT the euro was at Y115.02, up from a low of Y111.12 around the time the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  began.\par \par \uc2 The rise in the yen against the dollar has been something of a puzzle to officials. One explanation, rejected by Japanese authorities, is that big Japanese institutions have been bringing money home from abroad, which involves selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buying yen. Another is that Japanese households, some of whom have invested overseas to get higher interest rates, are also bringing money from abroad. A third is that speculators and investors who bet against the yen are now forced to buy it to close their positions.\par \par \uc2 A higher yen, among other things, hurts Japanese exports by making them more expensive to foreigners. Even before the earthquake, some Japanese manufacturers were complaining that the yen had risen too much.\par \par \uc2 Since the earthquake last week, G-7 finance ministers and central bankers have been in frequent contact, officials said. The possibility of a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the volatility in the yen was discussed but no consensus was reached until shortly before Thursday's call, which French officials had initiated.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner spoke with his French counterpart, Christine Lagarde, whose government currently chairs the G-7, as well as the Japanese finance minister, Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke and ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet to seek agreement that the group would deliver whatever the Japanese were asking. Mr. Geithner also alerted President Barack Obama.\par \par \uc2 Officials briefed on the G-7 call, which lasted about 90 minutes, said Mr. Bernanke and Mr. Trichet spoke first and said they would back the Japanese request for help; the British and the Canadians then followed. A senior French official said the possibility of foreign purchases of Japanese government bonds, an idea floated by the French finance minister, did not come up.\par \par \uc2 "No nation hesitated and all readily accepted Japan's request," Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said at a press conference Friday in Tokyo. He said the G-7 shared Japan's view that movements in the yen were excessive and disorderly.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, both to arrest the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and to keep financial markets functioning, has pumped trillions of yen into money markets over the past week. When a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply increases the supply of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its value usually falls. But that hasn't been happening to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the next logical move. "It's the only way they can stimulate the economy," said \uc2 BNP Paribas\b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Ryan, who described it as another form of monetary easing. "This is the last tool in the tool box," he said. "They have to make it work."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Noda said the Japanese authorities aren't targeting a specific level for the yen.\par \par \uc2 But Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Agricole, said "the psychologically important level of 80.00 will likely stick out as a key level to defend."\par \par \uc2 The governor of the Bank of Japan, Masaaki Shirakawa, said Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role will be to facilitate "powerful monetary easing and to ensure stability in financial markets" by providing "ample liquidity."\par \par \uc2 In large part, the move reflected the willingness of other countries to do what they could to make things easier for the beleaguered Japanese.\par \par \uc2 Said Daniel Price, who was an international-economic adviser in the George W. Bush White House: "The action underscores the commitment to cooperation and coordinated action that has been the hallmark of the G-7 in times of great stress or volatility."\par \par \uc2 Since the breakdown in 1971 of the international monetary system known as Bretton Woods (named for the New Hampshire resort where it was fashioned after World War II), the U.S. and its allies have used \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets both to slow or resist rapid and unwelcome moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. At times, they have acted when authorities considered exchange rates to be out of step with fundamental economic conditions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major economies was common in the 1990s, but the practice fell from favor in the late 1990s and now is used sparingly. The U.S., for instance, bought and sold the dollar in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on eight days in 1995, but has done so only twice since August 1995, according to the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Japanese traditionally have been more active in exchange markets. Without support from other countries, the Japanese sold yen to try to reduce its value in September-- a maneuver that was not regarded as a success.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Frankel, a Harvard University scholar of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the maneuvers tend to work under two conditions.\par \par \uc2 One is that several countries, including the U.S., join together. The second is that the exchange rate is seen as being "pretty far out of line based on fundamentals," and there's a shared agreement that it needs to be corrected.\par \par \uc2 "As we have long stated," the G-7 said Thursday night, "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates have adverse implications for economic and financial stability."\par \par \uc2 -By Damian Paletta And David Wessel, The Wall Street Journal; Damian.Paletta@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 --Nathalie Boschat, Arran Scott and Tatsuo Ito contributed to this article. [ 18-03-11 0841GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
