\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Ratings Says BoJ Actions Constructive For Japan Econ\b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--Fitch Ratings said Friday the Bank of Japan's actions to buy in full Y10 trillion in reconstruction bonds will help the country's economic recovery folowing a the devastating earthquake and tsunami that triggered a nuclear crisis in one of the country's atomic power plants.\par \par \uc2 "I view the BoJ's aggressive action to support financial market stability as constructive in assisting Japan's economy to recover from the disaster," said Andrew Colquhoun, sovereign analyst at Fitch in an email to Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese government said earlier it was planning to issue more than Y10 trillion in earthquake reconstruction bonds to be bought in full directly by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Sankei Newspaper reported Friday, citing government sources.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan and the government are aiming to rapidly finalize details and reach agreement with opposition parties on the plan, according to the Sankei.\par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been flooding the short-term money market with liquidity since Monday as last week's massive earthquake, devastating 10-meter tsunami and an ensuing nuclear crisis heightened concerns about the ability of banks to meet a surge in demand for funds from companies and households. The action comes as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and its Group of Seven counterparts pledged to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was driven to an all-time low of Y76.25 early Thursday morning, posing a threat to the export-driven Japanese economy. Amid concerns about the impact of the quake and the yen's appreciation, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made its easy stance clear to the markets.\par \par \uc2 -By Enda Curran, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-2-8272-4687; enda.curran@dowjones.com [ 18-03-11 0710GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i000ga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
