\par\par \b \uc2 UK MORNING BRIEFING: FTSE Set For Boost After G7  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,530 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SNAPHOT: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Shares seen higher, gilts mixed; euro, oil futures, spot gold higher\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  CRISIS HEADLINES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -G-7 Pledges Rare Joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  As \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sells Yen\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  To Redouble Fukushima Water Cooling Efforts; Radiation Levels Down\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Low Levels of Radioactive Material Detected in Pacific\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Injects Additional 3 Trillion Yen Into Money Market\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OTHER KEY HEADLINES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -U.N. Approves Attack on Libya, Oil Surges\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Bank Of England's Deputy Governor Against Immediate Rate Rise\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -UK Consumer Confidence Continues To Evaporate\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Ontario Teachers Fund: Supports TMX/LSE Planned Merger\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Libya No-Fly Zone Vital To Halt 'Greater Bloodshed' - UK\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -FTSE 250 Execs See Pre-Crisis Revenue Growth Returning - PwC\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  Says Backing For Riversdale Offer Hits 33.04% From 30.59%\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Watch for: 0930 GMT Feb CML mortgage lending figures\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SHARES: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London shares are likely to start higher as markets rebound slightly on some optimism that Japan can contain its nuclear crisis, while Libya looms after the U.N. authorized military action. For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE up 30 points at 5726.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Suggestions that the Japanese nuclear crisis is showing some signs of stabilizing, along with notes of support for the country's economy from the G-7 has provided a degree of stability for equity markets running into the weekend break," said Chris Weston, Institutional trader at IG Markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The major indices are all ticking higher as a result of this shift in sentiment, offering global equity markets some much needed relief and although the situation remains volatile, anything that instills confidence in the near term is likely to be well received."\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G-7 industrial powers pledged to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped yen on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Friday to keep the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster. The yen soared on the move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At a time when Japan is in a difficult situation, it is extremely significant that G-7 authorities agreed to act in concert and cooperate for the stability of the markets," Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said in announcing the agreement, the first since 2000, after a 90-minute teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said the united stand added "clout" to the announcement. It marked the first joint G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since 2000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro also surged against both the yen and the greenback on the G-7 announcement on expectations the ECB will do its part to keep the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  low. There has been no indication from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yet that it has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's and dollar's rallies came against a backdrop of escalating tensions in the Middle East with the United Nations earlier sanctioning a no-fly zone over Libya, setting the stage for a potential vote on a military move within a few hours.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Pentagon was already fine-tuning military options for "serious" strikes against ground and air targets should the White House order them, U.S. defense officials said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling lost some ground against the euro and dollar on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action, but basically was sidelined during the aftermath. Narrow movements from here were expected for Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GILTS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed Friday with military action against Libya on the horizon while Japan steps up efforts to contain its nuclear plant crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Britain has entered a hazardous period as new question marks hang over the recovery at a time of persistently above-target inflation, a top Bank of England official said to The Times in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Charlie Bean, the Deputy Governor, told The Times that it was still not clear whether the economy was passing through a soft patch or had entered "a more durable slowing" amid public worries about the Treasury's spending cuts and tax rises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bean signaled his opposition to immediate interest rate increases, saying that the Bank needed to get a "better handle on the prospects for the economy further down the road," The Times reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. consumers became even more pessimistic about the outlook for the economy and their job prospects during February, and less willing to spend, according to a monthly survey commissioned by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nationwide Building Society\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude rose Friday from ongoing tensions in the oil-rich regions amid impact from Japan's nuclear crisis, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in April, gained $1.65 to $103.07 per barrel while Brent North Sea crude for May was up $1.56 to $116.46.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The initial reaction to Japan is a sharp drop in oil demand in the short-term," said Jason Feer, analyst for Singapore-based Argus Media energy market analysts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But unrest in crude-producing Libya is a factor behind the surge in oil prices, analysts said. The UN Security Council meeting voted Thursday to permit "all necessary measures" to impose a no-fly zone, protect civilian areas and impose a ceasefire on Gadhafi's military. Enforcement will rely on air power as the resolution rules out sending ground troops.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is likely that US and some Western countries may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Libya, and as Gadhafi has the upper hand, it is closer to ending the civil war," Feer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LME metals were mostly lower on some mild profit taking after the powerful rally Thursday on a weak dollar and Japan reconstruction hopes. The dollar rallied against the yen in Asian trade on the G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and stabilized against the euro, so it's no longer giving metals much help.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Those who were buying into weakness on Tuesday are sitting on some nice gains, so you would expect a bit of profit taking," said a Singapore-based trader at a European bank. LME 3-month copper was at $9,523/ton, down $41.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,409.10/oz, up $5.10 since the New York close on buying in Tokyo as the JPY weakened, and oil rallied after UN authorization of Western military action against Gadhafi's forces in Libya.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Should oil prices move higher yet again, gold could be back in demand as a hedge against inflation," said Phillip Futures. Nymex April crude was at $103/bbl up $1.58, spurred by uncertainty over the timing and scale of the military moves in Libya. Tocom February 2012 gold was at Y3,700/gram, up Y139 as G-7 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  encourages investors back into asset markets, including gold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PRICES: \par  \par DJIA                       11774.59   +161.29   +1.39% \par Nasdaq                      2636.05    +19.23   +0.73% \par S&P 500                     1273.72    +16.84   +1.34% \par FTSE 100                    5698.48    +99.87   +1.78% \par FTSE 250                   11226.14   +193.56   +1.75% \par DJ UK Small-Cap TSM Index   3390.11    +62.65   +1.88% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225                  9217.72   +255.05   +2.85% \par S&P/ASX 200                 4615.50    +60.18   +1.32% \par Shanghai Comp               2913.68    +16.39   +0.57% \par Dow Future                 11808.50   +125.50    +1.1% \par NASDAQ Future               2243.88    +17.88    +0.8% \par S&P Future                  1279.85    +11.05    +0.9% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY                    81.76-79             +3.55% \par Range                         81.84   78.99 \par EUR/USD                   1.4078-79             +0.38% \par Range                        1.4087  1.3978 \par GBP/USD                   1.6171-74             +0.22% \par 