\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares To Rise As Oil, Dollar Rocket\b0\par\par\uc2 3,683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen higher, government debt mixed; euro, oil futures, spot gold higher\par \par \uc2 JAPAN CRISIS HEADLINES:\par \par \uc2 -Japan To Redouble Fukushima Water Cooling Efforts; Radiation Levels Down\par \par \uc2 -Low Levels of Radioactive Material Detected in Pacific\par \par \uc2 -G-7 Pledges Rare Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  As Crisis-Stricken \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sells Yen; Dollar Jumps\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Shirakawa: BOJ To Continue To Provide Ample Liquidity\par \par \uc2 -Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Injects Additional 3 Trillion Yen Into Money Market\par \par \uc2 -Japan Asks Indonesia To Increase Its LNG Supplies\par \par \uc2 -US Stock Funds Investing In Japan Had Weekly Inflow Of $956M - Lipper\par \par \uc2 -Pimco CEO: No Asia Redemptions Since Japan Quake -Bloomberg TV\par \par \uc2 -European Firms Face Japanese Component Shortage\par \par \uc2 -Bernanke, Trichet First Among G-7 Central Bankers Saying Would Help In Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -Source\par \par \uc2 -Purchase of JGBs By G-7 Not Discussed - Source\par \par \uc2 OTHER KEY HEADLINES:\par \par \uc2 -U.N. Approves Attack on Libya, Oil Surges\par \par \uc2 -ECB OKs EU Bailout Fund Plans, Prefers Greater Say\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Munich Re\uc2  Modifies Terms On 6.75% 2023 LT2 Buyback Offer\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: EBA: EU Stress Tests Will Focus On Banks' Core Tier 1 Capital Levels\par \par \uc2 -Airbus Under Investigation For 2009 \uc2 Air France\uc2  Crash\par \par \uc2 -EQT Gets At least Two Bids For KabelBW, Sale Or IPO Decision Next Week-Sources\par \par \uc2 -'Considerable Danger" In Libyan Military \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Germany Says\par \par \uc2 -EU Endorses And 'Ready To Implement' UN Libya Vote\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Sodiaal\uc2 , PAI Agree To Sell 51% Yoplait To \uc2 General Mills\uc2  - Sources\par \par \uc2 -Canada To Send Warplanes To Enforce Libya No-Fly Zone - Report\par \par \uc2 -French PM: Will Restrict Nuclear Exports To Developed Countries - Report\par \par \uc2 -TMX Group Chief Executive Sees LSE Deal Filing Within Weeks\par \par \uc2 -Bank Of England Deputy Governor Bean Against Immediate Rate Rise -Report\par \par \uc2 -UK Consumer Confidence Continues To Evaporate\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0700 GMT GER Feb PPI; 0930 GMT UK Feb CML mortgage lending figures\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets are recovering, along with gold and oil, as investors test the risk waters with hopes for Japan's chances of containing its damaged nuclear plant. However, the United Nations authorized military action against Libya, which could stir up risk aversion again. Until then, European bourses are likely to start higher, with quality government debt mixed. The euro, after the Group of Seven industrial powers launched a rare joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, is higher, along with spot gold and oil futures.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are likely to start higher as markets rebound slightly on some optimism that Japan can contain its nuclear crisis, while Libya looms after the U.N. authorized military action.\par \par \uc2 For Friday's opening, \uc2 BGC Partners\uc2  is calling the FTSE up 30 at 5726, the DAX up 73 at 6730, and the CAC up 37 at 3823.\par \par \uc2 "Today's much anticipated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the BoJ and the G-7 has seen a welcomed weakening of the Yen, and by effect, a boost to global equity markets," said Cameron Peacock, analyst at IG Markets.\par \par \uc2 "Market sentiment remains nervous given the uncertain situation in Japan," VTB Capital economist Neil MacKinnon said.\par \par \uc2 "The volatility will remain for a while as the market is moving on each headline that comes out from Japan," said equities head Atif Latif at Guardian Stockbrokers.\par \par \uc2 Scott Marcouiller at \uc2 Wells Fargo Advisors\uc2  said the market could get a sharp boost if Japanese efforts to restore electricity to the stricken Fukushima nuclear plant were successful as that would allow them cool down the reactors.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are higher Friday, after markets Thursday closed out their first day of gains this week as rising oil prices boosted energy companies and efforts to tame Japan's damaged nuclear-power plant eased some fears over the crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The market was due for a bit of a bounce, given how negative the sentiment got and how violent the downdraft was," said Carlton Neel, senior managing director at Virtus Investment Advisers. "What we're starting to see is the recognition that the rebuilding effort in Japan is going to take an enormous amount of resources, both human resources and natural resources."\par \par \uc2 Boosting natural-gas explorers, the head of the U.S. Energy Information Administration said Japan is likely to turn to liquefied natural gas to meet its energy needs as it recovers from the earthquake. Northern Oil & Gas rose 5.4%, Pioneer Drilling added 6.4% and \uc2 Oceaneering International\uc2  gained 4.4%.\par \par \uc2 Industrials also climbed, lifted by a 3.1% jump in international package shipper \uc2 FedEx\uc2  after its fourth-quarter earnings target topped analysts' mean estimate.\par \par \uc2 A surge in Japan's shares led most Asian stock markets higher Friday.\par \par \uc2 Some traders were circumspect about the Tokyo market's rise, noting the G-7 action had been anticipated and Japanese officials still have a massive recovery and rebuilding task on hand.\par \par \uc2 "The problems at the nuclear plant are far from over, and we have a three-day weekend ahead, so it's hard to keep buying on the weaker yen alone," said Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The G-7 pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in a decade as Japan dumped yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Friday to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record surge from further damaging an economy ravaged by earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster. The dollar soared on the move.\par \par \uc2 "At a time when Japan is in a difficult situation, it is extremely significant that G-7 authorities agreed to act in concert and cooperate for the stability of the markets," Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said in announcing the agreement after a 90-minute teleconference of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the united stand added "clout" to the announcement. It marked the first joint G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2000.\par \par \uc2 "We express our solidarity with the Japanese people in these difficult times, our readiness to provide any needed cooperation and our confidence in the resilience of the Japanese economy and financial sector," the communique from the G-7 said. "We will monitor exchange markets closely and will cooperate as appropriate."\par \par \uc2 Richard Grace, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at the Commonwealth Bank, said, "It looks like central banks will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as their market opens, which means round the clock \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and support. We'll probably see dollar/yen two or three big figures higher over the next 24 hours."\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Global Markets strategists Sean Callow and Robert Rennie said with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  flooding markets with liquidity in recent days, "this yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be successful."\par \par \uc2 The euro also surged against both the yen and the greenback on the G-7 announcement on expectations the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do its part to keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  low. There has been no indication from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet that it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the ECB is ever to step into the market, that will be during European trading hours, not Asian," one dealer said.\par \par \uc2 The euro's and dollar's rallies came against a backdrop of escalating tensions in the Middle East with the U.N. earlier sanctioning a no-fly zone over Libya, setting the stage for a potential vote on a military move within a few hours.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon was already fine-tuning military options for "serious" strikes against ground and air targets should the White House order them, U.S. defense officials said.\par \par \uc2 Sterling lost some ground against the euro and dollar on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, but basically was sidelined during the aftermath. Narrow movements from here were expected for Friday.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed Friday with military action against Libya on the horizon while Japan steps up efforts to contain its nuclear plant crisis.\par \par \uc2 SEB says the rally in bund prices is in its final stage before the longer-term bear market resumes.\par \par \uc2 Bunds have come under pressure from Thursday's Spanish and French multi-tranche bond auctions with in excess of EUR12.5 billion of paper sold. Apart from T-bills, no new paper is expected in Europe until next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday welcomed a draft European Council decision about the euro zone's stability mechanism, but said governments should consult European Union institutions when deciding upon the activation of the bailout fund.\par \par \uc2 Britain has entered a hazardous period as new question marks hang over the recovery at a time of persistently above-target inflation, a top Bank of England official said to The Times in London.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Bean, the Deputy Governor, told The Times that it was still not clear whether the economy was passing through a soft patch or had entered "a more durable slowing" amid public worries about the Treasury's spending cuts and tax rises.\par \par \uc2 Bean signalled his opposition to immediate interest rate increases, saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed to get a "better handle on the prospects for the economy further down the road," The Times reported.\par \par \uc2 U.K. consumers became even more pessimistic about the outlook for the economy and their job prospects during February, and less willing to spend, according to a monthly survey commissioned by the \uc2 Nationwide Building Society\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury bonds, now lower, snapped a three-day rally on Thursday as fear over Japan's nuclear crisis eased, spurring a relief rally in U.S. stocks.\par \par \uc2 Demand for safe assets also wilted on U.S. data showing improvement in the labor market and strong manufacturing growth in the Middle Atlantic states. An uptick in consumer prices suggested budding risks of inflation, which is the main threat to bonds' fixed returns over time. [ 18-03-11 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 But traders cautioned that the bond market could see two-way flows in the short term because of the uncertainty about the radiation from Japan's damaged nuclear plant and the ongoing political turmoil in the Middle East and North Africa. Besides the clashes in Bahrain, Libya warned on Thursday it could target military and civilian air and sea traffic in the Mediterranean should there be a foreign military \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aid anti-government rebels.\par \par \uc2 "It has been a mixed bag. The U.S. economic data have been decent, but there is a lot of uncertainty about Japan," said Srini Ramaswamy, head of liquid markets strategy at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  in New York. "That means the bond market will continue to see a lot of volatility in the short term."\par \par \uc2 Ramaswamy said investors likely prefer staying on the sidelines, contributing to less liquid conditions that can produce bigger swings in the market.\par \par \uc2 "Once we pass the period of flight-to-quality flows, Treasury yields will rise," said Robert Auwaerter, head of the fixed-income group at Vanguard Group in Valley Forge, Pa. "We have been bearish on Treasurys over the past six months, and we position for higher interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Auwaerter said the crisis in Japan is unlikely to undermine U.S. economic growth, adding that a rebuilding boom will emerge in Japan in coming months and Japanese investors will sell Treasurys to return funds home for the reconstruction effort.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo Friday, lead June Japanese government bond futures were down 0.04 at 139.66 points, weighed by the Nikkei's rise and the yen's sharp fall. The yield on the 10-year cash JGB was up two basis points at 1.22%.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to pump cash into Japan's financial system as needed to stabilize domestic financial markets as Japan and its G-7 counterparts act jointly to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude rose Friday from ongoing tensions in the oil-rich Middle East and North Africa regions amid Japan's nuclear crisis, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in April, gained $1.65 to $103.07 a barrel while Brent North Sea crude for May was up $1.56 to $116.46 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 "The initial reaction to Japan is a sharp drop in oil demand in the short-term," said Jason Feer, analyst for Singapore-based Argus Media energy market analysts.\par \par \uc2 But unrest in crude-producing Libya is a factor behind the surge in oil prices, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 The U.N. Security Council meeting voted Thursday to permit "all necessary measures" to impose a no-fly zone, protect civilian areas and impose a ceasefire on Moammar Gadhafi's military.\par \par \uc2 Enforcement will rely on air power as the resolution rules out sending ground troops.\par \par \uc2 "It is likely that U.S. and some Western countries may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Libya, and as Gadhafi has the upper hand, it is closer to ending the civil war," Feer said.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 LME metals are mostly lower on some mild profit taking after Thursday's powerful rally on a weak dollar and Japan reconstruction hopes.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied against the yen this morning on G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and stabilized against the euro, so it's no longer giving metals much help.\par \par \uc2 "Those who were buying into weakness on Tuesday are sitting on some nice gains, so you would expect a bit of profit taking," said a Singapore-based trader at a European bank. LME 3-month copper is at $9,523 a metric ton, down $41.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold is at $1,409.10 a troy ounce, up $5.10 since the New York close on buying in Tokyo as the yen weakens, and oil's rally after U.N. authorization of Western military action against Gadhafi's forces in Libya.\par \par \uc2 "Should oil prices move higher yet again, gold could be back in demand as a hedge against inflation," Phillip Futures said.\par \par \uc2 Tocom February 2012 gold is at Y3,700 a gram, up Y139 as G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encourages investors back into asset markets, including gold.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 18-03-11 0600GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11774.59  +161.29  +1.39% \par Nasdaq     2636.05   +19.23  +0.73% \par S&P 500    1273.72   +16.84  +1.34% \par FTSE 100   5696.11   +97.88  +1.75% \par Xetra DAX  6656.88  +143.04  +2.20% \par CAC40      3786.21  +89.65   +2.43% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9217.72  +255.05  +2.85% \par Hang Seng    22451.29 +166.86  +0.75% \par S&P/ASX 200   4615.50 + 60.18  +1.32% \par Taiwan Index  8393.54 +110.85  +1.34% \par S.Korea Kospi 11980.24 +21.21  +1.08% \par Shanghai Comp 2913.68  +16.39  +0.57% \par Bombay SenSex 17973.04 -176.83 -0.97% \par Dow Future    11808.50 +125.50 +1.1% \par NASDAQ Future 2243.88  +17.88  +0.8% \par S&P Future    1279.85  +11.05  +0.9% \par Above are as of 0525 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      81.76-79      +3.55% \par Range        81.84   78.99 \par EUR/USD      1.4078-79    +0.38% \par Range        1.4087  1.3978 \par AUD/USD      0.9945-48    +1.47% \par Range        0.9947  0.9779 \par GBP/USD      1.6171-74    +0.22% \par Range        1.6190  1.6097 \par USD/CHF      0.9070-75    +0.97% \par Range        0.9090  0.8976 \par EUR/GBP      0.8704-06    +0.16% \par Range        0.8722  0.8673 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.2768-75    +1.32% \par Range        1.2772  1.2579 \par Above are as of 0450 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 13.85%/14.15% \par Day earlier