\par\par \b \uc2 Bernanke, Trichet First Among G-7 Central Bankers Saying Would Help In Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -Source\b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 PARIS (Dow Jones)-Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the value of the yen was unanimously backed by all members of the Group of Seven industrialized nations, with U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet the first in expressing their support for the coordinated move, a G-7 official told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "Everyone was willing to help in as much as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was open to a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," the official said, noting this was in contrast to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in September 2010. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rise in the yen had then irked other G-7 members.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. and Canada rapidly followed Bernanke's and Trichet's lead in backing a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the official said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  made the case to its G-7 counterparts that the appreciation of the yen was essentially driven by speculative and not fundamental reasons, the official said. Japanese officials stressed that Japanese insurers didn't need to massively repatriate yen-denominated assets to shore up their balance sheets in order to meet insurance claims linked to the disasters that have devastated a large part of the country, the official added.\par \par \uc2 The purchase of Japanese government bonds by other G-7 countries wasn't discussed during the G-7 conference call, the official said. French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde Wednesday floated the idea as a way of helping Japan cope with market volatility in the aftermath of Friday's earthquake.\par \par \uc2 The impact of Japanese events on the outlook for global growth was only discussed in broad terms, with G-7 officials saying it remains too early to assess the full impact of the disasters.\par \par \uc2 "We agreed (the impact on growth) will depend on how quickly Japanese authorities manage to contain the nuclear fallout" from the earthquake and the ensuing tidal wave, the official said.\par \par \uc2 - By Nathalie Boschat, Dow Jones Newswires; +33 (0) 1 40 17 17 45; Nathalie.boschat@dowjones.com [ 18-03-11 0206GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110318e73i0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
