\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Maintain 2-Year/5-Year India OIS Flattener - SC\b0\par\par\uc2 1,795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 05:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0454 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered Bank maintains its flattener recommendation on the 2-year/5-year OIS spread (initiated on January 10 at 58bps; target 30bps; stop-loss 78bps). The house says large rate increases appear unlikely if supply-side dynamics continue to exert upward pressure on headline inflation. Also, elevated market rates (the 1-year CD rate has increased 340bps since April 2010) amid tight liquidity have already dampened investment sentiment. The house tips another 50 basis points of rate hikes for the rest of 2011 spread equally over next two meetings. The house expects economic growth to slow to 8.1% in FY12 from 8.5% in FY11. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones]Singapore's STI is up 0.2% at 2950.06 midday, rebounding after its 0.9% fall Thursday amid gains for regional markets on hopes Japan's nuclear catastrophe will be resolved. Volume is light at 531 million shares, reflecting pre-weekend caution. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says following the recent pull-back, its Singapore universe is trading at 13.4X 2011E, vs its five-year average of 14.5X. "Our P/B versus ROE analysis implies an end-2011E MSCI Singapore Index target of 438, implying 25% potential upside (corresponding STI target is 3711, also +25%)." The house continues to be positive on industrials for their exposure to global trade, and says its key stock ideas include Keppel (BN4.SG), \uc2 SembCorp Marine\uc2  (S51.SG) and NOL (N03.SG). It also likes Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), "one of the cheapest shipbuilding stocks globally" and is positive on commodity and tourism plays, like Olam (O32.SG), Wilmar (F34.SG), Noble (N21.SG), Genting (G13.SG) and CDLHT (J85.SG). Hutchison Port (NS8U.SG) debuts after lunch, with analysts expecting a tepid performance. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0449 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares are up 0.5% at 3499.949 in moderate volume, off an intraday high of 3511.673, with gains in most commodity-related stocks keeping the main index in positive territory, traders say. They expect the main index to move in a 3490-3520 range for the rest of the session. "Overall, the market is consolidating ahead of the weekend and as concerns over the situation in Japan and the Middle East linger," says a trader with Lautandhana Securities. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) is up 3.3% at IDR3,100, rival Indika (INDY.JK) is up 1.3% at IDR3,900, while \uc2 Indo Tambangraya\uc2  (ITMG.JK) is also up 2.1% at IDR47,350 on hopes for strong 2010 earnings. Profit-taking hits Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), which is down 1.5% at IDR4,975.(edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] "If Tokyo stocks continue to recover next week, there may be some swinging back in JGB yields," with the 10-year cash JGB yield possibly hitting as high as 1.25%, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  chief strategist Chotaro Morita; the 10-year yield is up 2.0 bps at 1.220%. Morita says the market's next focus will be the scale of the supplementary reconstruction budget; a local paper reported Friday that Japan may issue more than Y10 trillion in earthquake reconstruction bonds to be bought directly by the BOJ, but Morita calls this unrealistic. If that happens, "it would increase the market's distrust of the government for allowing such an disorderly budget," he says. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0445 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 3.1% at 9239.65 on the weaker yen following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (USD/JPY now 81.63), declines in radiation levels at Japan's troubled nuclear plants, and stronger share prices in most other Asian markets, says Hiroichi Nishi, equity division general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Radiation levels at the afflicted plant have declined slightly since Thursday afternoon, according to Japan's Nuclear and Industrial Safety Agency. "If buying in Nikkei futures kicks in, the Nikkei cash index may rise to 9,300 later today," Nishi says. 33/33 Topix subindexes are higher with exporters leading; Kyocera (6971.TO) is up 5.0% at Y7,830 and Fanuc (6954.TO) is up 2.3% at Y11,640. \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO), a major nuclear power plant maker, is up 6.3% at Y356. \uc2 Daikin Industries\uc2  (6367.TO) is up 8.8% at Y2,400 on expectations for demand when the earthquake recovery process begins.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 ends up 0.3% at 3339.51 as offshore markets improve and sentiment rises, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird; "The market has been weaker, while the leads offshore are positive and that tends to reflect on our market." She adds that Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) ended up 9.4% at NZ$2.22 after Thursday announcing better than expected 1H results; "Possibly it was market sentiment Thursday that meant we didn't see more of a reaction Thursday." She adds weakness in the NZD/USD was helping exporters; Skellerup (SKL.NZ) ended up 6.4% at NZ$1.33, Cavalier (CAV.NZ) ended up 5.9% at NZ$3.60 and Rakon (RAK.NZ) ended up 1.9% at NZ$1.06. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.3% at 1496.73 at midday after gains on Wall Street Thursday and amid mostly stronger regional markets. "After losses over the past few days, it's not a surprise to see this rebound ... but with uncertainties in Japan and the Middle East, we think the gains are precarious," says a dealer, adding the market may succumb to some profit taking in the afternoon session, ahead of the weekend. He tips 1500 as immediate resistance and 1489, the intraday low, as support. Market breadth is positive, with 408 advancers against 199 decliners. Gainers include \uc2 Tenaga Nasional\uc2  (5347.KU) is up 1.0% at MYR6.08, \uc2 IOI Corp\uc2 . (1961.KU) is up 1.1% at MYR5.63, Kulim (2003.KU) is 3.0% higher at MYR3.43 and Kossan Rubber (7153.KU) is up 1.9% at MYR3.25. Decliners include Maybank (1155.KU), down 0.5% at MYR8.71, and \uc2 Telekom Malaysia\uc2  (4863.KU), down 1.0% at MYR3.90. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] India bond yields are likely to rise in the near-term as India will begin its borrowing program for 1H of FY12 from April, says a dealer at a primary dealership. The Indian government is aiming to raise between 58%-64% of its borrowing for the next fiscal year during the April-September period, a senior finance ministry official said Friday. "The range is mostly in line with market expectations. But yields will rise from these levels when bond auctions begin," the dealer says. He expects the 8.08% 2022 bond yields to touch 8.20% when auctions begin from 8.11% currently. The government borrowing calendar is expected on March 25. The government typically frontloads its borrowing program. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  keeps Underweight on the India market as it believes while near-term fund flows may keep driving the country's outperformance, the economic momentum should stay weak, and will likely continue to pressure market multiples. It says further rate hikes look like a given following RBI's 25 bps hike in key policy rates Thursday; "inflation concerns remained RBIs focus even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  felt that investment growth may be slowing down," the house says. It adds "in our view, higher domestic fuel prices and borrowing costs are likely to be constraints on domestic growth and we maintain our view for GDP growth (forecast +7.7% in FY11) to be at the low-end of the consensus expectation." The Sensex is down 0.6% at 18,050.50. (raghavendra.upadhyaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end up 0.1% at 461.08, off an intraday low of 455.92, with investors coming back in the last minutes to buy after the key index fell more than 7.0% between Monday and Thursday, though there is an absence of any fresh market-boosting news. "Local sentiment has risen after foreigners were active in buying select blue-chip stocks, including Vietinbank (CTG.VH), after they were net sellers during the past few days," says a trader with EuroCapital Securities. He predicts more gains next week due to two factors: positive 1Q earnings and a positive end to Japan's nuclear crisis. Among the most actives, Vietinbank ended up 5.0% at VND31,700, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) closed up 4.6% at VND25,000 and \uc2 Hoang Anh Gia Lai (HAG.VH) \uc2 finished 2.2% lower at VND44,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, snapping a 5-session losing streak, following Wall Street's advance overnight. The main gauge PSEi is up 0.6% at 3829.88 with all sub-indices registering gains. "Global equities are staging a technical rally after the big economies said they will help with the recovery efforts in Japan," says Lawrence De Leon, a trader with Accord Capital Equities. De Leon adds, the index may trade in a 3750-3900 band next week. Most active stocks include Energy Development (EDC.PH), up 1.9% at PHP6.29; PLDT (TEL.PH), up 1.2% at PHP2,038; \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH), off 2.2% at PHP28.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are lower as traders cut their positions on concerns of further rate hikes as the RBI says it will continue with its anti-inflationary stance, says a trader with state-run bank. The 8.08% 2022 bond is at 99.76 from 99.99 at Thursday's close. He adds, "globally investors are pulling out of safe haven assets like bonds as fear over Japan's nuclear crisis is easing." He says bond prices are also pressured ahead of government's borrowing calendar for the first half of the next FY (due on March 25). A government official on Friday says, about 58%-64% of the fiscal year's borrowing will be during the April-September period. The trader tips the 8.08% 2022 bond yield in a 8.08%-8.14% band for the session from 8.08% at Thursday's close; the yield is at 8.11%. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2011 00:54 ET (04:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110318e73i000u6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
