\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Rubber Settles Up; Yen Boosts Tocom To Pre-Quake Levels\b0\par\par\uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 11:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian rubber settled higher Friday, with natural rubber futures on the bellwether Tokyo Commodity Exchange settling at pre-quake levels in a quick reversal of the massive sell-off earlier in the week that threatened potential defaults and huge losses for those caught long in the markets.\par \par \uc2 The rally was aided by a weaker yen after joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Group of Seven industrial powers sent the dollar as high as Y81.90, from Y79.19 before the announcement.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark August contract settled at Y408.6 a kilogram, up 2.9% from Thursday's settlement and 1.8% from a week earlier.\par \par \uc2 August Tocom rubber extended gains to close Y9.9 higher at Y418.5/kg in the night session, which is considered part of the next day's trading.\par \par \uc2 The market rose sharply in early trade, hitting the circuit breaker and triggering a temporary trading halt. The August contract hit a one-week high of Y425.5/kg before gains were pared by profit-taking ahead of a long weekend that includes a public holiday Monday.\par \par \uc2 Friday's rally was aided by a weaker yen after a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other Group of Seven industrial powers, sending the dollar surging to as high as Y81.90 at 0656 GMT, from Y79.19 before the announcement.\par \par \uc2 "Although there is a lot of activity and a rebound today due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market), the overall market sentiment in Japan still depends on ongoing developments at the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power complex," said Kaname Gokon, deputy general manager at Japanese commodity brokerage \uc2 Okato Shoji Co\uc2 .'s research division.\par \par \uc2 Japanese markets, including Tocom rubber, may gain sharply next week if the situation turns around at the nuclear plant, and the threat of radiation leaks recedes, Gokon said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, various moves by rubber producers to shore up prices also helped to improve sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Thailand's Deputy Prime Minister Suthep Thaugsuban said the government will support local prices at THB120 a kilogram ($3.96/kg). He also sought a temporary suspension of exports until markets stabilized, and called a meeting of agencies on Monday to discuss the proposal. Thailand is the biggest producer and exporter of natural rubber, accounting for one-third of global natural rubber production.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia, the second-biggest producer, has proposed to its members that a floor price of $4.00/kg be "defended," Suharto Honggokusumo, executive director of the Rubber Association of Indonesia, or Gapkindo, said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Thai government move has already had an impact on physical trade, as production is already low at this time of the year.\par \par \uc2 Prices of unsmoked sheet 3-grade rose sharply to THB132.55-THB134.55/kg Friday, from a low of THB89.00-THB102.59/kg Monday. Arrivals fell sharply to just 7 tons Friday from 122.5 tons Monday.\par \par \uc2 The government's decision to support prices is encouraging farmers to hold stocks back in anticipation of further price gains during the low production season, said a trader in Yala province.\par \par \uc2 Large exporters also bid aggressively, as they need the raw material in this season, he said.\par \par \uc2 They got some, but not enough, as several large exporters weren't offering Friday, citing low raw material availability.\par \par \uc2 "We keep increasing our offer prices to buy raw material over the trading day, but the quantity we can get is very little; there are many bids, particularly for STR20, but we can't sell," said one big Thai exporter.\par \par \uc2 Most other Asian rubber futures contracts also rose in line with Tocom's gains.\par \par \uc2 On the Shanghai Futures Exchange, benchmark rubber futures settled 2.5% higher, while ribbed smoked sheet 3-grade on the Agricultural Futures Exchange of Thailand hit the THB7 daily upper limit.\par \par \uc2 However, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said late Friday that it will raise banks' reserve requirement ratio by 0.50 percentage points, the third hike this year, as inflationary pressures remain in the spotlight. This may put downward pressure on prices next week, especially in the Shanghai market.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Asian Rubber Futures \par                          March 18           Change from \par                                             previous settlement \par Tocom    Aug  RSS3    Y408.6/kg             Up Y11.7 \par Shanghai Sep  SCR5    CNY35,105/ton         Up CNY850 \par Thai     Oct  RSS3    THB146.5/kg           Up THB7 \par Sicom    Apr  RSS3    524.9 U.S. cents/kg   Up 24.9 cents \par Sicom    Apr  TSR20   498 U.S. cents/kg     Up 12 cents \par  \par Physical prices in Asia, quoted in U.S. cents/kg, free on board: \par  \par  \par Grade  Shipment     March 18                March 17 \par                  Bids     Offers       Bids     Offers \par RSS3   Apr/May    -       -             -       - \par STR20  Apr/May    -       -             -       - \par SIR20  Apr/May    -       495-498       -       485-486 \par SMR20  Apr/May    -       497           -       490 \par  \par USS3   Mar       THB132.55-THB134.55/kg THB120.09-THB121.38/kg \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Huileng Tan, Dow Jones Newswires, +65 6415 4083; huileng.tan@dowjones.com\f2