\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Size Likely Small - Dealers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,577 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 02:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japan MOF remains in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, but the size of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is very likely to have been small, say five dealers based in Singapore, some of whom have seen direct flows going through the market related to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Some of them say the campaign that kicked off at 0000 GMT was "Japan's solo act"; so far the JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is being done only by Japan. Other central banks are expected to act later, during their market hours. The pair is at 81.26, off a post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  high of 81.49. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Global agricultural markets may have posted a short-term bottom, according to commodity analysts at \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group\uc2 , who noted a "decisive rebound" in farm product markets overnight after consolidating the day before. Wheat was the star performer with gains of 7%-8%, while rises for most other products were around 3% to 5%, they say, also looking at corn, soybeans, canola, sugar, cotton, coffee and cocoa. "The strength of the rebound in wheat overnight starts to give us some conviction that funds are already looking through the nuclear reactor problems in Japan and seeing this correction as a buying opportunity back into commodities." They add that the price action this time around in farm products already looks different from the last major "risk off" period around Feb. 22, which saw sell-offs for these products by 5%-7% in one session; "China could also be seeing value and buying on the dip." (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] Prospects for a swift resumption of Libyan oil exports near previous levels have receded further, after the UN passed a resolution authorizing a no-fly zone in the North African country, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Libya pumped 1.65 million barrels of crude a day prior to a popular uprising against the ruling regime of Col. Moammar Gadhafi, but currently two-thirds of production is offline. "While some countries, including some in Europe, might well still wish to make oil supply deals with the Libyan government, our expectation is that what will be a significantly reduced level of Libyan output is going to find it difficult to find markets to move into," BarCap says. Nymex crude for April delivery is now up $1.21 at $102.63 on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY may fall, tracking weakness in the USD index overnight. Friday's central parity will likely be set around 6.5680 from Thursday's 6.5690, says a Shanghai-based local bank trader. The pair is tipped to consolidate around 6.5700 vs 6.5744 in late Asian trade Thursday. Volume is thin these days because "the market expects the PBOC to stabilize the yuan until the dust settles in Japan, trading is quite thin," he says. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0106 GMT [Dow Jones] The HSI is likely to rebound taking its cue from Wall Street. A senior broker at a brokerage arm of Chinese banks says the HSI's 1.8% fall Thursday "was not indicative of the panicky sentiment in the market," and that barring a full-blown nuclear crisis, Thursday's intraday low of 22,123 may be a bottom for the market, atleast in the short term. Nuclear fears aside, local blue-chip stocks continue reporting results, and earnings for most have been solid. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  (0857.HK) and Henderson Land (0012.HK) are both likely to rise on above-consensus results; China Mobile (0941.HK) is likely to rebound after a two-day 4.2% fall post results. The HSI fell 1.8% to end at 22,284.43 Thursday - the lowest close for 2011. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0104 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares are likely to consolidate with a downward bias amid uncertainty surrounding Japanese nuclear crisis, while investors continue to fret about the impact of Japan's earthquake on China's economy. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is expected to consolidate below 2900 after ending down 1.1% at 2897.30 Thursday, analysts say; they put support at 2850. "The scale of Japan's radiation leak is more serious than people had expected" and worries about the Japanese earthquake consequences are still containing the market sentiment, says Shenyin Wanguo analyst Li Xiaoxuan. Also, China's still-high inflation, rising funding costs and a potentially sharp increase in the supply of new shares will also weigh on the domestic stock market, she adds. The Shenzhen Index fell 1.8% to 1284.96. (rose.yu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP may fall as local stocks are expected to rise following Wall Street's advance overnight, while the EUR/USD rises to $1.4000, supporting risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The pair may slide to 43.80 after settling at a seven-week high at 43.92 in late Asian trade Thursday. Traders peg support at 43.70 and resistance at 44. "I think the dollar will be rangebound today. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar rises to PHP44.00, while banks should start buying the dollar when it slides below 43.80," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatility hits the USD/SGD, in line with other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on G-7 announcement of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to force down the yen. But the news overall should be SGD positive, with a trader at a local bank tipping support for the USD/SGD at 1.2650. The risk-on sentiment brought by the G-7 cooperation might bring the carry trade back into Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had slowed as the yen soared, adds the trader; "this should keep the dollar down." The UN security council resolution on airstrikes against Libya adds some risk of oil price shocks, but for the moment this effect is more than outweighed by the yen returning to more normal levels. The pair drops to 1.2753 at 0025 GMT, shortly after the G-7 announcement, and is last trading at 1.2764. The trader eyes resistance at 1.2850. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY and NZD/JPY gain strongly as the G-7 announces coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the USD against the JPY but volatility continues as people closely watch Japan and Libya, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager Murray Hindley. "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk is the reason why these things have rallied and things would suggest they have finished on the downside but it is a $54,000 question," he says. He says markets will be focused on the nuclear situation in Japan and the events unfolding in Libya, "plus we have a Tokyo holiday on Monday that actually might slow things down, or could make the markets even more volatile because of a lack of liquidity." The AUD/JPY is at 80.15 vs 78.025 prior to the announcement and the NZD/JPY is at 58.675 vs 57.305.(rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is down at 3.0450 from 3.0530 late Thursday in line with regional markets on the G-7 announcement of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare down yen strength. A local trader says the pair is likely to head lower as risk sentiment returns to the market with the yen likely to return to more comfortable levels. He tips support at 3.0400, and says \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  is not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] Group of Seven (G-7)'s agreement on joint foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely provide support for the Nikkei in coming sessions after the index suffered in the wake of last Friday's massive earthquake and tsunami, says Hideyuki Ishiguro, investment strategy supervisor at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . The Nikkei is up 2.8% at 9212.13. "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows authorities overseas are also taking the situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seriously," he says. "The downside risk in the global economy remains on higher crude prices; the G-7 wants Japan to avoid a setback." A report that the Japanese government is planning to issue more than Y10 trillion in earthquake reconstruction bonds (to be bought in full by the Bank of Japan) would, if true, also be supportive of Japan share prices, Ishiguro says. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is expected to open higher, with Wall Street's rebound overnight and stronger Japanese stocks lending support, a local dealer says. Still, he thinks the upside is likely to be limited as investors continue to fret over the impact of Japan earthquake on the global economy and the rising tensions in the Middle East. "Many investors may want to stay away from the market, especially with the weekend approaching, as there are still many unresolved issues outside the country." He tips immediate resistance at 1500, with support at 1474. The KLCI closed 0.35 points lower at 1492.09 Thursday. UMW (4588.KU) may rise after its unit gets a $41.5 million \uc2 Petronas Carigali\uc2  contract. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2109ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i00016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
