 1.2753 at 0025 GMT, shortly after the G-7 announcement, and is last trading at 1.2764. The trader eyes resistance at 1.2850. (martin.vaughan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/JPY and NZD/JPY gain strongly as the G-7 announces coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the USD against the JPY but volatility continues as people closely watch Japan and Libya, says ANZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Manager Murray Hindley. "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk is the reason why these things have rallied and things would suggest they have finished on the downside but it is a $54,000 question," he says. He says markets will be focused on the nuclear situation in Japan and the events unfolding in Libya, "plus we have a Tokyo holiday on Monday that actually might slow things down, or could make the markets even more volatile because of a lack of liquidity." The AUD/JPY is at 80.15 vs 78.025 prior to the announcement and the NZD/JPY is at 58.675 vs 57.305.(rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is down at 3.0450 from 3.0530 late Thursday in line with regional markets on the G-7 announcement of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pare down yen strength. A local trader says the pair is likely to head lower as risk sentiment returns to the market with the yen likely to return to more comfortable levels. He tips support at 3.0400, and says \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  is not likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] Group of Seven (G-7)'s agreement on joint foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely provide support for the Nikkei in coming sessions after the index suffered in the wake of last Friday's massive earthquake and tsunami, says Hideyuki Ishiguro, investment strategy supervisor at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . The Nikkei is up 2.8% at 9212.13. "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows authorities overseas are also taking the situation in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seriously," he says. "The downside risk in the global economy remains on higher crude prices; the G-7 wants Japan to avoid a setback." A report that the Japanese government is planning to issue more than Y10 trillion in earthquake reconstruction bonds (to be bought in full by the Bank of Japan) would, if true, also be supportive of Japan share prices, Ishiguro says. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is expected to open higher, with Wall Street's rebound overnight and stronger Japanese stocks lending support, a local dealer says. Still, he thinks the upside is likely to be limited as investors continue to fret over the impact of Japan earthquake on the global economy and the rising tensions in the Middle East. "Many investors may want to stay away from the market, especially with the weekend approaching, as there are still many unresolved issues outside the country." He tips immediate resistance at 1500, with support at 1474. The KLCI closed 0.35 points lower at 1492.09 Thursday. UMW (4588.KU) may rise after its unit gets a $41.5 million \uc2 Petronas Carigali\uc2  contract. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is biased down as local shares are likely to rebound following Wall Street's rise overnight, says a local trader. She adds, although the USD/JPY is rising sharply on G-7's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pair is unlikely to follow suit. "Many traders shifted their focus to trade the U.S. dollar against the yen yesterday because they thought it was unlikely to make much profits on trading the U.S. dollar against the New Taiwan dollar," she says. She tips the pair to trade in a 29.450-29.550 band for the day; the pair was at 29.5740 in late Asian trade Thursday. Taiwan shares ended down 0.5% at 8282.69 Thursday; foreign investors were net sellers of NT$9.41 billion worth of local shares. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares are likely to open higher after a positive session on Wall Street, though ongoing caution amid Japan's woes could limit gains. "Wall Street was green on St. Patrick's Day, so we'll probably get a technical rebound. On the charts, the STI index seems to be sitting on the lower Bollinger Band, so that actually sets the index up for a nice rebound today," says an analyst at a local brokerage. On Thursday, the STI ends down 0.9% at 2942.88. "We'll be watching to see if things continue to be rosy, and there's no more worrying news from Japan; then we may see the index try to fill the gap at 3009 to 3020. However, we also have to note that there may not be very big bargain hunting because the market is still not oversold, that's the danger." All eyes will be on \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG), which makes its debut this afternoon, after it completed Southeast Asia's biggest ever IPO. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is up 0.6% at 1971.11 following U.S. stocks gains overnight. Some market fears over damages at the troubled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear-power plants in Japan seem to be easing, analysts say; "the situation in Japan is bad, but there are also hopes that it doesn't get to the worst-case scenario," says Shinhan Investment analyst Lee Sun-yeop. But, he adds, trade could become choppy later due to end-week profit-taking as uncertainties from Japan still remain in the news. He tips a 1975 resistance for the index. Shipbuilders lead gains on continued hopes for more orders with \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  (009540.SE) up 0.7% at KRW474,000 and \uc2 Samsung Heavy Industries\uc2  (010140.SE) up 1.3% at KRW37,950. Oil refiners are also up on earnings hopes amid growing refining margins recently; \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is up 4.3% at KRW216,500 and \uc2 S-Oil Corp\uc2  (010950.SE) rises 1.8% to KRW142,000. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] February 2012 gold futures on Tocom jump 3.9% from its 0400 GMT close and the contract is trading at Y3,701/gram, roughly $1,415/oz, with volumes at their highest level in 10 months; 106,834 contracts, equivalent to 106.8 tons, changed hands so far. Move matches the USD/JPY's sharp rise; the pair is up 2.3% since 0000 GMT at 81.363. A Tokyo-based trader sees local investors re-entering long positions in gold following recent liquidation on hopes that emergency G7 meeting will lead to weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : "I think investors are looking to that and coming back to precious metal futures in expectation of action." Spot gold is at $1,410.60/oz, up 0.6% since its informal open of $1,402.79/oz at 2200 GMT.(david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds may fall a tad, taking cues from U.S. Treasurys overnight after the fears over radiation leaks from Japan's damaged nuclear plants eased a little, a local trader says. But he adds the market is likely to be subdued on Friday, as investors may want to stay on the sidelines heading into the weekend. He tips yields on MGS to rise 1-2 bps. On Thursday, the yield on the August 2014 MGS ends at 3.36%, the September 2016 MGS yield ends at 3.53%, the yield on the September 2017 MGS ends at 3.70% and the July 2021 MGS yield ends at 3.99%, all 2 bps lower. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 3.1% at 9241.72 after the Group of Seven agrees to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing USD/JPY higher (now 81.25, up from low 79 premarket level). Most traders had expected that the G-7 would at least allow \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as opposed to unilateral action) is more positive than expected, says Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Still, any further rise for the Nikkei is likely limited to around 9,250, she says, citing continued concerns over Japan's ongoing nuclear plant woes. "The problems at the nuclear plant are far from solved, and with the three-day weekend ahead, it's hard to keep buying on the weaker yen alone," she says. All 33 Topix subindexes are higher, with exporters generally up; \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) is up 2.8% at Y3,140, and \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) is up 3.7% at Y4,400. Tepco (9501.TO) is up 11% at Y888. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com) [ 03-17-11 2103ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
