\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: The Work's Not Done For  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 14:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:55 (Dow Jones) \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to be followed up by policy action to work, says \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 's Paresh Upadhyaya. The Bank of Japan will either follow up with additional quantitative easing--pumping the economy with more liquidity--or, eventually, rate differentials between the Federal Reserve and BoJ will support the dollar, easing yen strength. BofA Merrill targets dollar at Y88 by year-end, compared with the current Y81. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:46 (Dow Jones) \uc2 LAN Airlines\uc2 ' (LAN) shares tumble in reaction to the Antitrust Court decision to review the proposed merger with Brazil's TAM. The review will likely delay the merger until next year, according to analysts. LAN shares recently trading at CLP11,935.00 ($24.76), down 1.3%, while Ipsa blue chip index gaining 0.3%. (carolina.pica@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (A Market Talk item published at 8:54 a.m. ET misstated an FDA panel's recommendation regarding menthol cigarettes. A corrected version, with updated stock activity, follows.)\par \par \uc2 (Dow Jones) Lorillard (LO) shares bouncing around this morning after FDA panel says removing menthol cigarettes from the US market would benefit the public health. LO fell more than 10% premarket, now up 2.7% to $80.95 in early trading. FDA isn't required to follow the committee's advice, but analysts have warned that the group could at least try to get menthol cigarettes off the shelves. LO derives the vast majority of its revenue from menthol products. \uc2 Reynolds American\uc2  (RAI) and \uc2 Altria\uc2  (MO) also rise after premarket decline. (melissa.korn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:43 (Dow Jones) If Chinese officials want to show their support for the G7's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the yen they could let the yuan appreciate more against the dollar, says economist and former top Treasury and Fed hand Ted Truman. "That could be the way they get involved," notes Truman, who previously directed the Fed's division of international finance. He adds appreciation of the yuan against the dollar wouldn't only effectively be an appreciation against the yen, but would also be in line with US policy goals. (michael.crittenden@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:42 (Dow Jones) Large asset managers suffer meaningful retail outflows this week as a result of the crisis in Japan and events in the Middle East. \uc2 Franklin Resources\uc2 , T Rowe Price and Invesco had retail equity outflows of $768M, $490M and $492M, respectively, according to EPFR. "We suspect stabilization is needed before we can see a flow recovery," Ticonderoga Securities analyst Douglas Sipkin says. In the interim, the most risk is at retail brokers, which have held up better than asset managers, but outflows and depreciating markets will pressure asset managers as well, he says. (daisy.maxey@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:24 (Dow Jones) It's looking like apparel makers Perry Ellis (PERY) and \uc2 New York & Co\uc2  (NWY) are going to have markedly different session. NWY is getting kudos, as 4Q profit surged with sales and margins climbing. "We see greater discipline on mark downs and inventory levels" under new CEO \uc2 Greg Scott\uc2 , who assumed the role last month and joined in mid 2010, Keybanc says. Meanwhile, PERY's 4Q profit fell 9.2%, amid write-downs and costs from a recent acquisition. PERY also cut its current-year EPS forecast to account for dilution from a recent capital raise. (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:19 (Dow Jones) The high costs of taking money out of Brazil will likely discourage Japanese investors from repatriating the $34B they hold in real-denominated investments, giving support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Banco Santander. High Brazilian interest rates and a tax on foreign investors to re-enter the market "creates little incentive to pull the money out," which in part explains why Brazil has fared better than other countries where the Japanese invest heavily, such as Australia and Canada, Santander says. (paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:16 (Dow Jones) Reports of a Libyan ceasefire have primed stocks for an even bigger Friday pop than what the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  set up. It'll be a good test of how wary investors remain of the nuclear crisis. "It's a positive development for any investor locked in on the Middle East and North Africa, but you still have the other factors out there," Todd Salamone, senior vice president at Schaeffer's Investment Research, says. While Libya's news takes the wind out of oil's sails and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves limit some of Japan's economic aftereffects, neither of today's positives change the radiation problem, which is what seemed to frighten investors most. DJIA futures up 126 at 11837. (brendan.conway@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:16 (Dow Jones) As the Fed gets into the game of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it doesn't have much ammo. According to 4Q data, the most current from the New York Fed, the US \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had $3.9B in cash yen and $8B in yen-denominated securities. The Fed must be hoping the psychological impact of them acting outweighs what they can actually do with yen selling. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (Dow Jones) Everything is looking rosy for Wall Street, including the ceasefire in Libya sparking a tumble in crude prices, G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and even spring-like weather in New York. But keep in mind this is Friday, which leaves a lot of time for investors to sit tight while there is still considerable uncertainty surrounding Japan's nuclear crisis. And usually, the earlier event-related buying starts, the earlier the "profit-taking" pullback begins. DJIA futures up 122, off Thursday's 11775 close. The DJIA should clear initial resistance at 11800-11850 at the open, but whether it can close above it is uncertain. Resistance at 11950-12000 should be very heavy. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:09 (Dow Jones) News that Libya has declared a ceasefire looks to be the catalyst to spark another session of major moves in the US stock market. In a volatile week, the Dow Jones Industrial Average has so far posted three consecutive day of triple-digit point moves for the first time since late July. While US stock futures were already solidly higher after the G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reports of Libya's cease-fire sends futures on the Dow industrials up 123 points to 11834 recently, while S&P 500 futures climb 15 to 1284. (kristina.peterson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 03-18-11 0955ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110318e73i00097\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
