\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/CNY Lower But Could Rise On Import Settlements\b0\par\par\uc2 2,027 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 08:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0721 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY is lower in afternoon trading but pares some of its earlier losses on import settlements, a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader says. The pair is at 6.5733, off an earlier 6.5693 low, and down from 6.5744 late Thursday; the trader tips the pair in a 6.5700-6.5800 range for the rest of the session. He adds the pair may rise more in the last hour of trading when corporate clients usually buy or sell foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Thursday and today we've seen slightly heavier corporate demand for the dollar, which has driven the dollar up against the yuan in an otherwise thin market." The central parity is set at 6.5668 from 6.5690 Thursday. Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.4450/6.4490 from 6.4600/6.4640 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] The \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  may trim the upper end of its 2011 GDP growth forecast (range) of 3.0%-5.0% due to higher oil prices following the political unrest in the Middle East and North Africa as well as the catastrophe in Japan, says Capital Nomura Securities' economist Nuchjarin Panarode. The robust exports in February may be a cushion for the Thai economic growth, so the lower-end is likely to be unchanged, she says. The house sets the year-end policy rate target at 2.75%. "The trend is on the rise but the timing hinges on several factors," she says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March raised its benchmark rate for the second time of the year by 25 basis points to 2.50%. Thailand swung back to a trade surplus of $1.77 billion in February as exports surged 31% to $18.87 billion. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0653 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.2% at 1495.55 in early afternoon trade on bargain buying after recent sharp losses. Market volume is thin at 610 million shares. A local dealer says that although sentiment remains positive, it may succumb to profit-taking later in the session. "Investors' time frames on the market are ultra short-term in these uncertain days... many wouldn't be comfortable holding too much stock over the weekend," the dealer says. he tips immediate resistance at 1500 and support at 1489 (intraday low). Market breadth is positive with 420 advancers against 203 decliners. Among gainers Dialog (7277.KU) is up 1.4% at MYR2.25, Kulim (2003.KU) is up 2.7% at MYR3.42, WCT (9679.KU) is up 1.0% at MYR2.99 and Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is up 1.5% at MYR3.35. Among decliners Telecom Malaysia (4863.KU) is down 1.0% at MYR3.90 and YTL (4677.KU) is down 1.0% at MYR7.18. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore's STI is up 0.1% at 2945.41, off earlier highs as despite sentiment getting a lift from efforts to stabilise Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , caution sets in before the weekend. After Japan's Nikkei closed 2.7% higher, Kim Eng says "investors welcomed a pledge by the Group of Seven nations to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices." But an analyst at a local brokerage says "the markets are off their highs as bargain hunting is not very strong, the issue in Japan is still a worry. Things can literally blow up over the weekend, so people will definitely not want to keep big positions open. If there are profits, people will take them. It's going to be sideways and we still think the market needs to clear 3032." Volume is very light at 626 million shares, with \uc2 Hutchison Port Holdings Trust\uc2  (NS8U.SG) accounting for 289 million on its debut. The ports business trust trades at US$0.955 vs US$1.01 IPO price after the world's largest IPO this year. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends up 1.1% at 1981.13 Friday with market fears over the damaged nuclear facility in Japan subsiding. Despite high volatility, the index posted a 1.3% weekly gain with investors keeping their hopes up on local stocks' fundamentals, says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  Bae Sung-young. Though uncertainties remain in Japan and Libya over the weekend, "the market will keenly watch if the index recovers the key 2,000 mark next week," he adds. Bae tips a 1950-2000 range for next week. Steelmakers and auto stocks were leading gainers on expectations that they are likely to benefit from any supply problems encountered by their Japanese industry peers; Posco (005490.SE) ended up 3.2% at KRW502,000 and \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) rose 2.4% at KRW60,900. But tech plays underperformed as investors remain unsure of any impact from Japanese components makers' troubled supply on the overall industry, analysts said; \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) ended flat at KRW891,000 and LG Display (034220.SE) ended off 1.1% to KRW34,850. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/HKD is lower on profit-taking after the G-7 agreed on joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in a decade to weaken the yen, says a trader at a Singapore bank. "There are some selling activities after the pair hit its 9-month high Thursday." He adds that a rebound in the local equity market is also hurting the pair, with the HSI rising 0.5% after it fell 1.8% Thursday. The trader tips the pair to track a 7.7950-7.8030 band in the near term. The pair at 7.7994 from 7.8020 late Thursday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0644 GMT [Dow Jones] While U.S. Treasurys are the obvious place for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF to park the proceeds from its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is "less clear" as U.S. debt is "less glamorous" considering the U.S.'s quantitative easing, fiscal position "and the potential for further dollar debasement," \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Also, "a concerted move into U.S. Treasurys would be a key driver for further USD/JPY downside on a medium-term basis" as the move may dampen U.S. yields, narrowing the spreads and sending investors back into buying JPY. While \uc2 UBS\uc2  expects a large portion of the MOF's funds will be automatically directed to Treasurys, large flows may head elsewhere this time, possibly the eurozone, as periphery debt markets to some extent have stabilized and yields are rising on expectations of ECB rate hikes. It adds, traditional high-yielding, high-beta \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as AUD, NZD and Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also benefit, but considering concerns in the Middle East and North Africa, "this may not be the right time for Japan to place its money there either." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  notes the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not last, and coordinated G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "ensures a far greater chance of success" though no guarantee. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is short term, it estimates \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF will run out of ammunition in about three and a half weeks, at Y2 trillion/day (September's assumed rate). Considering volumes in JPY trade stand around Y60 trillion/day, that's "simply not enough to make a difference barring capital controls; thus the Japanese authorities expressed a desire for joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for more resources and headline impact." It expects the earthquake to cause a short-term Japanese growth correction; if the global economy is sound and ready for further rebalancing, Japan will be able to rebound accordingly and the JPY, in trade weighted terms, will also adjust. It says targeting specific levels "is a futile gesture" and "China, in particular, might raise some eyebrows if Japan were allowed to guide the yen back towards a more competitive level, especially with outside help." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones]\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry Noda has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not targeting any specific levels, but "the psychologically important level of Y80.00 will likely stick out as a key level to defend," says Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Credit Agricole. Yet while the G-7 pledge of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will ensure some success" in the effort to ward off sharp JPY gains, "there is still a long way to go, with prospects of huge repatriation flows likely to make the task of weakening the JPY a difficult one," Kotecha says. The USD/JPY is at 81.80.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT (Dow Jones) The EUR/CHF is very much a function of global investors' assessment of risk, with Thursday's \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  policy statement having little impact on the cross, says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Then again, with financial markets gradually stepping back into riskier markets, the elevated EUR/CHF money market spread is likely to weigh on the franc going forward," according to economist Reto Huenerwadel. "At times of consolidating financial markets we'd thus expect EUR/CHF to be well supported," he adds. EUR/CHF is at 1.2724. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0611 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +1.4% at 8394.75 as most tech shares staged a strong rebound after their recent losses, says Concord Securities manager Allan Lin. He notes that many tech firms said there won't be a shortage of component supplies from Japan by the end of April. Still, "we still need to wait for more reports from Japanese companies on \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumption and potential losses," he adds. He tips the Taiex to trade in a 8200-8400 band early next week. TSMC (2330.TW) ends up 1.3% at 68.30 and HTC (2498.TW) ends up 3.0% at 1040.00. Hotel shares are higher after their recent underperformance; Formosa International Hotel (2707.TW) rises by its 7% daily limit to NT$448.50, while Ambassador Hotel (2704.TW) ends up 2.3% at NT$40.40. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0321ET ]\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets are mostly higher, led by the Tokyo market after the G-7 agreed to joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the JPY's recent surge as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggles with multiple natural disasters and an ongoing nuclear emergency. The Nikkei is +2.6%, the S&P/ASX is +1.6%, the HSI is +0.6%, the Kospi is +1.3%, the Taiex is +1.3%, the Shanghai Composite is +0.7% and the STI is +0.3%; the Sensex is 1.0% lower on political concerns after leaked cables. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the JPY pulled back sharply after the G-7 pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  dumped yen on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Japan's MOF sold JPY through the BOJ for the first time since September and Japanese finance minister Yoshihiko Noda said each G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its own timezone. The USD/JPY jumped to 81.88 from 78.93 late Thursday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 115.18 from 110.62 and the EUR/USD is at 1.4070 from 1.4020. China needs to effectively lower the massive amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  it buys to rein in inflation, a professor at PBOC's training institute says. February prices of newly built homes in 68 of the 70 Chinese cities covered by National Bureau of Statistics survey rose on-year. Mounting worries over Japan's nuclear crisis will prompt India to revisit its own nuclear safety strategies, says Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh. Spot gold is at $1,410.40/oz, up $6.40 from the NY close. April Nymex crude oil futures are up $1.40 at $103.26/bbl. (philip.vahn@dowjones.com) [ 03-18-11 0321ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110318e73i0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
